window.AE_BM_BANK = {"_comment": "Generated by tools/build_problem_bank.py from vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/. Do not edit by hand.", "subject": "business-management", "categories": {"short_theory_case": {"label": "Short theory cases", "points": 2.0}, "extra_practice_problem": {"label": "Extra Practice problems", "points": 4.0}}, "archetypes": {"unordered-items": "Unordered items", "reverse-engineering-ratios": "Reverse engineering & ratios", "narrative-case": "Narrative case", "year-on-year-cash": "Year-on-year & cash", "financing-leverage": "Financing & leverage"}, "units": {"1": "Topic 1 · The firm", "2": "Topic 2 · Value creation", "3": "Topic 3 · Financial management (I)"}, "counts": {"short_theory_case": 24, "extra_practice_problem": 16}, "items": [{"id": "C01", "category": "short_theory_case", "archetype": "theory-case", "theme": "circular-flow", "unit": 1, "title": "SkyCourier: real and financial flows of a drone-delivery start-up", "difficulty": 1, "points": 2.0, "time_minutes": 15, "tags": ["theory-case", "circular-flow", "factors-of-production"], "official": false, "source": "Practice case written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), based on Topic 1_The Firm_Types  Objectives.pdf, Slides 3-11", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank C01 - SkyCourier Circular Flow of Income.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>SkyCourier S.L. delivers medicines by drone to villages in the Pyrenees. It rents a hangar from a local farmer, employs twelve pilots and technicians, and was financed by investors who bought its shares. Each month it charges the regional health service and several pharmacies for the deliveries. In 2025 the families of the valley spent part of their wages on SkyCourier's new home-delivery service, and the company paid its first dividend to its shareholders.</p>", "setup_html": null, "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Identify SkyCourier's inputs and outputs, and classify the inputs as factors of production</p>", "hint_html": "<p>A business transforms inputs into outputs [Slides 3-4]. The classic factors are land, labour, capital and entrepreneurship.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Output: a service (drone delivery), not a good.</li><li>Land: the hangar site; labour: pilots and technicians; capital: drones, equipment and the investors' money; entrepreneurship: the founders who coordinate and bear the risk.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>SkyCourier's <strong>output</strong> is a <strong>service</strong>, the delivery of medicines and parcels. Its <strong>inputs</strong> are the factors of production [Slides 3-4]:</p>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Factor</th><th>In SkyCourier</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Land (natural resources)</td><td>The rented hangar site</td></tr><tr><td>Labour</td><td>The twelve pilots and technicians</td></tr><tr><td>Capital</td><td>Drones, charging stations, software; the money provided by investors</td></tr><tr><td>Entrepreneurship</td><td>The founders who bring the other factors together and take the risk</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The business activity is precisely the transformation of these inputs into the delivery service.</p>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Describe the real flows and the financial flows between SkyCourier and the households, naming the two markets in which they take place</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Every exchange has two sides that run in opposite directions [Slide 11]. Households own the factors and are the units of consumption [Slide 7].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Factor market: households deliver land, labour and capital; SkyCourier pays rent, wages and dividends.</li><li>Goods and services market: SkyCourier delivers the service; households pay for it.</li><li>Real and financial flows run in opposite directions; what the firm keeps is its profit.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>The circular flow of income links firms and households through two markets [Slides 5-11]:</p>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Market</th><th>Real flow</th><th>Financial flow (opposite direction)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Factor market</strong></td><td>Households → SkyCourier: the hangar (land), work (labour), savings invested in shares (capital)</td><td>SkyCourier → households: rent to the farmer, wages to the staff, dividends to the shareholders</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Market of goods and services</strong></td><td>SkyCourier → households: the delivery service</td><td>Households → SkyCourier: the price of the deliveries, paid out of their wages</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The flows close a circle: the wages paid by SkyCourier return to it, in part, when the families buy its home-delivery service. The <strong>real flows</strong> are the factors and the service; the <strong>financial flows</strong> are their monetary compensation, running the other way [Slide 11]. What SkyCourier keeps after paying all the factors is its <strong>profit</strong>, from which the dividend was paid.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": null}, {"id": "C02", "category": "short_theory_case", "archetype": "theory-case", "theme": "functional-areas", "unit": 1, "title": "Orbital Fasteners: sorting a day of activity into the functional areas", "difficulty": 1, "points": 2.0, "time_minutes": 15, "tags": ["theory-case", "functional-areas", "management-functions"], "official": false, "source": "Practice case written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), based on Topic 1_The Firm_Types  Objectives.pdf, Slides 13-15", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank C02 - Orbital Fasteners Functional Areas.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>Orbital Fasteners S.A. makes titanium bolts certified for satellites. On a single Monday: (1) the purchasing team ordered 2 tonnes of titanium bar; (2) the CNC lines produced 40,000 bolts; (3) the sales engineer presented the company at a space-industry fair; (4) the finance department negotiated a bank loan to buy a new thread-rolling machine; (5) it collected an invoice from a satellite integrator and paid the titanium supplier; (6) HR interviewed two quality inspectors; (7) the plant manager set the production targets for the next quarter and checked last month's scrap rate against the plan; (8) the IT team upgraded the traceability database.</p>", "setup_html": null, "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Classify the eight activities into real, financial and management functions</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Real functions follow the product (supply, production, marketing, support); financial functions follow the money (financing, investing, paying and collecting); management functions are planning, organization and control [Slide 13].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Real: 1 (supply), 2 (production), 3 (marketing), 6 and 8 (support: HR, information systems).</li><li>Financial: 4 (financing and investing), 5 (collecting revenues and paying for inputs).</li><li>Management: 7 (planning and control).</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Activity</th><th>Function</th><th>Group [Slide 13]</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1. Ordering titanium</td><td>Supply (acquisition of inputs)</td><td>Real</td></tr><tr><td>2. Producing bolts</td><td>Production</td><td>Real</td></tr><tr><td>3. Trade fair</td><td>Marketing</td><td>Real</td></tr><tr><td>4. Bank loan for a machine</td><td>Financing and investing</td><td>Financial</td></tr><tr><td>5. Collecting and paying</td><td>Receiving revenues and paying for inputs</td><td>Financial</td></tr><tr><td>6. Hiring inspectors</td><td>Support (Human Resources)</td><td>Real</td></tr><tr><td>7. Targets and scrap check</td><td>Planning and control</td><td>Management</td></tr><tr><td>8. Traceability database</td><td>Support (Information Systems)</td><td>Real</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Explain how the real and the financial cycles relate, and what engineers contribute to the management of a firm like this one</p>", "hint_html": "<p>The financial functions run alongside the real cycle [Slide 13]; recall the role of engineers in business management [Slide 15].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>The financial cycle mirrors the real one: funds raised → inputs paid → revenues collected → capital and interest returned.</li><li>Engineers: project planning, time management, problem solving, attention to detail, data processing, technical knowledge.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>The <strong>financial cycle runs alongside the real cycle</strong> [Slide 13]: the company raises funds (the loan of activity 4), uses them to pay for inputs (the titanium of activity 1, paid in activity 5), turns the inputs into bolts and sells them, collects the revenue (activity 5) and finally returns capital and interest to those who lent the money. Without the financial functions the real cycle stops; without the real cycle there is no revenue to repay the funds.</p>\n<p>Engineers bring <strong>project planning, time management and problem solving, attention to detail, experience in data processing, computer skills and technical knowledge</strong> [Slide 15]: exactly what activity 7 (targets and scrap control) and activity 8 (traceability data) require.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": null}, {"id": "C03", "category": "short_theory_case", "archetype": "theory-case", "theme": "management-process", "unit": 1, "title": "Atlas Line Maintenance: the four stages of the management process in a ramp-up", "difficulty": 1, "points": 2.0, "time_minutes": 15, "tags": ["theory-case", "management-process", "fayol"], "official": false, "source": "Practice case written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), based on Topic 1_The Firm_Types  Objectives.pdf, Slide 14", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank C03 - Atlas Line Maintenance Management Process.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>Atlas Line Maintenance S.L. has won a contract to perform the overnight checks of a low-cost airline at a new base. The station manager, Marta, has six months to be ready. She sets the goal of \"zero technical delays in the first summer\" and a plan to hire and certify 25 mechanics. She draws the shift structure and decides who is responsible for each aircraft type and which tools each shift needs. During the first weeks she holds daily briefings, encourages the team and mediates a conflict between the night and day shifts over handovers. Every Friday she compares the delays and the hours per check with the targets and adjusts the shifts.</p>", "setup_html": null, "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Identify the four stages of the management process in Marta's actions</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Henri Fayol's formulation: planning, organizing, leading, controlling [Slide 14].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Planning: the zero-delay goal and the hiring and certification plan.</li><li>Organizing: shift structure, responsibilities, tools.</li><li>Leading: daily briefings, motivation, resolving the shift conflict.</li><li>Controlling: weekly comparison of delays and hours with the targets.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Stage [Slide 14]</th><th>Content</th><th>At Atlas</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Planning</strong></td><td>Defining goals, establishing strategy, developing subplans</td><td>\"Zero technical delays\"; plan to hire and certify 25 mechanics</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Organizing</strong></td><td>What must be done, how, and by whom</td><td>Shift structure, responsibilities per aircraft type, tools per shift</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Leading</strong></td><td>Directing and motivating, resolving conflicts</td><td>Daily briefings, encouragement, mediation between shifts</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Controlling</strong></td><td>Monitoring that activities go as planned</td><td>Friday comparison of delays and hours per check with the targets</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Explain why the four stages form a cycle and what Marta does when she finds a deviation</p>", "hint_html": "<p>The four activities repeat around one common aim: achieving the organization's stated purpose [Slide 14].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Control results feed the next round of planning: the process is cyclical.</li><li>A deviation triggers corrective action: reorganizing the shifts or revising the plan, then leading the change.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>The management process is a <strong>cycle</strong> around the organization's purpose [Slide 14]. Control is not the end: when the Friday review shows a deviation (for example, more hours per check than planned), Marta corrects it by <strong>reorganizing</strong> the shifts or by <strong>revising the plan</strong> (more mechanics, new targets), and she must then <strong>lead</strong> the team through the change. The new results are controlled the following week, so the four stages keep repeating while the base operates.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": null}, {"id": "C04", "category": "short_theory_case", "archetype": "theory-case", "theme": "entrepreneur", "unit": 1, "title": "An eVTOL founder: the four views of the entrepreneur and her rights", "difficulty": 2, "points": 2.0, "time_minutes": 15, "tags": ["theory-case", "entrepreneur", "residual-income"], "official": false, "source": "Practice case written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), based on Topic 1_The Firm_Types  Objectives.pdf, Slides 16-18", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank C04 - eVTOL Founder Views of the Entrepreneur.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>Lucía, an aerospace engineer, noticed in 2023 that city hospitals needed faster transfers of organs than road ambulances could offer, and that nobody was serving that need. She designed an electric vertical-take-off aircraft with a new battery-cooling system, mortgaged her flat to fund the first prototype, and brought together a team of engineers, a supplier of carbon structures and a hangar owner. Today she signs every contract of the company, decides who works on each project, keeps whatever profit is left once everyone has been paid, and has been offered a large sum by an airline for her stake.</p>", "setup_html": null, "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Relate Lucía's behaviour to the four views of the entrepreneur</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Coordinator; risk taker (Knight, 1921); innovator (Schumpeter, 1950); \"on alert\" (Kirzner, 1973) [Slide 17].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>On alert (Kirzner): she detected an unserved need.</li><li>Innovator (Schumpeter): a new aircraft and battery-cooling system.</li><li>Risk taker (Knight): she mortgaged her flat.</li><li>Coordinator: she brought together engineers, supplier and hangar owner.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>View [Slide 17]</th><th>Evidence in the case</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>\"On alert\"</strong> (Kirzner, 1973)</td><td>She detected a new business opportunity, a need nobody was serving</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Innovator</strong> (Schumpeter, 1950)</td><td>She introduced a new product with a new battery-cooling technology</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Risk taker</strong> (Knight, 1921)</td><td>She risked her own flat to finance the prototype</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Coordinator</strong></td><td>She brought land (hangar), labour (engineers) and capital (supplier, funds) together</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Together these are the functions of the entrepreneur: coordinate and control production, ensure payment of the factors, take risky decisions, innovate, and detect opportunities [Slide 17].</p>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Explain which rights make Lucía \"the entrepreneur\" of her company</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Team production needs a controller, and the incentive to be one comes from four rights [Slide 18].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Right to the residual income (the profit left after paying the factors).</li><li>Right to control and reassign the other factors (who works on each project).</li><li>Being the central party in all contracts.</li><li>Right to sell these rights (the airline's offer).</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>A firm is teamwork, and team production needs someone who controls it; the incentive to take that role comes from four <strong>rights</strong> [Slide 18]. Lucía holds all of them: she receives the <strong>residual income</strong> (the profit left once every factor is paid); she <strong>controls and reassigns</strong> the other factors (she decides who works on each project); she is the <strong>central party in all the contracts</strong> with the inputs (she signs them); and she can <strong>sell these rights</strong> (the airline's offer for her stake). The person who holds all these rights is, by definition, the entrepreneur.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": null}, {"id": "C05", "category": "short_theory_case", "archetype": "theory-case", "theme": "legal-forms", "unit": 1, "title": "Three engineers choosing a legal form for a composite-repair shop", "difficulty": 2, "points": 2.0, "time_minutes": 15, "tags": ["theory-case", "legal-forms", "liability"], "official": false, "source": "Practice case written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), based on Topic 1_The Firm_Types  Objectives.pdf, Slides 20-24", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank C05 - Three Engineers Choosing a Legal Form.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>Ana, Bruno and Carla want to open a shop that repairs composite parts of light aircraft. Ana would invest 60,000 m.u. of savings; Bruno and Carla only their work. A single faulty repair could lead to claims far larger than everything they own, so they worry about losing their homes. They want to start quickly and cheaply, but in five years they hope to open three more shops with outside investors, and they want the business to continue if one of them leaves.</p>", "setup_html": null, "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Compare the sole proprietorship, the partnership and the corporation on the features that matter to the three engineers</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Look at the number of owners, liability, ease of starting, permanence and ability to raise funds [Slides 21-24].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Sole proprietorship: one owner only, unlimited liability: ruled out.</li><li>Partnership: easy to start, pooled talents; unlimited and joint liability, relative impermanence.</li><li>Corporation: limited liability, permanence, easy transfer of shares, greater financial capability; complex and costly, double taxation.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Sole proprietorship</th><th>Partnership</th><th>Corporation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Owners</td><td>One person</td><td>Two or more co-owners</td><td>Shareholders</td></tr><tr><td>Liability</td><td>Unlimited</td><td><strong>Unlimited and joint</strong></td><td><strong>Limited</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Starting</td><td>Simple</td><td>Few restrictions</td><td>Complex and costly</td></tr><tr><td>Permanence</td><td>Impermanent</td><td>Relatively impermanent</td><td>Permanent</td></tr><tr><td>Funds</td><td>Hard to raise</td><td>Pooled funds and talents</td><td>Greater financial capability</td></tr><tr><td>Taxation</td><td>No separate tax</td><td>No separate tax</td><td>Special and double taxation</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>A sole proprietorship belongs to <strong>one</strong> person [Slide 21], so it does not fit three owners. A partnership is easy to set up through a partnership agreement and pools Ana's money with Bruno's and Carla's skills [Slide 22], but each partner answers for the firm's debts with all their personal wealth, jointly [Slide 23]. A corporation is a separate legal entity whose owners have <strong>limited</strong> liability [Slide 24].</p>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Recommend a legal form and justify it with the engineers' goals</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Weigh their biggest fear (claims) and their plans (outside investors, continuity) against the cost of forming the company.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Recommend a corporation: limited liability protects their homes; permanence; easy entry of investors through shares.</li><li>Accept its drawbacks: cost of formation, regulation and disclosure, double taxation.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>The <strong>corporation</strong> fits best. Limited liability addresses their main fear: a large claim would affect the company's assets, not their homes. It is <strong>permanent</strong>, so the business continues if one partner leaves, and ownership is <strong>easily transferred through shares</strong>, which is how the outside investors of the expansion would enter; it also has greater financial capability [Slide 24]. The price is a more complex and costly formation, government regulation and disclosure requirements, and double taxation; the engineers should accept these costs in exchange for protection and growth. The course also lists forms of partnership that limit liability: the limited liability partnership (LLP) [Slide 20] and the limited partnership, where some partners answer only up to what they invested [Slide 29]. An LLP would also meet the fear of claims, but the corporation fits better with the plans for outside investors and continuity.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": null}, {"id": "C06", "category": "short_theory_case", "archetype": "theory-case", "theme": "agency-problem", "unit": 1, "title": "Meseta Aerostructures: a CEO, dispersed shareholders and the agency problem", "difficulty": 2, "points": 2.0, "time_minutes": 15, "tags": ["theory-case", "agency-problem", "corporate-governance"], "official": false, "source": "Practice case written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), based on Topic 1_The Firm_Types  Objectives.pdf, Slides 25-26, and Topic 2_ Value creation.pdf, Slide 4", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank C06 - Meseta Aerostructures Agency Problem.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>Meseta Aerostructures S.A. is listed on the stock exchange; no shareholder owns more than 2% of its shares. Its CEO, a professional manager, is paid a fixed salary that grows with the number of employees under her. In three years she has bought two loss-making companies, moved headquarters to an expensive tower and ordered a corporate jet, while the share price has fallen by 30%. The annual report gives little detail on these decisions, and most small shareholders do not attend the general meeting.</p>", "setup_html": null, "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Explain why this situation is an agency problem</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Who owns the company and who controls it? Compare their objectives and the information each side has [Slide 25].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Ownership (shareholders) is separated from control (the professional CEO).</li><li>The CEO pursues her own benefit (size, prestige) instead of the shareholders' (value).</li><li>Different access to information and different objectives; dispersed shareholders lack information and incentives to monitor.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>In large corporations the company is run by <strong>managers</strong>, professional employees, while it is owned by the <strong>shareholders</strong>: ownership is separated from control [Slide 25]. At Meseta, the CEO's pay rises with the number of employees, so her objective is <strong>size and prestige</strong> (acquisitions, tower, jet), not the value of the shares. Shareholders and managers have <strong>different objectives</strong> and <strong>different access to information</strong> (the report gives little detail), and with stakes below 2% no shareholder has enough information or <strong>incentive</strong> to assess her performance. The result is a conflict of interest between ownership and control: the <strong>agency problem</strong>.</p>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Propose mechanisms that would align the CEO's decisions with the shareholders' interests</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Use the control mechanisms of [Slide 26] and the alignment mechanisms of Topic 2 [Slide 4].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Select a Board of Directors that supervises the CEO.</li><li>Contracts linked to goals (pay tied to profit or share value instead of headcount); incentive compensation.</li><li>Audits and indicators.</li><li>External discipline: the market of enterprises (takeover risk) and the labour market for managers.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>Shareholders must be able to control how the manager acts [Topic 1, Slide 26]:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Select the Board of Directors</strong> so that it actually supervises the CEO and approves large acquisitions.</li><li><strong>Contracts linked to goals:</strong> replace pay tied to headcount with incentive compensation tied to profit or share value [Topic 2, Slide 4].</li><li><strong>Request audits</strong> of the acquisitions and expenses, and publish <strong>indicators</strong> that let shareholders judge performance.</li><li>Outside the firm, the <strong>market of enterprises</strong> (a falling share price invites a takeover that would replace the CEO) and the <strong>labour market</strong> for managers (her reputation) also discipline her [Topic 2, Slide 4].</li></ol>\n<p>Increasingly, shareholders with large blocks of shares take the decisions [Slide 26]; a group of institutional investors could build such a block at Meseta.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": null}, {"id": "C07", "category": "short_theory_case", "archetype": "theory-case", "theme": "other-forms", "unit": 1, "title": "A drone-spraying cooperative and a launcher joint venture", "difficulty": 2, "points": 2.0, "time_minutes": 15, "tags": ["theory-case", "cooperative", "joint-venture"], "official": false, "source": "Practice case written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), based on Topic 1_The Firm_Types  Objectives.pdf, Slides 27-29", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank C07 - Drone Spraying Cooperative and a Launcher Joint Venture.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>Two situations in the same region:</p>\n<p><strong>Case 1.</strong> Forty vineyard owners cannot each afford a crop-spraying drone. They create an organization that buys three drones and hires two pilots; any vineyard owner of the area may join, each member has one vote in the assembly regardless of size, and at the end of the year what is left after paying the expenses is returned to each member in proportion to the hectares sprayed.</p>\n<p><strong>Case 2.</strong> A European launcher manufacturer and a Japanese engine company set up a company owned 50/50 for the sole purpose of developing a reusable upper stage over eight years. When the stage is certified, the company will be dissolved.</p>", "setup_html": null, "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Identify the form of business ownership in each case and justify it with its defining features</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Compare Case 1 with the five principles of a cooperative [Slide 27], and Case 2 with the \"other forms\" of [Slide 29].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Case 1: cooperative association (open membership, democratic control, benefits in proportion to participation, user-members are owners); closest type: a buying co-op.</li><li>Case 2: joint venture: a special partnership for a specific task or project, common in international business.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p><strong>Case 1 is a cooperative association</strong> [Slides 27-28]: an incorporated organization whose <strong>user-members are also its owners</strong> and get back the revenue left after expenses. It follows the cooperative principles: <strong>open membership</strong> (any vineyard owner may join), <strong>democratic control</strong> (one member, one vote), and <strong>benefits distributed in proportion to each member's participation</strong> (hectares sprayed). Of the course's common types, the closest is a <strong>buying co-op</strong>, which helps members buy at lower prices (here, a spraying service); it is not an agricultural co-op in the course's sense, which helps member farmers <strong>market</strong> their products [Slide 28].</p>\n<p><strong>Case 2 is a joint venture</strong> [Slide 29]: a special type of partnership set up by firms to <strong>accomplish a specific task or project</strong>, the reusable upper stage, and dissolved once the task is done. It is very common in <strong>international business</strong>, as here.</p>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Explain one advantage of each form for its members</p>", "hint_html": "<p>What could the members not achieve alone?</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Co-op: access to expensive equipment and lower costs, with democratic control and fair sharing.</li><li>Joint venture: sharing a large, risky project and combining complementary know-how across countries without merging the parent companies.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>The <strong>cooperative</strong> gives small vineyard owners access to equipment that none could buy alone, at cost, while keeping control democratic and sharing the surplus fairly. The <strong>joint venture</strong> lets two firms from different countries <strong>share the cost and risk</strong> of a long, uncertain project and <strong>combine complementary know-how</strong> (launcher and engines) without merging the whole companies; each parent keeps its independence in the rest of its business. These advantages are reasoning applied to the case; the deck defines the forms but does not list their advantages.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": null}, {"id": "C08", "category": "short_theory_case", "archetype": "theory-case", "theme": "enterprise-types", "unit": 1, "title": "Classifying three aerospace enterprises, and why firms exist at all", "difficulty": 1, "points": 2.0, "time_minutes": 15, "tags": ["theory-case", "types-of-enterprises", "market-and-enterprise"], "official": false, "source": "Practice case written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), based on Topic 1_The Firm_Types  Objectives.pdf, Slides 12 and 19", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank C08 - Classifying Three Aerospace Enterprises.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>Consider three enterprises:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>TitanMine</strong>, a private company with 35 employees that extracts titanium ore in one region and sells it only in its own country;</li><li><strong>AeroNational</strong>, a state-owned manufacturer of military transport aircraft with 9,000 employees, plants in three countries and customers in twenty;</li><li><strong>Wingtip Consulting</strong>, a one-person business that advises flying schools on safety management in several markets of one country.</li></ul>\n<p>A classmate claims: \"Firms are unnecessary; each engineer could simply sell their work to whoever needs it on the market.\"</p>", "setup_html": null, "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Classify the three enterprises using the economic and legal criteria of the course</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Economic: sector, size, scope of the business. Legal: ownership of the factors, form of ownership [Slide 19].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>TitanMine: primary, small, one market and national, private.</li><li>AeroNational: secondary, big, multi-market and multinational, state-owned; corporation.</li><li>Wingtip: tertiary, small, multi-market and national, private, sole proprietorship.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Criterion [Slide 19]</th><th>TitanMine</th><th>AeroNational</th><th>Wingtip Consulting</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sector</td><td>Primary (extraction)</td><td>Secondary (manufacturing)</td><td>Tertiary (services)</td></tr><tr><td>Size</td><td>Small</td><td>Big</td><td>Small</td></tr><tr><td>Scope</td><td>One market, national</td><td>Multi-market, multinational</td><td>Multi-market, national</td></tr><tr><td>Ownership of the factors</td><td>Private</td><td>State-owned</td><td>Private</td></tr><tr><td>Form of ownership</td><td>Company (likely a corporation)</td><td>Corporation</td><td>Sole proprietorship</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The deck gives the criteria and their classes but no thresholds; reading 35 employees as small and 9,000 as big is the usual interpretation (extension).</p>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Answer the classmate using the course's explanation of why enterprises exist</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Scarce resources, unlimited wants, specialization and the two ways of coordinating it [Slide 12].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Wants are met through specialization (division of labour), which requires coordination.</li><li>Coordination can be done by the market or by the enterprise; the enterprise appears where there is a dissatisfied need that it coordinates better.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>Economics studies how scarce resources are used to satisfy unlimited wants; those wants are met through <strong>specialization</strong>, the division of labour, and specialization requires <strong>coordination</strong> [Slide 12]. Coordination can be carried out either by the <strong>market</strong>, as the classmate suggests, or by the <strong>enterprise</strong>. Building a transport aircraft requires thousands of specialists working together on one product: coordinating them through separate market contracts would be impractical, so an enterprise appears to coordinate them (reasoning applied to the case, beyond the slides). In the course's words, an enterprise appears because there is a <strong>dissatisfied need</strong> [Slide 12]. Wingtip shows that, for simple services, one person selling directly to the market can also work.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": null}, {"id": "C09", "category": "short_theory_case", "archetype": "theory-case", "theme": "objectives", "unit": 2, "title": "Costa Airport: profit maximization or value creation?", "difficulty": 2, "points": 2.0, "time_minutes": 15, "tags": ["theory-case", "business-objectives", "value-creation"], "official": false, "source": "Practice case written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), based on Topic 2_ Value creation.pdf, Slides 3-7", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank C09 - Costa Airport Profit Maximization or Value Creation.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>A private company operates Costa Airport. A new board member argues: \"Our only objective is to maximize this year's profit: let's double parking fees, cut the night shift of air-traffic support staff and postpone the runway resurfacing.\" The finance director replies that the board should instead aim to \"maximize the value of the company\", and the operations director warns that the airline customers, the unions, the city council and the regional government will all react to such measures.</p>", "setup_html": null, "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Explain the limitations of profit maximization as the objective of the firm, using the board member's proposal</p>", "hint_html": "<p>The Classical Theory's objective has four limitations [Slide 3].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Profit is hard to define and quantify (this year vs future years).</li><li>It ignores the risk of achieving the profits (postponed resurfacing, safety, reaction of airlines).</li><li>The very idea of \"maximizing\" is questionable.</li><li>It gives an excessive role to the entrepreneur, whereas ownership and control are separated.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>The proposal follows the classical objective, <strong>profit maximization</strong>, which has four limitations [Slide 3]:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Profit is difficult to define and to quantify:</strong> cutting maintenance raises this year's profit but lowers future profits (a degraded runway, a closure for emergency works).</li><li><strong>It ignores risk:</strong> the cuts raise the risk of incidents and of losing airlines, which a single profit figure does not show.</li><li><strong>The concept of maximization itself is questionable</strong> [Slide 3]; in practice managers aim at a minimum level of profit that satisfies the shareholders [Slide 4].</li><li><strong>It gives an excessive role to the entrepreneur:</strong> in a company like this, ownership and control are separated, and the board, managers and shareholders have different goals.</li></ol>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Explain why the finance director's objective is the one the course proposes, and how stakeholders shape it</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Organizational theory: objectives are the outcome of a negotiation among stakeholders, limited by survival and unequal power [Slides 5-6]. Recall the unified vision of survival, profit and growth [Slide 7].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Objectives result from negotiation among stakeholders (shareholders, customers, employees, State, society...).</li><li>Constraints: the firm must survive; groups have different bargaining power.</li><li>The resulting objective: maximize the value of the enterprise (create value), linking survival, profit and growth.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>According to <strong>organizational theory</strong>, the objectives of the firm are the <strong>outcome of a negotiation among its stakeholders</strong>: here the shareholders, the airlines (customers), the employees and unions, the city council and regional government (the State), suppliers, banks and society [Slides 5-6]. The negotiation is limited because the firm must <strong>survive</strong> and because <strong>not all groups have the same power</strong>. The objective that satisfies the most powerful group while respecting the others and the firm's survival is to <strong>maximize the value of the enterprise</strong>, to create value [Slide 6]. In the unified vision, value comes from a cycle in which sales generate profits, retained profits finance investments (such as the resurfacing) and these renew the products and markets [Slide 7]; sacrificing the investment for one year's profit breaks that cycle.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": null}, {"id": "C10", "category": "short_theory_case", "archetype": "theory-case", "theme": "stakeholders", "unit": 2, "title": "Closing an engine-parts plant: mapping the stakeholders and their power", "difficulty": 2, "points": 2.0, "time_minutes": 15, "tags": ["theory-case", "stakeholders", "organizational-theory"], "official": false, "source": "Practice case written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), based on Topic 2_ Value creation.pdf, Slides 4-6", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank C10 - Engine Plant Closure and its Stakeholders.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>TurboCast S.A., a maker of turbine blades, plans to close its oldest plant, in a town of 8,000 inhabitants where it employs 600 people, and move production to a new automated plant. The shareholders support the plan; the main bank, which financed the new plant, wants it to happen fast. The unions call a strike. The regional government offers a subsidy if the plant stays open two more years. The engine manufacturer that buys 70% of the blades demands that deliveries are not interrupted. Local suppliers and environmental groups also make their voices heard.</p>", "setup_html": null, "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Identify the stakeholders in the case and what each one wants</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Around management stand shareholders, banks and financial agents, customers, suppliers, the State, employees and unions, and society [Slide 5].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Shareholders: higher profit and value through automation.</li><li>Bank: repayment of the loan for the new plant.</li><li>Employees and unions: keep their jobs.</li><li>State (regional government): employment in the region.</li><li>Customer (engine maker): uninterrupted deliveries.</li><li>Suppliers and society: local business, environment.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Stakeholder [Slide 5]</th><th>In the case</th><th>What it wants</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Shareholders</td><td>Owners of TurboCast</td><td>Lower costs and higher value through automation</td></tr><tr><td>Banks and financial agents</td><td>Lender of the new plant</td><td>Repayment; a fast transition that makes the plant profitable</td></tr><tr><td>Employees and unions</td><td>600 workers</td><td>To keep their jobs and conditions</td></tr><tr><td>The State</td><td>Regional government</td><td>Employment and activity in the region</td></tr><tr><td>Customers</td><td>Engine manufacturer</td><td>Uninterrupted, on-time deliveries</td></tr><tr><td>Suppliers</td><td>Local suppliers</td><td>To keep their contracts</td></tr><tr><td>Society</td><td>Town, environmental groups</td><td>Local activity, responsible behaviour (CSR)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Explain, using organizational theory, how the final decision is likely to be reached</p>", "hint_html": "<p>The objective is negotiated, limited by survival and by unequal bargaining power [Slide 6].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>The decision is a negotiated outcome, not one group's wish.</li><li>It satisfies the group with the most bargaining power (here the engine maker, which buys 70%, together with shareholders and bank) while taking the others into account.</li><li>Constraint of survival: TurboCast must stay competitive.</li><li>A likely compromise: a phased closure (subsidy, two years), guaranteed deliveries, relocation or retraining.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>Organizational theory sees the objective as the <strong>outcome of a negotiation among stakeholders</strong>, limited by two facts: the firm must <strong>survive</strong>, and <strong>not all groups have the same power</strong> [Slide 6]. Here the engine manufacturer has very high bargaining power (70% of sales), as do the shareholders and the bank; the unions and the government have less power but can impose costs (strikes, subsidies withdrawn). The likely outcome is a <strong>compromise</strong>: the move to the automated plant goes ahead, because survival requires competitive costs, but it is <strong>phased</strong> over two years with the regional subsidy, deliveries are protected with stock built in advance, and part of the staff is relocated or retrained. That decision satisfies the most powerful groups while taking the others into account, which is what maximizing the value of the enterprise means in practice.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": null}, {"id": "C11", "category": "short_theory_case", "archetype": "theory-case", "theme": "generic-environment", "unit": 2, "title": "A small-launcher start-up reads its generic environment", "difficulty": 1, "points": 2.0, "time_minutes": 15, "tags": ["theory-case", "generic-environment", "pestel"], "official": false, "source": "Practice case written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), based on Topic 2_ Value creation.pdf, Slides 11-13", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank C11 - Small Launcher Generic Environment.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>Altair Launch S.L. is developing a rocket for satellites under 200 kg. In its business plan it lists: interest rates have doubled in two years and venture capital is scarcer; the European Union has created a fund to secure independent access to space, and a new national space law sets licensing rules; additive manufacturing has made engine parts much cheaper, while reusable rockets are becoming a key technology; the country trains many aerospace engineers, but public opinion is increasingly worried about space debris and the noise of the launch site.</p>", "setup_html": null, "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Classify each element into the four groups of factors of the generic environment</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Economic, political-legal, socio-cultural and technological factors [Slide 11]. (Many textbooks use PESTEL, which separates environmental and legal factors; the course uses four groups.)</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Economic: interest rates, scarce venture capital.</li><li>Political-legal: EU fund, national space law and licensing.</li><li>Socio-cultural: supply of trained engineers (education), public concern about debris and noise (value system).</li><li>Technological: additive manufacturing (key), reusability (incipient/key).</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Group [Slide 11]</th><th>Element of the case</th><th>Effect on Altair</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Economic</strong></td><td>Higher interest rates, less venture capital</td><td>Financing becomes harder and costlier</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Political-legal</strong></td><td>EU fund for independent access to space; national space law and licences</td><td>Opportunity (public demand and funding) and a regulatory requirement</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Socio-cultural</strong></td><td>Many trained engineers (level of education); concern about debris and noise (value system)</td><td>Easier hiring; pressure on the launch site and on debris mitigation</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Technological</strong></td><td>Additive manufacturing; reusable rockets</td><td>Cheaper engines; a key technology that may become a must</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The course's four groups have no separate environmental factor: the table places the public's concern under the socio-cultural value system (public opinion), while the outside-in view of [Slide 13] also lists the natural environment among the forces around the industry. The PESTEL framework of other books would treat debris and noise as a separate environmental factor (extension).</p>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Explain the difference between the generic and the specific environment and why Altair needs both</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Who is affected by each layer, and what is analysed at the centre [Slides 11-13]?</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Generic environment: factors that affect every firm in the country/economy.</li><li>Specific environment: the firm's own industry (rivalry, entry and exit barriers, substitutes, customers, suppliers), analysed with Porter's five forces.</li><li>The analysis moves from the outside in.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>The <strong>generic environment</strong> affects all firms, whatever their industry: the interest rates or the space law hit every company in the country [Slide 11]. The <strong>specific environment</strong> is the environment of the firm's <strong>own industry</strong>: the rivalry among launch providers, entry and exit barriers, substitutes (for example rideshare launches on large rockets), and the relationships with customers and suppliers [Slide 12]. The analysis moves <strong>from the outside in</strong>, from the economy, technology and politics to the industry at the centre [Slide 13]. Altair needs both: the generic factors explain the conditions it cannot change, and the specific ones explain whether it can earn money against its direct competitors.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": null}, {"id": "C12", "category": "short_theory_case", "archetype": "theory-case", "theme": "five-forces", "unit": 2, "title": "Island regional airlines under Porter's five forces", "difficulty": 2, "points": 2.0, "time_minutes": 20, "tags": ["theory-case", "porter-five-forces", "industry-analysis"], "official": false, "source": "Practice case written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), based on Topic 2_ Value creation.pdf, Slides 12-15", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank C12 - Island Regional Airline Five Forces.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>Three airlines fly between the islands of an archipelago with similar turboprops and similar fares; none is clearly larger. Two manufacturers in the world build turboprops of that size, and a single company supplies the jet fuel at the airports. Most passengers are price-sensitive residents who compare fares online, plus the regional government, which buys tickets for public employees. A fast-ferry company has just started services between the two main islands. To start a new airline one needs aircraft, an operating certificate and slots at airports that are almost full.</p>", "setup_html": null, "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.5, "prompt_html": "<p>Analyse the five competitive forces of this industry, rating each one as high, medium or low with a reason</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Rivalry, supplier power, buyer power, threat of entry, threat of substitutes, each with its determinants [Slides 14-15].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Rivalry: high (three similar firms, little differentiation, no leader).</li><li>Suppliers: high (two aircraft makers, one fuel supplier).</li><li>Buyers: high (price-sensitive residents; the government as a large buyer).</li><li>New entrants: low-medium (capital, certificate, full slots: legal and access barriers).</li><li>Substitutes: medium-high (fast ferry on the main route).</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Force [Slides 14-15]</th><th>Rating</th><th>Determinants in the case</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Rivalry among existing firms</strong></td><td>High</td><td>Three firms of similar size (low concentration, no leader), similar product and fares (little differentiation)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Bargaining power of suppliers</strong></td><td>High</td><td>Only two turboprop manufacturers and a single fuel supplier: high relative bargaining power</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Bargaining power of buyers</strong></td><td>High</td><td>Residents are price-sensitive and compare online; the government buys in volume</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Threat of new entrants</strong></td><td>Low to medium</td><td>Capital requirements (aircraft), legal and regulatory barriers (operating certificate), access to \"distribution\" (airport slots almost full)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Threat of substitutes</strong></td><td>Medium to high</td><td>The fast ferry: buyers' propensity to substitute depends on its relative price and travel time</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 0.5, "prompt_html": "<p>Conclude on the attractiveness of the industry and suggest one strategic response for an airline</p>", "hint_html": "<p>The stronger the forces, the lower the profitability the industry allows. Connect your answer with the sources of competitive advantage [Slide 20].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Low attractiveness: four of five forces are strong.</li><li>A response: cost advantage (fleet standardization, high capacity utilization) or differentiation (schedule, connections, service) that weakens buyer power; possibly cooperation or consolidation to reduce rivalry.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>With strong rivalry, strong suppliers, strong buyers and a growing substitute, the industry is <strong>unattractive</strong>: the profit available is squeezed from every side, and only the entry barriers protect it. An airline can respond by building a <strong>cost advantage</strong> (a single aircraft type [Slide 21], high capacity utilization [Slide 22]) to survive the price war, or a <strong>differentiation advantage</strong> (frequent schedules, connections with long-haul flights, loyalty schemes for residents) that reduces buyers' price sensitivity [Slides 23-24].</p>"}], "interpretation_html": null}, {"id": "C13", "category": "short_theory_case", "archetype": "theory-case", "theme": "barriers-to-entry", "unit": 2, "title": "Can a car-seat maker enter the aircraft-seat market? Barriers to entry", "difficulty": 2, "points": 2.0, "time_minutes": 15, "tags": ["theory-case", "barriers-to-entry", "diversification"], "official": false, "source": "Practice case written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), based on Topic 2_ Value creation.pdf, Slides 12, 15 and 25", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank C13 - Aircraft Seat Market Barriers to Entry.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>ComfortAuto S.A., a large maker of car seats, wants to enter the market of passenger seats for commercial aircraft. Three established firms supply almost all airlines. Each new seat model must pass crash and flammability certification that takes two years. Airlines buy seats through long contracts linked to aircraft orders, and the established firms have decades of reputation for reliability. They produce in large volumes in plants built for this purpose, and they own patents on lightweight seat frames. ComfortAuto, however, has strong engineering, low-cost purchasing of foam and fabrics, and spare plant capacity.</p>", "setup_html": null, "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Identify the barriers to entry ComfortAuto faces, using the determinants of the threat of entry</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Capital requirements, economies of scale, absolute cost advantage, product differentiation, access to distribution channels, legal and regulatory barriers [Slide 15].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Legal and regulatory: two-year certification; patents.</li><li>Product differentiation: reputation for reliability.</li><li>Access to distribution channels: long contracts linked to aircraft orders.</li><li>Economies of scale: incumbents' volumes in dedicated plants.</li><li>Capital requirements and absolute cost advantages (patents, experience).</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Determinant [Slide 15]</th><th>In the case</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Legal and regulatory barriers</strong></td><td>Two years of crash and flammability certification per model; patents on lightweight frames</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Product differentiation</strong></td><td>The incumbents' decades of reputation for reliability make airlines reluctant to switch</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Access to distribution channels</strong></td><td>Seats are sold through long contracts tied to aircraft orders, already held by the incumbents</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Economies of scale</strong></td><td>Incumbents produce large volumes in dedicated plants, so their unit cost is lower</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Absolute cost advantage</strong></td><td>Patents and accumulated experience (economies of learning)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Capital requirements</strong></td><td>Certification programmes and adapting a plant require large investment before any sale</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>These barriers make the threat of entry low and protect the profitability of the three incumbents.</p>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Assess whether the diversification makes sense for ComfortAuto</p>", "hint_html": "<p>A diversification decision asks two questions: how attractive is the sector, and can the firm achieve a competitive advantage in it [Slide 25]?</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Attractive sector: high barriers keep profitability high for those inside.</li><li>Competitive advantage: ComfortAuto has transferable strengths (engineering, cheap inputs, spare capacity) but lacks certification, reputation and contracts.</li><li>Related diversification: a gradual entry (a niche, a partnership or acquiring a small certified firm) is more realistic.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>The diversification answers the two questions of [Slide 25] differently. <strong>Attractiveness:</strong> the same barriers that block ComfortAuto keep the industry profitable for those inside, so the sector is attractive. <strong>Competitive advantage:</strong> ComfortAuto brings real strengths (seat engineering, low-cost inputs, spare capacity), which makes this a <strong>related diversification</strong>, but it lacks certification, reputation and access to contracts. Entering head-on would be slow and risky; a more realistic path is to start in a niche (crew seats, business-jet seats), partner with an incumbent, or acquire a small certified firm.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": null}, {"id": "C14", "category": "short_theory_case", "archetype": "theory-case", "theme": "strategy-levels", "unit": 2, "title": "Nordic MRO Group: corporate strategy, business strategy and levels of decision", "difficulty": 2, "points": 2.0, "time_minutes": 15, "tags": ["theory-case", "corporate-strategy", "business-strategy"], "official": false, "source": "Practice case written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), based on Topic 2_ Value creation.pdf, Slides 8-10 and 16-19", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank C14 - Nordic MRO Group Levels of Strategy.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>At its annual meeting, the Nordic MRO Group took these decisions: (1) to open a maintenance base in Morocco to serve African airlines; (2) to buy the company that overhauls the landing gear it used to outsource; (3) to compete in its engine-overhaul business by guaranteeing the shortest turnaround time in Europe, even at higher prices; (4) to schedule a second shift in the paint hangar during the summer; (5) to change the supplier of cleaning products next month.</p>", "setup_html": null, "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Classify decisions 1–3 as corporate or business strategy, naming the dimension of scope or the source of advantage involved</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Corporate strategy decides <strong>where</strong> the firm competes (its scope: geographical, vertical, product); business strategy decides <strong>how</strong> it competes (cost or differentiation) [Slides 9, 17-19].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>1: corporate strategy, geographical scope.</li><li>2: corporate strategy, vertical scope (backward integration).</li><li>3: business strategy, differentiation advantage (turnaround time, price premium).</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Type [Slides 9, 18]</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1. Base in Morocco</td><td><strong>Corporate strategy</strong></td><td><strong>Geographical scope</strong> (internationalization), part of the question \"which businesses should we be in?\"</td></tr><tr><td>2. Buying the landing-gear overhauler</td><td><strong>Corporate strategy</strong></td><td><strong>Vertical scope</strong> (backward vertical integration)</td></tr><tr><td>3. Shortest turnaround at higher prices</td><td><strong>Business (competitive) strategy</strong></td><td><strong>Differentiation</strong>: a valued, unique feature that earns a price premium [Slides 20, 23]</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Corporate strategy answers \"which businesses should we be in?\" and business strategy \"how should we compete?\" [Slide 17].</p>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Place decisions 4 and 5 in the levels of decision, and state what makes a strategy successful</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Strategic, tactic and operative decisions [Slide 8]; the three pillars of a successful strategy [Slide 10].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>4: tactic decision (one area; it implements the strategy).</li><li>5: operative decision (routine).</li><li>The deck only names the three levels; their characterisation is the usual reading (extension).</li><li>Success: long-term, simple and agreed objectives; deep understanding of the competitive environment; objective appraisal of resources; effective implementation.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>Decisions 1–3 are <strong>strategic</strong>: long-term, affecting the whole firm. Decision 4 (a summer shift in one hangar) is <strong>tactic</strong>: it concerns one area over a season and implements the strategy. Decision 5 (a cleaning supplier next month) is <strong>operative</strong>: routine and short term. The deck names the three levels [Slide 8]; this characterisation of each one is the usual reading (extension).</p>\n<p>A strategy succeeds through <strong>effective implementation</strong> resting on <strong>long-term, simple and agreed objectives</strong>, a <strong>deep understanding of the competitive environment</strong> and an <strong>objective appraisal of resources</strong> [Slide 10]. Nordic's differentiation on turnaround time, for instance, only works if it has the hangar capacity and staff (resources) to deliver it.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": null}, {"id": "C15", "category": "short_theory_case", "archetype": "theory-case", "theme": "cost-advantage", "unit": 2, "title": "LowOrbit: the drivers behind a cost-leadership strategy in satellite buses", "difficulty": 2, "points": 2.0, "time_minutes": 15, "tags": ["theory-case", "cost-leadership", "competitive-advantage"], "official": false, "source": "Practice case written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), based on Topic 2_ Value creation.pdf, Slides 20-22", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank C15 - LowOrbit Satellite Bus Cost Leadership.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>LowOrbit S.A. sells the \"bus\" (platform) of small Earth-observation satellites at 40% below its rivals. It builds one standard design with interchangeable modules instead of custom platforms, and has designed every part to be machined in a single setup. Its line now produces 120 buses a year, and the hours per bus have fallen by half since the first 20 units as the teams repeated the same tasks. It bought a solar-cell supplier located next to the plant, keeps the factory running in three shifts, and its managers are paid for removing waste in every process.</p>", "setup_html": null, "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Identify the drivers of cost advantage present in LowOrbit</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Economies of learning, economies of scale, production techniques, product design, input costs, capacity utilization, residual efficiency [Slides 21-22].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Product design: one standard design, interchangeable modules, design for manufacture.</li><li>Economies of scale: 120 buses a year.</li><li>Economies of learning: hours per bus halved with repetition.</li><li>Input costs: ownership of a low-cost input (solar cells), location.</li><li>Capacity utilization: three shifts.</li><li>Residual efficiency: managers paid to remove waste (motivation, culture).</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Driver [Slides 21-22]</th><th>Evidence at LowOrbit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Product design</strong></td><td>Standardization of designs and components; parts designed for manufacture (single setup)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Economies of scale</strong></td><td>120 buses a year: specialization and division of labour</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Economies of learning</strong></td><td>Hours per bus halved as routines improved</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Input costs</strong></td><td>Ownership of a low-cost input (the solar-cell supplier) and a location advantage</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Capacity utilization</strong></td><td>Three shifts spread the fixed costs over more units</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Residual efficiency</strong></td><td>Managerial efficiency, motivation and culture of eliminating waste</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Explain what a cost advantage is and what risk LowOrbit runs with this strategy</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Compare the two sources of competitive advantage [Slide 20].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Cost advantage: offering a similar product at a lower cost than rivals.</li><li>Risk: standardization limits customization; customers who value unique features may prefer differentiated rivals; rivals may copy the cost drivers.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>A <strong>cost advantage</strong> means offering a <strong>similar product at a lower cost</strong> than competitors, whereas a differentiation advantage obtains a <strong>price premium from a unique product</strong> [Slide 20]. LowOrbit's standard bus is its main cost driver, but it is also its main risk: customers whose missions need a custom platform will go to differentiated rivals, and competitors can copy standardization and scale. To keep the advantage it must keep reducing costs faster than its rivals (learning, scale) while keeping the product \"similar enough\" in performance to theirs.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": null}, {"id": "C16", "category": "short_theory_case", "archetype": "theory-case", "theme": "differentiation", "unit": 2, "title": "Aurum Interiors: tangible and intangible differentiation in business jets", "difficulty": 1, "points": 2.0, "time_minutes": 15, "tags": ["theory-case", "differentiation", "competitive-advantage"], "official": false, "source": "Practice case written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), based on Topic 2_ Value creation.pdf, Slides 20 and 23-24", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank C16 - Aurum Business-Jet Interiors Differentiation.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>Aurum Interiors S.L. designs cabins for private jets and charges about twice what its competitors charge. Its cabins use sound-absorbing panels that make them the quietest in the market and seats 20% lighter than average, and they are delivered with a personalised maintenance plan and a 24-hour technical hotline. Each cabin is signed by a well-known designer, carries a numbered plaque, and owners are invited to an exclusive yearly event. Aurum's account managers accompany each customer from the first sketch to years after delivery.</p>", "setup_html": null, "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Classify the sources of Aurum's differentiation as tangible or intangible</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Tangible differentiation rests on observable characteristics (features, performance, packaging, complementary services); intangible differentiation appeals to image, status, identity and exclusivity [Slide 24].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Tangible: quietness (performance), lighter seats (materials/performance), maintenance plan and hotline (complementary services).</li><li>Intangible: designer's signature, numbered plaque, exclusive event (status, exclusivity).</li><li>The account managers: the whole supplier–customer relationship.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Type [Slide 24]</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Quietest cabins (sound-absorbing panels)</td><td>Tangible</td><td>Performance, observable and measurable</td></tr><tr><td>Seats 20% lighter</td><td>Tangible</td><td>Materials and performance</td></tr><tr><td>Maintenance plan and 24-hour hotline</td><td>Tangible</td><td>Complementary services</td></tr><tr><td>Designer's signature, numbered plaque</td><td>Intangible</td><td>Image, status, desire for exclusivity</td></tr><tr><td>Exclusive yearly event</td><td>Intangible</td><td>Identity and status of belonging to a select group</td></tr><tr><td>Account managers from sketch to after-sales</td><td>Whole relationship</td><td>Total customer responsiveness [Slide 24]</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Explain how differentiation allows Aurum to charge twice the price, and what it must protect</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Porter: \"providing something unique that is valuable to the buyer beyond simply offering a low price\" [Slide 23].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Differentiation creates value for the customer, which earns a price premium [Slide 20].</li><li>The premium must exceed the extra cost of differentiating.</li><li>Aurum must protect what is valuable and hard to copy: reputation, design, relationship.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>Differentiation means <strong>providing something unique that the buyer values</strong> beyond a low price [Slide 23]; because the customer values it, they accept a <strong>price premium</strong> [Slide 20]. Aurum's quiet, light cabins and its exclusive image create that value, so jet owners pay double. The strategy works only while the premium exceeds the extra cost of the panels, designers and services, and while the uniqueness lasts. The tangible features can eventually be copied; the intangible ones (brand, designer, relationship with owners) are harder to imitate and are what Aurum must protect.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": null}, {"id": "C17", "category": "short_theory_case", "archetype": "theory-case", "theme": "diversification", "unit": 2, "title": "Vega Avionics: reasons for and types of diversification", "difficulty": 2, "points": 2.0, "time_minutes": 15, "tags": ["theory-case", "diversification", "corporate-strategy"], "official": false, "source": "Practice case written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), based on Topic 2_ Value creation.pdf, Slides 25-26", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank C17 - Vega Avionics Diversifying into Medical Devices.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>Vega Avionics S.A. makes flight-control electronics. Its sales follow the aircraft-order cycle: in good years profits soar, in crises they collapse. The board studies three moves: (A) launching a line of electronic monitors for hospital intensive-care units, using its expertise in certified, fault-tolerant electronics; (B) opening a sales office in India to sell its current avionics to Indian aircraft makers; (C) buying a chain of hotels near airports because \"it is profitable and has nothing to do with aircraft cycles\".</p>", "setup_html": null, "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Give the reasons why Vega may want to diversify, and classify moves A, B and C</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Reasons: growth, risk, profit [Slide 25]. Types: related vs unrelated; forms: vertical, horizontal, geographical [Slide 26].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Reasons: risk (volatile earnings tied to the aircraft cycle), growth, profit.</li><li>A: horizontal diversification, related (shared technology).</li><li>B: geographical diversification of existing products, related.</li><li>C: horizontal diversification, unrelated.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>Firms diversify for three reasons [Slide 25]: <strong>growth</strong>, <strong>risk</strong> (diversification reduces the volatility of earnings) and <strong>profit</strong>. Vega's main motive is <strong>risk</strong>: its earnings depend on one cycle, and businesses that do not follow it would stabilise them; growth and profit are secondary motives.</p>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Move</th><th>Form [Slide 26]</th><th>Related or unrelated</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A. Hospital monitors</td><td>Horizontal diversification (new industry)</td><td><strong>Related</strong>: it expands Vega's existing line of certified electronics into a new market [Slide 26]</td></tr><tr><td>B. Sales office in India</td><td>Geographical diversification (new market)</td><td><strong>Related</strong>: same products, new market</td></tr><tr><td>C. Airport hotels</td><td>Horizontal diversification (new industry)</td><td><strong>Unrelated</strong>: it adds a new, unrelated product line [Slide 26]</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Evaluate move C with the two questions of a diversification decision</p>", "hint_html": "<p>How attractive is the sector, and can Vega achieve a competitive advantage in it [Slide 25]?</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>The hotel sector may be attractive, but Vega has no source of competitive advantage there.</li><li>Unrelated diversification reduces risk; whether it adds profitability depends on achieving an advantage in the new sector.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>A diversification decision asks <strong>how attractive the sector is</strong> and <strong>whether the firm can achieve a competitive advantage in it</strong> [Slide 25]. Hotels near airports may be profitable, but Vega brings nothing that would give it an advantage over hotel chains: no shared technology, customers or skills. The move would lower the volatility of earnings, but shareholders could achieve that themselves by investing in hotel companies (an argument beyond the slides). Move A, related to Vega's know-how, answers both questions far better.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": null}, {"id": "C18", "category": "short_theory_case", "archetype": "theory-case", "theme": "vertical-integration", "unit": 2, "title": "A launcher maker buys its engine supplier: vertical integration", "difficulty": 2, "points": 2.0, "time_minutes": 15, "tags": ["theory-case", "vertical-integration", "corporate-strategy"], "official": false, "source": "Practice case written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), based on Topic 2_ Value creation.pdf, Slides 19 and 26-27", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank C18 - Launcher Maker Buying its Engine Supplier.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>Helios Launch S.A. builds medium rockets. Its engines come from a single independent supplier that has raised prices twice in two years and delayed deliveries, and the two firms argue about who must pay for each design change. Helios is negotiating to buy the supplier. A director objects: engine technology is changing fast, the supplier also sells engines to other launch companies, and Helios has no experience in managing a factory of that size. Another director proposes, instead, to also buy the satellite-integration company that prepares customers' payloads before launch.</p>", "setup_html": null, "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Identify the type of vertical integration in each proposal and the advantages Helios seeks with the purchase of the engine supplier</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Backward integration moves toward the inputs; forward integration toward the customer [Slide 27].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Buying the engine supplier: backward vertical integration; buying payload integration: forward vertical integration.</li><li>Advantages: control of inputs, reduction of transaction costs (disputes over design changes), independence, better competitive position, reduction of information asymmetries.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>The deck names forward and backward vertical integration [Slide 27]; in the standard reading (extension), backward integration moves toward the inputs and forward integration toward the customer. Buying the engine supplier is therefore <strong>backward vertical integration</strong>, and buying the satellite-integration company would be <strong>forward vertical integration</strong>. Both change the <strong>vertical scope</strong> of the firm, replacing a market interface with an administrative one [Slide 19].</p>\n<p>The advantages Helios seeks with the engine supplier [Slide 27]:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>control of inputs</strong>: no more unexpected price rises or delays;</li><li><strong>reduction of transaction costs</strong>: the disputes over design changes become internal decisions;</li><li><strong>independence</strong> from a single supplier and a <strong>better competitive position</strong>;</li><li><strong>reduction of information asymmetries</strong>: Helios will know the real costs and status of the engines.</li></ul>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Use the disadvantages of vertical integration to evaluate the director's objection</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Increased risk, decreased flexibility, loss of specialization advantages, increased administrative costs [Slide 27].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Decreased flexibility: tied to its own engine technology when it changes fast.</li><li>Loss of specialization advantages: the supplier's scale from selling to other launchers may be lost.</li><li>Increased administrative costs and risk: managing a large new factory without experience.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>The director's points match the <strong>disadvantages</strong> of vertical integration [Slide 27]. When engine technology changes fast, owning the factory means <strong>decreased flexibility</strong>: Helios cannot simply switch to a better engine on the market. If rival launch companies stop buying from a supplier owned by a competitor, the factory loses volume and the <strong>advantages of specialization</strong>. Running a large factory without experience brings <strong>increased administrative costs</strong> and <strong>increased risk</strong>. A balanced conclusion: integration is worth it if engines are critical and the supplier abuses its power, but Helios should first consider alternatives such as a long-term contract or a second supplier.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": null}, {"id": "C19", "category": "short_theory_case", "archetype": "theory-case", "theme": "value-chain", "unit": 2, "title": "SkyEye Drones: mapping activities onto Porter's value chain", "difficulty": 1, "points": 2.0, "time_minutes": 15, "tags": ["theory-case", "value-chain", "porter"], "official": false, "source": "Practice case written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), based on Topic 2_ Value creation.pdf, Slides 28-31", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank C19 - SkyEye Drone Maker Value Chain.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>SkyEye Drones S.L. makes surveying drones. Its activities include: (1) receiving and storing motors and cameras from suppliers; (2) assembling and testing the drones; (3) packing and shipping them to customers; (4) online advertising and demonstrations to surveying firms; (5) repairs and software updates after sale; (6) negotiating contracts with component suppliers; (7) recruiting and training technicians; (8) developing the flight software and testing new materials; (9) accounting, legal advice, quality assurance and general management.</p>", "setup_html": null, "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Classify each activity as a primary or a support activity of the value chain, naming it</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Primary: inbound logistics, operations, outbound logistics, marketing and sales, service [Slide 30]. Support: procurement, human resource management, technological development, infrastructure [Slide 31].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Primary: 1 inbound logistics, 2 operations, 3 outbound logistics, 4 marketing and sales, 5 service.</li><li>Support: 6 procurement, 7 HRM, 8 technological development, 9 infrastructure.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Activity</th><th>Value-chain activity</th><th>Type</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1. Receiving and storing components</td><td>Inbound logistics</td><td>Primary [Slide 30]</td></tr><tr><td>2. Assembling and testing</td><td>Operations</td><td>Primary</td></tr><tr><td>3. Packing and shipping</td><td>Outbound logistics</td><td>Primary</td></tr><tr><td>4. Advertising and demonstrations</td><td>Marketing and sales</td><td>Primary</td></tr><tr><td>5. Repairs and updates after sale</td><td>Service</td><td>Primary</td></tr><tr><td>6. Negotiating with suppliers</td><td>Procurement</td><td>Support [Slide 31]</td></tr><tr><td>7. Recruiting and training</td><td>Human resource management</td><td>Support</td></tr><tr><td>8. Flight software and materials</td><td>Technological development</td><td>Support</td></tr><tr><td>9. Accounting, legal, quality, management</td><td>Infrastructure</td><td>Support</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Explain what the value chain is for and where SkyEye's margin comes from</p>", "hint_html": "<p>The chain arranges resources into activities customers are willing to pay for; the management of the activities and of their linkages gives the competitive advantage [Slides 28-29].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>The activities together produce the margin [Slide 29], read (extension) as what customers pay minus the cost of all activities.</li><li>Competitive advantage comes from managing the activities and their linkages (e.g. software development linked to after-sales updates).</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>The value chain (Porter, 1985) shows that a firm is not a random collection of resources but a set of <strong>activities</strong> that produce goods customers are willing to pay for; together they produce the <strong>margin</strong> shown at the end of the chain [Slide 29], usually read (extension) as the difference between what customers pay and the cost of all the activities. The <strong>management of the activities and of their linkages</strong> is what gives the competitive advantage: for SkyEye, the link between technological development (activity 8) and service (activity 5) allows software updates that keep old drones competitive, a reason for customers to pay more.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": null}, {"id": "C20", "category": "short_theory_case", "archetype": "theory-case", "theme": "economic-financial-structure", "unit": 3, "title": "Rotorline: investing and financing decisions, economic and financial structure", "difficulty": 1, "points": 2.0, "time_minutes": 15, "tags": ["theory-case", "economic-structure", "financial-structure"], "official": false, "source": "Practice case written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), based on Topic 3_Financial Management (I).pdf, Slides 3-6", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank C20 - Rotorline Economic and Financial Structure.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>The finance manager of Rotorline S.A., a helicopter-tour operator, has two items on her desk this week. First, whether to buy a fourth helicopter now or wait until next spring, and whether to replace the old hangar. Second, how to pay for whatever is bought: a bank has offered a five-year loan, the shareholders could put in more money, or the company could keep this year's profit instead of paying dividends. She knows that the helicopter would earn about 9% per year and that the money to buy it would cost, on average, about 7%.</p>", "setup_html": null, "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Distinguish the two types of decisions the finance manager faces and link each one to a side of the balance sheet</p>", "hint_html": "<p>What to invest in and when? How to obtain the money to invest? [Slide 3]. Economic and financial structure [Slides 4-5].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Investing decisions (helicopter, hangar, timing) → economic structure → assets (what is owned).</li><li>Financing decisions (loan, new equity, retained profit) → financial structure → equity and liabilities (what is owed).</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>The finance manager faces the two decisions of the finance area [Slide 3]:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Investing decisions</strong> — <em>what to invest in and when?</em>: the fourth helicopter (now or in spring) and the new hangar. They define the <strong>economic structure</strong>, the use of the financial resources in goods and rights: the <strong>assets</strong>, what the company owns [Slides 4-5].</li><li><strong>Financing decisions</strong> — <em>how to obtain the money?</em>: bank loan, new equity from shareholders, or retained profit. They define the <strong>financial structure</strong>, the sources of financing: <strong>equity and liabilities</strong>, what the company owes [Slides 4-5, 6].</li></ul>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Use the 9% and 7% figures to explain the objective of the company in financial terms</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Assets produce profitability; equity and liabilities have a cost [Slide 5].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Assets generate profitability; financing has a cost.</li><li>Objective: maximize value, which requires profitability &gt; cost.</li><li>9% &gt; 7%: the helicopter creates value.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>The <strong>assets</strong> produce <strong>profitability</strong>, while <strong>equity and liabilities</strong> have a <strong>cost</strong>; the objective of the company is to <strong>maximize value</strong>, which requires that <strong>profitability exceed cost</strong> [Slide 5]. The new helicopter would earn about 9% per year while the funds to buy it cost about 7%, so it creates value: each year it returns 2 points more than it costs. If the cheapest financing available cost more than 9%, the same helicopter would destroy value, which is why the investing and financing decisions must be taken together.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": null}, {"id": "C21", "category": "short_theory_case", "archetype": "theory-case", "theme": "financing-map", "unit": 3, "title": "Nacelle Works: placing seven sources of financing on the map", "difficulty": 2, "points": 2.0, "time_minutes": 15, "tags": ["theory-case", "financial-structure", "sources-of-financing"], "official": false, "source": "Practice case written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), based on Topic 3_Financial Management (I).pdf, Slides 21-35", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank C21 - Nacelle Works Mapping its Sources of Financing.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>Nacelle Works S.A. manufactures engine nacelles. Its CFO lists how the company was financed last year: (1) profits of previous years kept in the company; (2) a capital increase subscribed by a venture fund; (3) bonds maturing in 2032; (4) a seven-year bank loan for a new autoclave; (5) a contract to use a robotic drilling cell for five years with an option to buy it at the end; (6) suppliers of aluminium who are paid at 90 days; (7) customer invoices sold to a specialised company that collects them and bears the risk if the customer does not pay.</p>", "setup_html": null, "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Classify each source by nature, by origin and by term</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Nature: equity or liabilities [Slide 21]; origin: company resources or external resources [Slide 22]; term: long or short [Slides 29-35].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Equity, company resources: (1) self-financing (internal); (2) equity capital (external equity).</li><li>Liabilities, external, long term: (3) bonds, (4) long-term loan, (5) leasing.</li><li>Liabilities, external, short term: (6) accounts payable (trade credit), (7) factoring.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Instrument</th><th>Nature [Slide 21]</th><th>Origin [Slide 22]</th><th>Term</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Self-financing (retained earnings)</td><td>Equity</td><td>Company resources (internal equity)</td><td>Permanent</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Equity capital, rights issue</td><td>Equity</td><td>Company resources (external equity)</td><td>Permanent</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Bonds</td><td>Liabilities</td><td>External</td><td>Long term [Slide 29]</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Long-term bank loan</td><td>Liabilities</td><td>External</td><td>Long term [Slide 31]</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Leasing (use with purchase option)</td><td>Liabilities</td><td>External</td><td>Long term [Slide 33]</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Accounts payable (trade credit)</td><td>Liabilities</td><td>External</td><td>Short term [Slide 35]</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Factoring</td><td>Liabilities</td><td>External</td><td>Short term [Slide 35]</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Explain the difference between a bond and a long-term loan, and between factoring and a trade discount</p>", "hint_html": "<p>A bond is a debt <strong>security</strong>; factoring transfers the risk of insolvency [Slides 29, 31, 35].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Bond: a loan in the form of a security; the issuer owes the holders principal and coupon at maturity.</li><li>Loan: an amount received and paid back at a certain time, with debt interest.</li><li>Factoring: the credit is sold and the factor assumes the insolvency risk.</li><li>Trade discount: the bank advances the amount of a bill before maturity after deducting expenses; the risk stays with the company.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>A <strong>bond</strong> is a <strong>debt security</strong>: a loan in the form of a security, in which the issuer is the borrower and the bondholder the lender; the issuer must repay the principal and pay the coupon at maturity [Slide 29]. With a <strong>long-term loan</strong>, the company receives an amount that must be paid back at a certain time and pays <strong>debt interest</strong> for it [Slide 31].</p>\n<p>With <strong>factoring</strong>, the company <strong>sells</strong> its credit to a factoring company, which collects it and <strong>assumes the risk of insolvency</strong> [Slide 35]. With a <strong>trade discount</strong>, the bank only <strong>advances</strong> the amount of a bill before it falls due, after deducting interest and commission [Slide 35]; if the customer does not pay, the company usually has to return the money (discount with recourse, extension).</p>"}], "interpretation_html": null}, {"id": "C22", "category": "short_theory_case", "archetype": "theory-case", "theme": "self-financing", "unit": 3, "title": "Talon Avionics: pay a dividend or keep the profit?", "difficulty": 2, "points": 2.0, "time_minutes": 15, "tags": ["theory-case", "self-financing", "dividends"], "official": false, "source": "Practice case written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), based on Topic 3_Financial Management (I).pdf, Slides 23-26 and 37", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank C22 - Talon Avionics Dividend or Self-Financing.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>Talon Avionics S.A. earned a profit of 800,000 m.u. in 2025. Its retired founding shareholders, who live on the dividends, ask the board to distribute 600,000 m.u. The CEO wants to distribute only 160,000 m.u. and keep the rest to develop a new radar altimeter next year; otherwise she would have to ask a bank for a loan or issue new shares, which the founders dislike because they would lose part of their control.</p>", "setup_html": null, "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the pay-out and self-financing ratios of each proposal</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Pay-out = dividends / profits; self-financing = retained earnings / profits [Slide 23].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Founders: pay-out 75%, self-financing 25%.</li><li>CEO: pay-out 20%, self-financing 80%.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Proposal</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Dividends</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Retained</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Pay-out</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Self-financing</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Founders</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">600,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">200,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$600{,}000/800{,}000 = 75\\%$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$200{,}000/800{,}000 = 25\\%$</td></tr><tr><td>CEO</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">160,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">640,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$160{,}000/800{,}000 = 20\\%$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$640{,}000/800{,}000 = 80\\%$</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Explain the advantages and drawbacks of self-financing compared with a bank loan or a rights issue, and give your recommendation</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Think about repayment, explicit vs implicit cost, control, and the fact that paying dividends is not compulsory [Slides 23-26, 37].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Self-financing: internal equity generated by the activity; no repayment, no explicit cost, no new owners; but it has an opportunity cost for shareholders and depends on profits.</li><li>Loan: explicit cost (interest), must be repaid, increases leverage and risk.</li><li>Rights issue: no repayment, but new shareholders dilute control.</li><li>Dividends are not compulsory; a balanced pay-out may be agreed.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p><strong>Self-financing</strong> (retained earnings) is internal equity generated by the company's normal activity [Slide 23]. It does not have to be repaid, carries no <strong>explicit</strong> cost and brings in no new owners, so the founders keep their control; its cost is <strong>implicit</strong>: the shareholders give up dividends today (an opportunity cost, [Slide 37]).</p>\n<p>The alternatives have other drawbacks: a <strong>bank loan</strong> carries an explicit cost (interest), must be repaid, and raises leverage and risk [Slides 31, 36]; a <strong>rights issue</strong> need not be repaid but brings in new shareholders who share future dividends [Slides 25-26] and voting power (extension).</p>\n<p><strong>Recommendation:</strong> since paying dividends is not compulsory [Slide 26] and the founders want to keep control, the CEO's high self-financing is the way to fund the project that needs no repayment, carries no explicit cost and keeps control in their hands. A compromise (for example a 30–40% pay-out) can still meet part of the founders' income needs if the project does not require all 640,000 m.u. (a suggestion beyond the course).</p>"}], "interpretation_html": null}, {"id": "C23", "category": "short_theory_case", "archetype": "theory-case", "theme": "long-short-financing", "unit": 3, "title": "A flight school's simulator: leasing, a long-term loan or a credit line?", "difficulty": 2, "points": 2.0, "time_minutes": 15, "tags": ["theory-case", "leasing", "bank-financing"], "official": false, "source": "Practice case written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), based on Topic 3_Financial Management (I).pdf, Slides 31, 33 and 35", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank C23 - Flight School Simulator Leasing Loan or Credit Line.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>Cumulus Flight School S.L. needs a full-flight simulator that will be used for about eight years. A bank offers three options: (A) a long-term loan for the full price, repaid over eight years with interest; (B) a leasing contract: the school uses the simulator in exchange for monthly payments for eight years and can buy it at the end for a small amount; (C) a credit line with a limit equal to the price, renewable every year, on which interest is paid only on the amount drawn. Separately, the school expects short cash gaps every winter, when fewer students enrol.</p>", "setup_html": null, "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Describe each of the three instruments</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Long-term loans and bank credits [Slide 31]; leasing [Slide 33]; short-term bank financing [Slide 35].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Loan: an amount received and repaid at a certain time, paying debt interest.</li><li>Leasing: use of a fixed asset for periodic payments; the lessee has the right to buy at the end; finance or operating leasing.</li><li>Credit line: the bank makes a maximum available; interest only on the amount borrowed; higher rates.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<ul><li><strong>(A) Long-term loan:</strong> the school receives an amount that must be paid back at a certain time and pays for the service through the <strong>debt interest</strong> [Slide 31].</li><li><strong>(B) Leasing:</strong> the school obtains the <strong>use of a fixed asset</strong> in exchange for contractual, periodic payments; as lessee, it has the <strong>right to buy</strong> the asset at the end of the period. It can be <strong>finance</strong> or <strong>operating</strong> leasing [Slide 33].</li><li><strong>(C) Bank credit (credit line):</strong> the bank makes a <strong>maximum amount</strong> available; the school borrows what it needs up to that limit and pays interest <strong>only on the amount borrowed</strong>; interest rates are higher. Renewed yearly, it is short-term bank financing [Slides 31, 35].</li></ul>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Recommend how to finance the simulator and the winter cash gaps</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Match the term of the financing to the life of what it finances.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Simulator (8-year asset): long-term financing, loan or leasing; leasing also offers flexibility and the purchase option.</li><li>Winter gaps (short, recurring): the credit line, since interest is paid only on the amount drawn.</li><li>Financing a long-lived asset with a yearly-renewable credit line is risky and expensive.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>The simulator will be used for eight years, so it should be financed with <strong>long-term</strong> resources: either the <strong>loan</strong> (A) or the <strong>leasing</strong> (B), whose eight-year payments match its useful life. Leasing is attractive for a flight school because it obtains the use of the asset [Slide 33] without a large initial outlay (reasoning applied to the case, beyond the slides) and keeps the <strong>option to buy</strong> it at the end. Financing it with the <strong>credit line</strong> (C) would be a mistake: the line must be renewed every year, so the bank could refuse (reasoning applied to the case, beyond the slides), and its interest rates are higher [Slide 31].</p>\n<p>The <strong>winter cash gaps</strong>, instead, are short and recurring, so the <strong>credit line</strong> is the right tool: the school draws only what it needs for a few weeks and pays interest <strong>only on the amount borrowed</strong> [Slide 31].</p>"}], "interpretation_html": null}, {"id": "C24", "category": "short_theory_case", "archetype": "theory-case", "theme": "accrual-classification", "unit": 3, "title": "Radome Composites: revenues vs collections and current vs non-current items", "difficulty": 2, "points": 2.0, "time_minutes": 15, "tags": ["theory-case", "accrual-vs-cash", "balance-sheet"], "official": false, "source": "Practice case written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), based on Topic 3_Financial Management (I).pdf, Slides 7-8 and 12-17", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank C24 - Radome Composites Accruals and Classification.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>The owner of Radome Composites S.L., which makes radar domes for aircraft noses, says: \"In December we delivered 20 domes to an airline that will pay in February, so they are not sales of this year. We received in November a 30,000 m.u. advance for domes to be delivered next March, so that is income of this year. The electricity of December will be paid in January, so it is a cost of next year. And the five-year bank loan is all a long-term debt, even the instalment due next June.\"</p>", "setup_html": null, "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.5, "prompt_html": "<p>Correct the owner's four statements using the accrual principle and the classification of the balance sheet</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Revenues are recorded when the sale is made, expenses when goods and services are received, whether paid or not [Slide 8]. Current items are settled within one year [Slides 14-16].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>December deliveries: revenue of this year; the amount owed is an account receivable (current asset).</li><li>November advance: not revenue yet; a liability (unearned revenue) until delivery in March.</li><li>December electricity: expense of this year; unpaid amount is a current liability.</li><li>Loan: the instalment due next June is a current liability; the rest is long-term.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Owner's statement</th><th>Correction</th><th>Basis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>December deliveries are not sales of this year</td><td><strong>They are revenue of this year</strong>: the sale was made in December. The amount owed is an <strong>account receivable</strong>, a current asset</td><td>Revenues are recorded when the sale is made, paid or not [Slide 8]; current assets [Slide 14]</td></tr><tr><td>The November advance is income of this year</td><td><strong>It is not revenue yet</strong>: no domes have been delivered. It is a <strong>liability</strong> (unearned revenue) until March</td><td>Liabilities include unearned revenues [Slide 13]</td></tr><tr><td>December electricity is a cost of next year</td><td><strong>It is an expense of this year</strong>: the service was received in December. The unpaid amount is a <strong>current liability</strong></td><td>Expenses are recorded when the services are received [Slide 8]</td></tr><tr><td>The whole loan is long-term</td><td><strong>The instalment due next June is a current liability</strong>; only the instalments due after one year are long-term</td><td>Current liabilities are paid within the coming year [Slide 16]</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 0.5, "prompt_html": "<p>Explain why the income statement and the balance sheet need these rules</p>", "hint_html": "<p>What does each statement report, and over what time [Slides 7, 12]?</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>The income statement reports performance over a period: it must match the revenues and expenses of that period, not cash movements.</li><li>The balance sheet reports the financial position at a point in time: classifying by maturity shows liquidity.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>The <strong>income statement</strong> reports the <strong>performance</strong> of the business over a specific period [Slide 7]: it must contain the revenues earned and the expenses incurred in that period, whatever happens to the cash, otherwise the profit of a year would depend on when customers happen to pay. The <strong>balance sheet</strong> reports the <strong>financial position at a particular point in time</strong> [Slide 12]: splitting assets and liabilities into current and non-current shows whether the company can meet the debts due within the year with the resources that become cash within the year.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": null}, {"id": "P01", "category": "extra_practice_problem", "archetype": "narrative-case", "theme": "narrative-case", "unit": 3, "title": "T3 Exercise 1: revenues vs collections, expenses vs payments, depreciation", "difficulty": 1, "points": 4.0, "time_minutes": 25, "tags": ["official-exercise", "accrual-vs-cash", "depreciation"], "official": true, "source": "T3_Financial Statements_EXERCISES.pdf, Exercise 1 (official exercise, solved in Problem - T3 Exercise 1 - Accrual vs Cash Basis and Linear Depreciation)", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank P01 - T3 Exercise 1 Accrual vs Cash and Depreciation.md", "page": "subjects/business-management/problemas/topic-3-financial-statements.html#exercise-1", "statement_html": "<p>A company started activity on 1 January of the present year. At 31 March it has the following goods, acquired on 31 December of the previous year:</p>\n<ul><li>factory building 1,000 m.u., duration 25 years;</li><li>office building 1,500 m.u., duration 50 years;</li><li>machinery 300 m.u., duration 10 years;</li><li>transport equipment 100 m.u., duration 5 years.</li></ul>\n<p>Information for the first quarter:</p>\n<ul><li>personnel salaries of the quarter 450 m.u., of which 75 m.u. are pending to be paid after 31/3;</li><li>materials purchased and used in production 700 m.u., of which 125 m.u. are pending to be paid after 31/3;</li><li>energy consumption estimated at 230 m.u., nothing paid to date;</li><li>sales invoiced to customers 1,930 m.u., of which 315 m.u. had not been collected at 31/3; the rest was collected in due time. In manufacturing all the units sold, all the purchased materials were consumed.</li></ul>\n<p>Ignore corporation taxes.</p>\n<p>a) At 31/3, find the income, expenses, collections (cash inflows), payments (cash outflows), investments and depreciation of the quarter (linear depreciation along the lifetime of the goods).</p>\n<p>b) How does the answer change if only half of the purchased materials had been consumed in manufacturing the units sold?</p>", "setup_html": "<p><strong>Period:</strong> first quarter (1/01 to 31/03), $\\frac{3}{12} = \\frac{1}{4}$ of a year. The fixed assets were bought on 31/12 of the previous year, so they are used during the <strong>whole</strong> quarter.</p>\n<p>The exercise trains the distinction of [Slide 8]: a <strong>revenue</strong> is recorded when the sale is made, whether or not it is collected; an <strong>expense</strong> when the good or service is received or consumed, whether or not it is paid. Collections and payments are the cash side of the same operations.</p>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Revenue / expense of Q1</th><th>Collection / payment in Q1</th><th>Pending at 31/3</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sales</td><td>Revenue 1,930</td><td>Collection $1{,}930 - 315 = 1{,}615$</td><td>Receivable 315</td></tr><tr><td>Salaries</td><td>Expense 450</td><td>Payment $450 - 75 = 375$</td><td>Salaries payable 75</td></tr><tr><td>Materials (all consumed)</td><td>Expense 700</td><td>Payment $700 - 125 = 575$</td><td>Supplier payable 125</td></tr><tr><td>Energy</td><td>Expense 230</td><td>Payment 0</td><td>Energy payable 230</td></tr><tr><td>Fixed assets</td><td>Depreciation 30</td><td>0</td><td>Net value $2{,}900 - 30$</td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the depreciation of the quarter of each fixed asset</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Linear depreciation without residual value: cost / years of life, times 3/12 for a quarter.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Annual depreciation 120 m.u.; quarter 30 m.u.</li><li>Factory 10.0; office 7.5; machinery 7.5; transport 5.0 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Depreciation of the quarter</h4>\n<p>For each asset, with no residual value given:</p>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$D_{\\text{quarter}} = \\frac{\\text{Cost}}{\\text{Years of life}} \\times \\frac{3}{12}$$</div>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Asset</th><th>Annual depreciation</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Quarter</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Factory building</td><td>$1{,}000 / 25 = 40$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$40 / 4 = 10.0$</td></tr><tr><td>Office building</td><td>$1{,}500 / 50 = 30$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$30 / 4 = 7.5$</td></tr><tr><td>Machinery</td><td>$300 / 10 = 30$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$30 / 4 = 7.5$</td></tr><tr><td>Transport equipment</td><td>$100 / 5 = 20$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$20 / 4 = 5.0$</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td><strong>120</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>30.0</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 2.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Find the income, expenses, collections, payments and investments of the quarter</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Income and expenses follow the accrual principle; collections and payments only count what moved cash. The assets were bought before the quarter began.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Income 1,930; expenses 1,410 m.u.; profit 520 m.u.</li><li>Collections 1,615; payments 950 m.u.; investments of the quarter 0</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Part (a)</h4>\n<ul><li><strong>Income (revenues):</strong> the sales invoiced, <strong>1,930 m.u.</strong></li><li><strong>Expenses:</strong> $450 + 700 + 230 + 30 = $ <strong>1,410 m.u.</strong> (so the profit of the quarter is $1{,}930 - 1{,}410 = 520$ m.u.).</li><li><strong>Collections:</strong> $1{,}930 - 315 = $ <strong>1,615 m.u.</strong></li><li><strong>Payments:</strong> $(450 - 75) + (700 - 125) + 0 = 375 + 575 = $ <strong>950 m.u.</strong> Depreciation is not a payment.</li><li><strong>Investments of the quarter:</strong> <strong>0 m.u.</strong> The fixed assets (total $1{,}000 + 1{,}500 + 300 + 100 = 2{,}900$ m.u.) were acquired on 31/12 of the previous year, before the quarter began. If the question is read as \"the investment the company works with\", the answer is that 2,900 m.u.; state the reading you use in the exam.</li><li><strong>Depreciation:</strong> <strong>30 m.u.</strong></li></ul>"}, {"label": "c", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>How does the answer change if only half of the materials had been consumed?</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Only the material consumed is an expense; the rest is inventory. Did the payments change?</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Expenses 1,060; profit 870 m.u.; inventory 350 m.u.</li><li>Collections and payments unchanged</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 3. Part (b): only half of the materials consumed</h4>\n<p>Only the material <strong>consumed</strong> is an expense [Slide 8, Slide 10]: $700 / 2 = 350$ m.u. The other 350 m.u. stay in the warehouse as <strong>inventory</strong>, a current asset [Slide 14].</p>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Magnitude</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">(a)</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">(b)</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Change</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Income</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,930</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,930</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0</td></tr><tr><td>Expenses</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,410</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$450 + 350 + 230 + 30 = 1{,}060$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−350</td></tr><tr><td>Profit</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">520</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">870</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+350</td></tr><tr><td>Collections</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,615</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,615</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0</td></tr><tr><td>Payments</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">950</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">950</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0</td></tr><tr><td>Inventory at 31/3</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">350</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+350</td></tr><tr><td>Depreciation</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">30</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">30</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Payments do not change: the company <strong>paid</strong> for what it <strong>bought</strong> (575 of the 700), regardless of how much it <strong>used</strong>.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": "<ul><li><strong>Profit is not cash:</strong> in (a) the profit is 520 while the net cash generated is $1{,}615 - 950 = 665$. The difference, $665 - 520 = 145$, is explained item by item: $+30$ (depreciation, an expense without payment) $+75 + 125 + 230$ (expenses not yet paid) $- 315$ (revenue not yet collected) $= 145$.</li><li><strong>Part (b)</strong> raises the profit by 350 without any extra cash: unconsumed material is an asset, not an expense.</li><li><strong>Check of depreciation:</strong> the annual total 120 times $\\frac{1}{4}$ gives 30.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Official solution, <code>T3_Financial Statements_SOLUTIONS.pdf</code>, pp. 1-2</div><p>Part (a) coincides with the key: income 1,930; collections 1,615; payments 950; expenses 1,410; depreciation 30; investments 0. In part (b) the key also states that revenue, collections and payments do not change and that the unused material appears as inventory.</p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout-warning\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Erratum in the official solution (part b)</div><p>The key gives total expenses of 1,124 for part (b), but its own items add to $450 + 350 + 230 + 30 = 1{,}060$. That is the value obtained above, and the only one consistent with a profit that rises by exactly the 350 of material that was not consumed.</p></div>"}, {"id": "P02", "category": "extra_practice_problem", "archetype": "narrative-case", "theme": "narrative-case", "unit": 3, "title": "T3 Exercise 2: JUGA S.A. opening balance sheet with a patent contributed in kind", "difficulty": 1, "points": 4.0, "time_minutes": 20, "tags": ["official-exercise", "balance-sheet", "contributions-in-kind"], "official": true, "source": "T3_Financial Statements_EXERCISES.pdf, Exercise 2 (official exercise, solved in Problem - T3 Exercise 2 - JUGA SA Opening Balance Sheet)", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank P02 - T3 Exercise 2 JUGA Opening Balance Sheet.md", "page": "subjects/business-management/problemas/topic-3-financial-statements.html#exercise-2", "statement_html": "<p>The JUGA S.A. firm is started with a shareholders' contribution of 60,000 €. Of those, 30,000 € are paid in and deposited in a bank account, and the remaining 30,000 € correspond to the transfer of a patent. With the cash of the bank account, raw materials are purchased for 10,500 € and machinery for 15,000 €. Detail the balance sheet at the start of operations.</p>", "setup_html": "<ul><li>The capital contributed is <strong>60,000 €</strong>: 30,000 € in money and 30,000 € in kind (a patent).</li><li>The purchases are paid <strong>with the bank account</strong> (no debt is created).</li><li>It is the <strong>start</strong> of operations: nothing has been produced or sold yet, so there are no revenues or expenses and no income statement.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Assets</th><th>Liabilities</th><th>Equity</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Money contributed</td><td>Bank +30,000</td><td></td><td>Capital +30,000</td></tr><tr><td>Patent contributed</td><td>Patent +30,000</td><td></td><td>Capital +30,000</td></tr><tr><td>Raw materials bought</td><td>Raw materials +10,500; bank −10,500</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Machinery bought</td><td>Machinery +15,000; bank −15,000</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Each row keeps Assets = Liabilities + Equity [Slide 12]: the contributions increase both sides; the purchases only change the composition of the assets.</p>", "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.5, "prompt_html": "<p>Find the bank balance and classify every item</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Only the cash part of the contribution reaches the bank; the purchases are paid from it. The patent is a non-current asset.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Bank 4,500 €</li><li>Patent and machinery non-current; raw materials and bank current; capital 60,000 € in equity</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Bank balance</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Bank} = 30{,}000 - 10{,}500 - 15{,}000 = 4{,}500 \\text{ €}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Classification</h4>\n<ul><li><strong>Patent:</strong> non-current asset [Slide 15].</li><li><strong>Machinery:</strong> non-current asset (equipment) [Slide 15].</li><li><strong>Raw materials:</strong> inventory, a current asset [Slide 14]; not consumed yet, so not an expense.</li><li><strong>Bank:</strong> cash, a current asset [Slide 14].</li><li><strong>Share capital:</strong> contributed capital [Slide 17].</li></ul>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 2.5, "prompt_html": "<p>Prepare the balance sheet at the start of operations</p>", "hint_html": "<p>The contribution in kind (the patent) is capital exactly like the cash.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Total 60,000 € on both sides</li><li>Non-current assets 45,000 €; current assets 15,000 €</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 3. Balance sheet at the start of operations</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Assets</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">€</th><th>Liabilities and equity</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">€</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Patent</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">30,000</td><td>Share capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">60,000</td></tr><tr><td>Machinery</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">15,000</td><td><strong>Equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>60,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Non-current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>45,000</strong></td><td><strong>Liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>0</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Raw materials</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">10,500</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td></tr><tr><td>Bank</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">4,500</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>15,000</strong></td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>60,000</strong></td><td><strong>Total liabilities and equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>60,000</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}], "interpretation_html": "<ul><li><strong>Balance:</strong> $45{,}000 + 15{,}000 = 60{,}000 = 60{,}000 + 0$.</li><li><strong>Typical mistake:</strong> keeping the bank at 30,000 € while also adding the machinery and the raw materials gives assets of 85,000 € against 60,000 € of equity. Spending cash on an asset never changes the total.</li><li><strong>Financial structure</strong> [Slide 36]: the company is financed 100% with equity ($L/E = 0$).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Official solution, <code>T3_Financial Statements_SOLUTIONS.pdf</code>, p. 3</div><p>The balance sheet coincides with the key in every line: bank 4,500; patent 30,000; machinery 15,000; raw materials 10,500; share capital 60,000; total 60,000. The bank movements (+30,000, −15,000, −10,500) are the same.</p></div>"}, {"id": "P03", "category": "extra_practice_problem", "archetype": "unordered-items", "theme": "unordered-items", "unit": 3, "title": "T3 Exercise 3: Libby's Company balance sheet from a list of accounts", "difficulty": 1, "points": 4.0, "time_minutes": 20, "tags": ["official-exercise", "balance-sheet", "classification"], "official": true, "source": "T3_Financial Statements_EXERCISES.pdf, Exercise 3 (official exercise, solved in Problem - T3 Exercise 3 - Libbys Company Balance Sheet Classification)", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank P03 - T3 Exercise 3 Libbys Balance Sheet Classification.md", "page": "subjects/business-management/problemas/topic-3-financial-statements.html#exercise-3", "statement_html": "<p>Prepare the balance sheet of Libby's Company using the following figures (in euros):</p>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Account</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">€</th><th>Account</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">€</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Short-term (current) loan debts</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">15,500</td><td>Accounts payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,500</td></tr><tr><td>Long-term loan debts</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3,000</td><td>Equipment</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">13,000</td></tr><tr><td>Money on its bank account</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3,000</td><td>Raw materials (purchased this year but not used yet)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">12,500</td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">500</td><td>Furniture</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,500</td></tr><tr><td>Paid-in capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">12,000</td><td>Retained earnings from the year</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2,000</td></tr><tr><td>Accounts receivable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3,500</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "setup_html": "<p>Every account is already a balance-sheet item: there are no revenues or expenses. The task is pure <strong>classification</strong> into the five blocks of [Slide 18] and a check that the totals agree.</p>", "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.5, "prompt_html": "<p>Classify every account into its block of the balance sheet</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Ask of each account whether it is an asset, a liability or equity, and whether it is settled within one year.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Raw materials not used yet are inventory (current asset)</li><li>Retained earnings are equity, not a liability</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Account</th><th>Block</th><th>Reason</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Equipment</td><td>Non-current asset</td><td>Long-lived resource [Slide 15]</td></tr><tr><td>Furniture</td><td>Non-current asset</td><td>Long-lived resource [Slide 15]</td></tr><tr><td>Raw materials (not used yet)</td><td>Current asset (inventory)</td><td>Will be used within the year [Slide 14]</td></tr><tr><td>Accounts receivable</td><td>Current asset</td><td>Turned into cash within the year [Slide 14]</td></tr><tr><td>Bank account</td><td>Current asset (cash)</td><td>[Slide 14]</td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td>Current asset (cash)</td><td>[Slide 14]</td></tr><tr><td>Paid-in capital</td><td>Equity: contributed capital</td><td>[Slide 17]</td></tr><tr><td>Retained earnings from the year</td><td>Equity: retained earnings</td><td>Earnings not paid as dividends [Slide 13]</td></tr><tr><td>Long-term loan debts</td><td>Long-term liability</td><td>Not paid within the year [Slide 16]</td></tr><tr><td>Short-term loan debts</td><td>Current liability</td><td>Paid within the year [Slide 16]</td></tr><tr><td>Accounts payable</td><td>Current liability</td><td>Owed to suppliers, short term [Slide 13, Slide 16]</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the totals of each block</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Add the accounts of each block separately before building the statement.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Non-current assets 14,500; current assets 19,500 €</li><li>Equity 14,000; long-term liabilities 3,000; current liabilities 17,000 €</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Block totals</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Non-current assets} = 13{,}000 + 1{,}500 = 14{,}500$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Current assets} = 12{,}500 + 3{,}500 + 3{,}000 + 500 = 19{,}500$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Equity} = 12{,}000 + 2{,}000 = 14{,}000$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Long-term liabilities} = 3{,}000, \\qquad \\text{Current liabilities} = 15{,}500 + 1{,}500 = 17{,}000$$</div>"}, {"label": "c", "points": 1.5, "prompt_html": "<p>Present the balance sheet</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Order: non-current before current assets; equity, long-term, then current liabilities.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Total 34,000 € on both sides</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Balance sheet</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Assets</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">€</th><th>Liabilities and equity</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">€</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Equipment</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">13,000</td><td>Paid-in capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">12,000</td></tr><tr><td>Furniture</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,500</td><td>Retained earnings</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Non-current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>14,500</strong></td><td><strong>Equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>14,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Raw materials</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">12,500</td><td>Long-term loan debts</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3,000</td></tr><tr><td>Accounts receivable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3,500</td><td><strong>Long-term liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>3,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Bank account</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3,000</td><td>Short-term loan debts</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">15,500</td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">500</td><td>Accounts payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,500</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>19,500</strong></td><td><strong>Current liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>17,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>34,000</strong></td><td><strong>Total liabilities and equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>34,000</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}], "interpretation_html": "<ul><li><strong>Balance:</strong> $14{,}500 + 19{,}500 = 34{,}000 = 14{,}000 + 3{,}000 + 17{,}000$.</li><li><strong>Financial structure</strong> [Slide 36]: liabilities $L = 3{,}000 + 17{,}000 = 20{,}000$, equity $E = 14{,}000$, $E + L = 34{,}000$:</li></ul>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\%E = \\frac{14{,}000}{34{,}000} = 41.2\\%, \\qquad \\%L = \\frac{20{,}000}{34{,}000} = 58.8\\%, \\qquad \\frac{L}{E} = \\frac{20{,}000}{14{,}000} = 1.43$$</div>\n<p>The company has more debt than equity, and most of it (17,000 of 20,000) is due within the year: by [Slide 36], a high leverage indicates higher risk to shareholders.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Typical mistakes:</strong> retained earnings are equity, not a liability; accounts receivable (owed <strong>to</strong> the company) are an asset, accounts payable (owed <strong>by</strong> the company) a liability.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Official solution, <code>T3_Financial Statements_SOLUTIONS.pdf</code>, p. 4</div><p>The balance sheet coincides with the key in every block: current assets 19,500; non-current assets 14,500; current liabilities 17,000; non-current liabilities 3,000; owners' equity 14,000; total 34,000. The key asks only for the balance sheet, so the leverage figures in Phase 4 are an addition based on Slide 36.</p></div>"}, {"id": "P04", "category": "extra_practice_problem", "archetype": "unordered-items", "theme": "unordered-items", "unit": 3, "title": "T3 Exercise 4: Hawkins Partners income statement with inventory variation", "difficulty": 1, "points": 4.0, "time_minutes": 20, "tags": ["official-exercise", "income-statement", "inventory"], "official": true, "source": "T3_Financial Statements_EXERCISES.pdf, Exercise 4 (official exercise, solved in Problem - T3 Exercise 4 - Hawkins Partners Income Statement and Inventories)", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank P04 - T3 Exercise 4 Hawkins Partners Income Statement.md", "page": "subjects/business-management/problemas/topic-3-financial-statements.html#exercise-4", "statement_html": "<p>Prepare the income statement for Hawkins Partners using the following figures (in euros), knowing that the applicable corporate tax rate is 30%:</p>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Item</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">€</th><th>#</th><th>Item</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">€</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Initial inventory</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">600</td><td>6</td><td>Interests from the long-term debt</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">5,500</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Purchase of goods (sold 100%)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">84,000</td><td>7</td><td>Wages and salaries</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">12,700</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Rental costs</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,000</td><td>8</td><td>Water, gas and electricity</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,200</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Property sold to another company (income)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">10,500</td><td>9</td><td>Sales</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">130,800</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Interests received by the company</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">15,200</td><td>10</td><td>Inventory at the end of the period</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">500</td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "setup_html": "<p>The statement mixes the three kinds of income-statement lines of [Slide 9]: items of the primary operation (sales, goods, wages, rent, utilities), <strong>non-operating items</strong> (interests, the sale of property) [Slide 11], and the tax. It also gives the initial and final <strong>inventory</strong>, which adjust the cost of goods sold.</p>", "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Classify every item into its line of the income statement</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Separate the cost of goods sold, the operating expenses and the non-operating items (interest, sale of property).</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Interest received, interest paid and the property sale are non-operating items</li><li>Inventories enter only through the cost of goods sold</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Line of the income statement</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>9. Sales</td><td>Sales revenue</td></tr><tr><td>1, 2, 10. Inventories and purchases</td><td>Cost of goods sold</td></tr><tr><td>3, 7, 8. Rent, wages, utilities</td><td>Operating expenses [Slide 10]</td></tr><tr><td>4. Property sold (income)</td><td>Non-operating item: gain on the sale of property [Slide 11]</td></tr><tr><td>5. Interests received</td><td>Non-operating item: interest revenue [Slide 8, Slide 11]</td></tr><tr><td>6. Interests on long-term debt</td><td>Non-operating item: interest expense [Slide 11]</td></tr><tr><td>Tax 30%</td><td>Income taxes [Slide 11]</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the cost of goods sold</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Opening inventory + purchases − closing inventory.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Cost of goods sold 84,100 €</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Cost of goods sold</h4>\n<p>The goods sold are those available (initial inventory + purchases) minus those still in stock at the end:</p>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{COGS} = 600 + 84{,}000 - 500 = 84{,}100 \\text{ €}$$</div>\n<p>The inventory fell by $600 - 500 = 100$ €: those 100 € of goods were also sold, so they add to the purchases.</p>"}, {"label": "c", "points": 2.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Prepare the income statement</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Gross profit → income from operations → income before taxes → net income, with a 30% tax.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Gross profit 46,700 €; income before taxes 52,000 €</li><li>Net income 36,400 €</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Income statement</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Computation</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">€</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sales revenue</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">130,800</td></tr><tr><td>− Cost of goods sold</td><td>Step 1</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">84,100</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Gross profit</strong></td><td>$130{,}800 - 84{,}100$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>46,700</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Wages and salaries</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">12,700</td></tr><tr><td>− Rental costs</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Water, gas and electricity</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,200</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income from operations</strong></td><td>$46{,}700 - 14{,}900$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>31,800</strong></td></tr><tr><td>+ Gain on property sold</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">10,500</td></tr><tr><td>+ Interests received</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">15,200</td></tr><tr><td>− Interests on long-term debt</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">5,500</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income before taxes</strong></td><td>$31{,}800 + 10{,}500 + 15{,}200 - 5{,}500$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>52,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Income taxes (30%)</td><td>$0.30 \\times 52{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">15,600</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Net income</strong></td><td>$52{,}000 - 15{,}600$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>36,400</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}], "interpretation_html": "<ul><li><strong>Non-operating items add 20,200 €</strong> ($10{,}500 + 15{,}200 - 5{,}500$): almost 39% of the income before taxes does not come from the primary operation, which is why the slides separate them [Slide 11].</li><li><strong>The tax base</strong> is the income before taxes (52,000), not the income from operations [Slide 11].</li><li><strong>Reading of item 4:</strong> the statement labels the 10,500 € as income from the property sold; no book value of the property is given, so the whole amount is taken as the gain on the sale.</li><li><strong>Check:</strong> $130{,}800 + 10{,}500 + 15{,}200 = 156{,}500$ of revenues and gains; $84{,}100 + 14{,}900 + 5{,}500 = 104{,}500$ of expenses; $156{,}500 - 104{,}500 = 52{,}000$.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Official solution, <code>T3_Financial Statements_SOLUTIONS.pdf</code>, p. 5</div><p>Cost of goods sold 84,100 (purchases 84,000 plus the inventory variation of 100), operating expenses 14,900, interests received 15,200, interests paid 5,500, income before taxes 52,000, tax 15,600 and net income 36,400 coincide with the key.</p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout-warning\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Discrepancy with the official solution (EBIT)</div><p>The key includes the 10,500 € of the property sold (labelled \"Income from rentals\") in the income from operations, so its EBIT is 42,300 €. Here the amount is shown below EBIT, as a non-operating gain, following the definition of Slide 11 (gains and losses on the sale of equipment and investments). Both layouts give the same income before taxes (52,000 €) and the same net income (36,400 €); only the EBIT subtotal changes: 31,800 € here and 42,300 € in the key. For an exam marked against this key, expect EBIT = 42,300 €.</p></div>"}, {"id": "P05", "category": "extra_practice_problem", "archetype": "narrative-case", "theme": "narrative-case", "unit": 3, "title": "T3 Exercise 5: a large company's income statement with discounts, taxes and dividends", "difficulty": 2, "points": 4.0, "time_minutes": 30, "tags": ["official-exercise", "income-statement", "dividends"], "official": true, "source": "T3_Financial Statements_EXERCISES.pdf, Exercise 5 (official exercise, solved in Problem - T3 Exercise 5 - Corporate Income Statement, Taxes and Dividends)", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank P05 - T3 Exercise 5 Corporate Income Statement and Dividends.md", "page": "subjects/business-management/problemas/topic-3-financial-statements.html#exercise-5", "statement_html": "<p>Prepare the income statement of a company with the following information for 2017:</p>\n<ul><li>Gross sales during the year reached 2,002 million €. The company's trade policy gives a 3% discount to its Type A customers, those whose annual purchases from the company exceed 120 million €. Type A customers account for 40% of sales. Assume total sales = gross sales − discount.</li><li>Purchases of raw materials amounted to 962 million €.</li><li>The workforce consists of 7,800 employees. The average salary is 30,000 € per year, and the average spending on Social Security is 30% of the salary.</li><li>Payments for other operating expenses amounted to 457 million €.</li><li>In recent years the company has invested a total of 1,200 million € in fixed assets with an average lifespan of 10 years (linear depreciation, no residual value).</li><li>The company obtained an international loan of 225 million € in 2015, at 6% interest along the whole year; the whole principal will be repaid in 2020.</li><li>The corporate tax rate is 30%. The company has agreed to a dividend of 50%.</li><li>Assume no change in stocks of raw materials or finished products during the year.</li></ul>", "setup_html": "<p>All amounts are expressed in <strong>millions of euros (M€)</strong>. Salaries are given per employee in €, so they must be converted: $1 \\text{ M€} = 10^6$ €.</p>\n<p><strong>Hypotheses:</strong></p>\n<ol><li>\"No change in stocks\" means initial inventory = final inventory, so $\\text{COGS} = \\text{Purchases}$.</li><li>The 120 M€ threshold only defines who is a Type A customer; the statement already says these customers make 40% of the sales, so the discount applies to that 40%.</li><li>The loan principal is not an expense: only its interest enters the income statement [Slide 11].</li><li>The dividend of 50% is applied to the <strong>net income</strong> [Slide 23].</li></ol>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Line</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Gross sales − discount</td><td>Sales revenue (net)</td></tr><tr><td>Raw materials (no stock change)</td><td>Cost of goods sold</td></tr><tr><td>Salaries + Social Security</td><td>Operating expense</td></tr><tr><td>Other operating expenses</td><td>Operating expense</td></tr><tr><td>Depreciation of fixed assets</td><td>Operating expense [Slide 10]</td></tr><tr><td>Interest on the loan</td><td>Non-operating item [Slide 11]</td></tr><tr><td>Tax 30%</td><td>Income taxes</td></tr><tr><td>Dividend 50%</td><td>Distribution of net income (not an expense)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the net sales and the personnel expense</p>", "hint_html": "<p>The 3% discount applies only to Type A customers (40% of sales). Social Security is 30% on top of salaries.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Net sales 1,977.976 M€</li><li>Personnel 304.2 M€</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Net sales</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Type A sales} = 0.40 \\times 2{,}002 = 800.8 \\text{ M€}, \\qquad \\text{Discount} = 0.03 \\times 800.8 = 24.024 \\text{ M€}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Net sales} = 2{,}002 - 24.024 = 1{,}977.976 \\text{ M€}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Personnel expense</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Salaries} = 7{,}800 \\times 30{,}000 = 234{,}000{,}000 \\text{ €} = 234 \\text{ M€}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Social Security} = 0.30 \\times 234 = 70.2 \\text{ M€}, \\qquad \\text{Personnel} = 234 + 70.2 = 304.2 \\text{ M€}$$</div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the depreciation and the interest of 2017</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Linear depreciation over 10 years with no residual value; the loan bears 6% over the whole year.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Depreciation 120 M€; interest 13.5 M€</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 3. Depreciation and interest</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$D = \\frac{1{,}200 - 0}{10} = 120 \\text{ M€/year}, \\qquad I = 225 \\times 0.06 = 13.5 \\text{ M€}$$</div>"}, {"label": "c", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Prepare the income statement of 2017</p>", "hint_html": "<p>No change in stocks: purchases equal the cost of goods sold.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Gross profit 1,015.976 M€; income before taxes 121.276 M€</li><li>Net income 84.893 M€</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 4. Income statement 2017</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Computation</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">M€</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Net sales</td><td>Step 1</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,977.976</td></tr><tr><td>− Cost of goods sold (raw materials)</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">962.000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Gross profit</strong></td><td>$1{,}977.976 - 962$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>1,015.976</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Personnel</td><td>Step 2</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">304.200</td></tr><tr><td>− Other operating expenses</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">457.000</td></tr><tr><td>− Depreciation</td><td>Step 3</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">120.000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income from operations</strong></td><td>$1{,}015.976 - 881.2$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>134.776</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Interest expense</td><td>Step 3</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">13.500</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income before taxes</strong></td><td>$134.776 - 13.5$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>121.276</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Income taxes (30%)</td><td>$0.30 \\times 121.276$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">36.383</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Net income</strong></td><td>$121.276 - 36.3828$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>84.893</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "d", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Distribute the net income</p>", "hint_html": "<p>A dividend of 50% of the net income; the rest is self-financing.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Dividends 42.447 M€; retained earnings 42.447 M€</li><li>Pay-out 50%, self-financing 50%</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>!Problem - T3 Exercise 5 - Corporate Income Statement, Taxes and Dividends#Step 5. Distribution of the net income [Slide 23]</p>"}], "interpretation_html": "<ul><li><strong>Applying the discount to all the sales</strong> would deduct $0.03 \\times 2{,}002 = 60.06$ M€ instead of 24.024: the 40% restriction matters.</li><li><strong>The loan repayment in 2020</strong> does not appear in 2017: repaying principal is a payment that is never an expense.</li><li><strong>Exact values:</strong> income taxes 36.3828 M€ and net income 84.8932 M€ (shown rounded to three decimals in the table).</li><li><strong>Dividends</strong> come after taxes and are not an expense: the income statement ends at the net income, and the 50/50 split is shown separately.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Official solution, <code>T3_Financial Statements_SOLUTIONS.pdf</code>, p. 6</div><p>Every line coincides with the key: discount 24.024; net sales 1,977.976; cost of goods sold 962; personnel 304.2; other operating expenses 457; depreciation 120; EBIT 134.776; interest 13.5; income before taxes 121.276; tax 36.3828; net income 84.8932; dividends 42.4466 and retained earnings 42.4466 (all in M€).</p></div>"}, {"id": "P06", "category": "extra_practice_problem", "archetype": "narrative-case", "theme": "narrative-case", "unit": 3, "title": "T3 Exercise 6: Company X full cycle and the effect of uncalled capital", "difficulty": 2, "points": 4.0, "time_minutes": 30, "tags": ["official-exercise", "balance-sheet", "uncalled-capital", "cash"], "official": true, "source": "T3_Financial Statements_EXERCISES.pdf, Exercise 6 (official exercise, solved in Problem - T3 Exercise 6 - Company X Full Cycle Balance Sheet and Uncalled Capital)", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank P06 - T3 Exercise 6 Company X Full Cycle and Uncalled Capital.md", "page": "subjects/business-management/problemas/topic-3-financial-statements.html#exercise-6", "statement_html": "<p>Company X was incorporated on 1/01/2017 with a subscription of shares worth 100 m.u., fully paid in cash. On 1/01/2017 it bought, with cash: a) an area (land) for 20 m.u.; b) equipment with a life of 10 years (straight-line depreciation) and a purchase price of 20 m.u.; c) raw materials for 20 m.u.</p>\n<p>The company hires an employee for a total annual cost (salaries, Social Security, etc.) of 10 m.u. per year. This employee processes half of the raw material and sells it for a total of 50 m.u. Sales are the same every day of the year. The average collection period is 3 months.</p>\n<p>The single tax rate on the profits of the company is 30% (paid on 31/12/2017). The company follows a strict self-financing strategy.</p>\n<ol><li>Prepare the balance sheet at 31/12/2017, justifying and explaining the intermediate calculations.</li><li>Explain how the accounting statements would change if, instead of 100%, the shareholders had paid only 90% of the 100 m.u. subscribed.</li></ol>", "setup_html": "<p><strong>Period:</strong> calendar year 2017. <strong>Closing:</strong> 31/12/2017.</p>\n<p><strong>Hypotheses:</strong></p>\n<ol><li>The employee's cost is paid during the year.</li><li>Land has no useful life given and is not depreciated; the equipment has no residual value given (zero).</li><li><strong>Strict self-financing</strong> [Slide 23]: the whole net income is retained (pay-out 0%).</li><li>The tax is paid on 31/12, so no tax liability remains.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Income statement 2017</th><th>Cash 2017</th><th>Open at 31/12/2017</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Capital</td><td>—</td><td>+100</td><td>Share capital 100</td></tr><tr><td>Land</td><td>—</td><td>−20</td><td>Land 20</td></tr><tr><td>Equipment</td><td>Depreciation 2</td><td>−20</td><td>Equipment $20 - 2$</td></tr><tr><td>Raw materials</td><td>COGS 10 (half consumed)</td><td>−20</td><td>Inventory 10</td></tr><tr><td>Employee</td><td>Personnel 10</td><td>−10</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>Sales</td><td>Revenue 50</td><td>+37.5</td><td>Receivables 12.5</td></tr><tr><td>Tax</td><td>8.4</td><td>−8.4</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the depreciation, the cost of goods sold and the receivables at year end</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Only half of the raw material is processed and sold. With a 3-month collection period and even sales, 3/12 of the sales are uncollected.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Receivables 12.5 m.u.; inventory 10 m.u. left</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Depreciation and cost of goods sold</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$D = \\frac{20 - 0}{10} = 2 \\text{ m.u.}, \\qquad \\text{COGS} = \\frac{20}{2} = 10 \\text{ m.u.}, \\qquad \\text{Inventory} = 20 - 10 = 10 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Receivables from the collection period</h4>\n<p>Sales are spread evenly over the year and customers pay 3 months after buying. At 31/12 the sales of the last 3 months are still unpaid:</p>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Receivables} = 50 \\times \\frac{3}{12} = 12.5 \\text{ m.u.}, \\qquad \\text{Collected} = 50 - 12.5 = 37.5 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Prepare the income statement of 2017</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Tax 30%, paid on 31/12.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Gross profit 40.0 m.u.; net income 19.6 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 3. Income statement 2017</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Computation</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sales revenue</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">50.0</td></tr><tr><td>− Cost of goods sold</td><td>Step 1</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">10.0</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Gross profit</strong></td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>40.0</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Personnel</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">10.0</td></tr><tr><td>− Depreciation</td><td>Step 1</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2.0</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income from operations = income before taxes</strong></td><td>no interest</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>28.0</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Income taxes (30%)</td><td>$0.30 \\times 28$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">8.4</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Net income</strong></td><td>$28 - 8.4$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>19.6</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>All 19.6 is retained (strict self-financing).</p>"}, {"label": "c", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the cash and prepare the balance sheet at 31/12/2017</p>", "hint_html": "<p>A strict self-financing strategy means no dividends.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Cash 59.1 m.u.</li><li>Total 119.6 m.u. on both sides</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 4. Cash at 31/12/2017</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Cash} = 100 - 20 - 20 - 20 + 37.5 - 10 - 8.4 = 59.1 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 5. Balance sheet at 31/12/2017</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Assets</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th><th>Liabilities and equity</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Land</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">20.0</td><td>Share capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">100.0</td></tr><tr><td>Equipment ($20 - 2$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">18.0</td><td>Retained earnings (net income 2017)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">19.6</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Non-current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>38.0</strong></td><td><strong>Equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>119.6</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Inventory (raw materials)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">10.0</td><td><strong>Liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>0</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Accounts receivable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">12.5</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">59.1</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>81.6</strong></td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>119.6</strong></td><td><strong>Total liabilities and equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>119.6</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "d", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Explain the changes if the shareholders had paid only 90% of the capital</p>", "hint_html": "<p>The subscribed capital stays; the unpaid part is shown as a deduction. Does the income statement change?</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Income statement unchanged; cash 49.1 m.u.</li><li>Total 109.6 m.u. on both sides</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 6. What changes</h4>\n<p>The shareholders <strong>subscribed</strong> 100 m.u. but <strong>paid in</strong> only $0.90 \\times 100 = 90$ m.u. The remaining 10 m.u. is capital not yet paid (uncalled capital): the shareholders still owe it to the company.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Income statement:</strong> <strong>no change</strong>. Revenues and expenses do not depend on how the capital was paid; there is no interest involved. Net income stays 19.6.</li><li><strong>Cash:</strong> 10 m.u. less: $59.1 - 10 = 49.1$ m.u. All the purchases (60) can still be paid in cash, since the company receives 90.</li><li><strong>Equity:</strong> the contributed capital is what the stockholders actually provided [Slide 13, Slide 17], so the subscribed capital is shown minus the unpaid part: $100 - 10 = 90$.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Assets</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th><th>Liabilities and equity</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Non-current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>38.0</strong></td><td>Subscribed capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">100.0</td></tr><tr><td>Inventory</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">10.0</td><td>− Uncalled capital (unpaid by shareholders)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">(10.0)</td></tr><tr><td>Accounts receivable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">12.5</td><td>Retained earnings</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">19.6</td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">49.1</td><td><strong>Equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>109.6</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>71.6</strong></td><td><strong>Liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>0</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>109.6</strong></td><td><strong>Total liabilities and equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>109.6</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Both totals fall by exactly the 10 m.u. not paid.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": "<ul><li><strong>Balance:</strong> $38 + 81.6 = 119.6 = 100 + 19.6$ (requirement 1); $38 + 71.6 = 109.6 = 90 + 19.6$ (requirement 2).</li><li><strong>Equity bridge:</strong> equity = capital paid + net income retained: $100 + 19.6$ and $90 + 19.6$.</li><li><strong>Profit vs cash:</strong> after the purchases of 1/01, the cash generated during the year is $37.5 - 10 - 8.4 = 19.1$, while the profit is 19.6. Reconciliation: $19.6 + 2 + 10 - 12.5 = 19.1$, that is, profit + depreciation (expense, never a payment) + materials consumed (expense whose payment was made on 1/01 with the purchase) − sales not collected yet.</li><li><strong>Presentation note:</strong> some textbooks show the 10 m.u. owed by shareholders as an asset instead of deducting it from capital; then both totals stay at 119.6. The deduction used here is the one consistent with \"contributed capital\" in [Slide 17]. Both readings give the same net equity provided (109.6) and leave the income statement unchanged.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Official solution, <code>T3_Financial Statements_SOLUTIONS.pdf</code>, pp. 7-8</div><p>Requirement 1 coincides with the key: sales 50; cost of goods sold 10; personnel 10; depreciation 2; EBIT 28; tax 8.4; net income 19.6; cash 59.1; receivables 12.5; inventory 10; land 20; equipment 18; total assets 119.6. The key does not solve requirement 2 (90 % paid in), so that part of the solution is not covered by it.</p></div>"}, {"id": "P07", "category": "extra_practice_problem", "archetype": "narrative-case", "theme": "narrative-case", "unit": 3, "title": "T3 Additional 1: CIRCONSA half-year statements", "difficulty": 3, "points": 4.0, "time_minutes": 50, "tags": ["official-exercise", "half-year", "cash", "balance-sheet"], "official": true, "source": "T3_Additional_EXERCISES.pdf, Exercise 1 (official exercise, solved in Problem - T3 Extra 1 - Circonsa Balance Sheet and Income Statement)", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank P07 - T3 Extra 1 Circonsa Half-Year Statements.md", "page": "subjects/business-management/problemas/topic-3-extra-exam-problems.html#problem-1", "statement_html": "<p>The financial director of CIRCONSA is working on its financial statements (balance sheet and income statement) as of 30 June 2015. It has the balance of the company as of 31 December 2014 and the following operations that took place from 1 January 2015 to 30 June 2015. The company sells a certain product.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>i.</strong> During the semester the company sold 1,200 units at 20 m.u./unit (every month the same amount, 200 u.). Customers pay 30 days after the sale. A very important customer (30% of the sales), who bought an equal amount each month, was given a 10% discount on its purchases.</li><li><strong>ii.</strong> During the semester 1,000 units were purchased (40% in the first quarter and 60% in the second) at 12 m.u./u. Suppliers are paid at 90 days.</li><li><strong>iii.</strong> At 31/12/2014 there is a stock of 200 units valued at 12 m.u./u.</li><li><strong>iv.</strong> During the first quarter of 2015 the taxes and the outstanding supplier balances of the previous year were paid.</li><li><strong>v.</strong> During the first quarter of 2015 the customer debt outstanding from the previous year was collected, except 300 m.u. from a customer who requested to defer payment until July 2016.</li><li><strong>vi.</strong> Personnel expenses were 350 m.u./month (plus 150 for Social Security) and general expenses 300 m.u./month. Personnel and Social Security are paid in the month in which they occur; overheads two months after.</li><li><strong>vii.</strong> On 1/04/2015 a renting contract (3 years) for computer equipment was signed with a specialised company, 400 m.u. per month, with payment at 120 days (payment at the end of each month).</li><li><strong>viii.</strong> On 1/04/2015 a patent was bought for 2,400 m.u., to be paid on 1/07/2015. It is amortised in 4 years.</li><li><strong>ix.</strong> On 1/02/2015 a credit of 2,000 m.u. was obtained, to be returned a year later with 12% annual interest; interest and principal are paid on 1/02/2016.</li><li><strong>x.</strong> At 31/12/2014 the company had only one machine, bought on 1/07/2014, amortised linearly with a residual value of 20%.</li><li><strong>xi.</strong> On 1/02/2015 a capital increase (fully paid, no issue premium) of 10,000 m.u. was made. The same day a new machine costing 12,000 m.u. was bought: 8,000 paid in cash and the remainder on 1/07/2015. It is amortised in 4 years with zero residual value.</li><li><strong>xii.</strong> Assume a 20% corporate tax, recorded in the balance at 30/06/2015 as \"taxes to pay\".</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Balance sheet at 31/12/2014 (m.u.):</strong> current assets 5,400 (cash 1,200; accounts receivable 1,800; inventory 2,400); non-current assets 450 (machinery 500; accumulated depreciation 50); current liabilities 800 (unpaid taxes 350; suppliers 450); owners' equity 5,050 (retained earnings 850; paid-in capital 4,200); total 5,850.</p>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> calculate the balance sheet and the profit and loss account of CIRCONSA at 30 June 2015.</p>", "setup_html": "<p><strong>Period:</strong> 1/01/2015 to 30/06/2015, <strong>6 months</strong>. <strong>Closing instant:</strong> 30/06/2015. Every monthly figure is multiplied by the months it applies inside this window.</p>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>Event</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Jan-Mar</td><td>Sales 200 u./month; purchases 400 u. (Q1); payment of 2014 taxes and suppliers; collection of 2014 receivables except 300</td></tr><tr><td>1/02</td><td>Credit 2,000 received; capital increase 10,000; machine 2 bought (8,000 paid)</td></tr><tr><td>1/04</td><td>Renting starts (400/month); patent bought (2,400, payable 1/07)</td></tr><tr><td>Apr-Jun</td><td>Sales 200 u./month; purchases 600 u. (Q2)</td></tr><tr><td>30/06</td><td><strong>Closing</strong></td></tr><tr><td>1/07</td><td>Payment of patent and of the 4,000 left on machine 2 (after the closing)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Hypotheses</strong> (all read from the statement):</p>\n<ol><li>Sales are uniform (200 u./month) and collected 30 days later, so at 30/06 only <strong>June's sales</strong> are uncollected.</li><li>Q1 purchases are paid 90 days later, inside the semester; <strong>Q2 purchases</strong> are still unpaid at 30/06.</li><li>Overheads are paid two months later: January-April are paid, <strong>May and June</strong> are unpaid.</li><li>The renting is paid at 120 days: none of the April-June instalments has been paid at 30/06.</li><li>Simple interest, prorated by months. Tax: 20% of income before taxes, left unpaid.</li><li>Machine 1: its useful life is not given; it is deduced from the opening balance (Phase 3, Step 5).</li></ol>\n<p>The three questions of the method (income statement? cash? open balance item at 30/06?) applied to each operation:</p>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Op.</th><th>Income statement (6 months)</th><th>Cash in (+) / out (−)</th><th>Open at 30/06</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>i. Sales</td><td>Net sales 23,280</td><td>+19,400 (Jan-May)</td><td>Receivables 3,880 (June)</td></tr><tr><td>ii-iii. Purchases and stock</td><td>COGS 14,400</td><td>−4,800 (Q1)</td><td>Inventory 0; suppliers 7,200 (Q2)</td></tr><tr><td>iv. 2014 taxes and suppliers</td><td>—</td><td>−350 − 450</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>v. 2014 receivables</td><td>—</td><td>+1,500</td><td>Long-term receivable 300</td></tr><tr><td>vi. Personnel + SS</td><td>3,000</td><td>−3,000</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>vi. Overheads</td><td>1,800</td><td>−1,200 (Jan-Apr)</td><td>Overheads payable 600</td></tr><tr><td>vii. Renting</td><td>1,200</td><td>0</td><td>Renting payable 1,200</td></tr><tr><td>viii. Patent</td><td>Amortisation 150</td><td>0</td><td>Patent 2,400 − 150; patent payable 2,400</td></tr><tr><td>ix. Credit</td><td>Interest 100</td><td>+2,000</td><td>Credit 2,000; interest payable 100</td></tr><tr><td>x. Machine 1</td><td>Depreciation 50</td><td>0</td><td>Machine 500 − 100</td></tr><tr><td>xi. Capital increase</td><td>—</td><td>+10,000</td><td>Capital +10,000</td></tr><tr><td>xi. Machine 2</td><td>Depreciation 1,250</td><td>−8,000</td><td>Machine 12,000 − 1,250; debt 4,000</td></tr><tr><td>xii. Tax</td><td>266</td><td>0</td><td>Taxes payable 266</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Each figure is derived in Phase 3.</p>", "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Sales, purchases, stock and the items pending from 2014</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Draw the timeline month by month: what is collected at 30 days and paid at 90 days falls inside or outside the semester?</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Receivables from June sales 3,880 m.u.</li><li>Second-quarter purchases (7,200) unpaid at 30/06</li><li>300 m.u. of 2014 receivables deferred to July 2016</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Sales and receivables (op. i)</h4>\n<p>Gross sales per month: $200 \\times 20 = 4{,}000$ m.u. The important customer buys 30% of them, so its discount per month is</p>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$0.30 \\times 4{,}000 \\times 0.10 = 120 \\text{ m.u./month}$$</div>\n<p>Net sales per month: $4{,}000 - 120 = 3{,}880$ m.u. Over the semester:</p>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Net sales} = 6 \\times 3{,}880 = 23{,}280 \\text{ m.u.} \\quad (= 24{,}000 - 720)$$</div>\n<p>Collections at 30 days: the sales of January to May are collected by 30/06 ($5 \\times 3{,}880 = 19{,}400$); June's sales are still owed: <strong>receivables 3,880</strong>.</p>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Purchases, stock and cost of goods sold (ops. ii, iii)</h4>\n<p>Units: initial stock 200 + purchases 1,000 = 1,200 available; 1,200 sold, so the ending stock is 0 units. All units cost 12 m.u., so</p>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{COGS} = \\text{Initial inventory} + \\text{Purchases} - \\text{Ending inventory} = 2{,}400 + 12{,}000 - 0 = 14{,}400 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<p>Purchases: Q1 $= 0.40 \\times 1{,}000 \\times 12 = 4{,}800$, paid at 90 days (inside the semester); Q2 $= 0.60 \\times 1{,}000 \\times 12 = 7{,}200$, <strong>unpaid at 30/06</strong>.</p>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 3. Items from 2014 (ops. iv, v)</h4>\n<p>Paid in Q1: taxes 350 and suppliers 450 (cash −800; both liabilities disappear). Collected in Q1: $1{,}800 - 300 = 1{,}500$. The remaining 300 is due in July 2016, more than one year after 30/06/2015, so it becomes a <strong>non-current receivable</strong> [Slide 15].</p>\n<p>None of these affects the income statement: they settle revenues and expenses of 2014.</p>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Personnel, overheads, renting, fixed assets and the credit</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Separate the expense of the semester from what is paid; depreciate each asset only for the months it was used.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Overheads payable 600; renting payable 1,200 m.u.</li><li>Machine 2 balance and patent due 1/07/2015: current liabilities</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 4. Personnel, overheads and renting (ops. vi, vii)</h4>\n<ul><li>Personnel + Social Security: $6 \\times (350 + 150) = 3{,}000$, all paid.</li><li>Overheads: $6 \\times 300 = 1{,}800$ of expense; paid two months later, so January-April are paid ($4 \\times 300 = 1{,}200$) and May-June are owed: <strong>600 payable</strong>.</li><li>Renting: April, May, June, $3 \\times 400 = 1{,}200$ of expense; paid at 120 days, nothing paid by 30/06: <strong>1,200 payable</strong>.</li></ul>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 5. Fixed assets: depreciation and amortisation (ops. viii, x, xi)</h4>\n<p><strong>Machine 1.</strong> Cost 500, residual value $0.20 \\times 500 = 100$, depreciable base $500 - 100 = 400$. The opening accumulated depreciation is 50 for the 6 months from 1/07/2014 to 31/12/2014, so the annual depreciation is $50 \\times \\frac{12}{6} = 100$ and the useful life is $\\frac{400}{100} = 4$ years. For the semester:</p>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$D_1 = \\frac{500 - 100}{4} \\times \\frac{6}{12} = 50 \\text{ m.u.}, \\qquad \\text{accumulated} = 50 + 50 = 100$$</div>\n<p><strong>Machine 2.</strong> Bought 1/02, used 5 months (February-June), zero residual value:</p>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$D_2 = \\frac{12{,}000 - 0}{4} \\times \\frac{5}{12} = 1{,}250 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Patent.</strong> Bought 1/04, used 3 months:</p>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$A = \\frac{2{,}400}{4} \\times \\frac{3}{12} = 150 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<p>Total depreciation and amortisation: $50 + 1{,}250 + 150 = 1{,}450$ m.u. None of it is a payment.</p>\n<p>Payments: machine 2, 8,000 paid in cash; the other 4,000 and the whole patent (2,400) are due on 1/07/2015, one day after the closing, so they are <strong>current liabilities</strong> at 30/06.</p>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 6. Credit and interest (op. ix)</h4>\n<p>Received 1/02 (cash +2,000), returned on 1/02/2016, within one year of 30/06/2015: <strong>current liability</strong>. Interest from 1/02 to 30/06 (5 months), paid only in 2016:</p>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$I = 2{,}000 \\times 0.12 \\times \\frac{5}{12} = 100 \\text{ m.u.} \\quad \\text{(expense and interest payable)}$$</div>"}, {"label": "c", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Prepare the income statement of the semester</p>", "hint_html": "<p>The interest of the credit accrues even though it is paid in 2016.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Income from operations 1,430 m.u.</li><li>Net income 1,064 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 7. Income statement for the semester</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Computation</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Net sales</td><td>$24{,}000 - 720$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">23,280</td></tr><tr><td>− Cost of goods sold</td><td>Step 2</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">14,400</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Gross profit</strong></td><td>$23{,}280 - 14{,}400$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>8,880</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Personnel and Social Security</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Overheads</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,800</td></tr><tr><td>− Renting</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,200</td></tr><tr><td>− Depreciation and amortisation</td><td>$50 + 1{,}250 + 150$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,450</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income from operations</strong></td><td>$8{,}880 - 7{,}450$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>1,430</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Interest expense</td><td>Step 6</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">100</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income before taxes</strong></td><td>$1{,}430 - 100$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>1,330</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Income taxes (20%)</td><td>$0.20 \\times 1{,}330$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">266</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Net income</strong></td><td>$1{,}330 - 266$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>1,064</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "d", "points": 1.5, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the cash and prepare the balance sheet at 30/06/2015</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Cash = opening cash + collections − payments; every amount still open is a receivable or a payable.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Cash 16,300 m.u.</li><li>Total 33,880 m.u. on both sides</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 8. Cash at 30/06/2015</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Inflows</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th><th>Outflows</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Opening cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,200</td><td>2014 taxes</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">350</td></tr><tr><td>2014 receivables collected</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,500</td><td>2014 suppliers</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">450</td></tr><tr><td>Sales Jan-May collected</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">19,400</td><td>Q1 purchases</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">4,800</td></tr><tr><td>Credit</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2,000</td><td>Personnel + SS</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3,000</td></tr><tr><td>Capital increase</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">10,000</td><td>Overheads Jan-Apr</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,200</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Machine 2 (cash part)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">8,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>34,100</strong></td><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>17,800</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Cash}_{30/06} = 34{,}100 - 17{,}800 = 16{,}300 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 9. Balance sheet at 30/06/2015</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Assets</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th><th>Liabilities and equity</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Machinery (cost $500 + 12{,}000$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">12,500</td><td>Paid-in capital ($4{,}200 + 10{,}000$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">14,200</td></tr><tr><td>− Accumulated depreciation ($100 + 1{,}250$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">(1,350)</td><td>Retained earnings (2014)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">850</td></tr><tr><td>Patent ($2{,}400 - 150$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2,250</td><td>Net income of the semester</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,064</td></tr><tr><td>Long-term receivable (due July 2016)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">300</td><td><strong>Equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>16,114</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Non-current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>13,700</strong></td><td>Credit (due 1/02/2016)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2,000</td></tr><tr><td>Inventory</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0</td><td>Interest payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">100</td></tr><tr><td>Accounts receivable (June)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3,880</td><td>Suppliers (Q2 purchases)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">7,200</td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">16,300</td><td>Debt for machine 2 (due 1/07)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">4,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>20,180</strong></td><td>Debt for patent (due 1/07)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2,400</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Overheads payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">600</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Renting payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,200</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Taxes payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">266</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td><strong>Current liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>17,766</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>33,880</strong></td><td><strong>Total liabilities and equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>33,880</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>There are no long-term liabilities: every debt is due before 30/06/2016.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": "<ul><li><strong>Balance:</strong> $13{,}700 + 20{,}180 = 33{,}880 = 16{,}114 + 17{,}766$.</li><li><strong>Equity bridge:</strong> equity grows from 5,050 to 16,114, that is $+11{,}064 = 10{,}000$ (capital increase) $+ 1{,}064$ (net income).</li><li><strong>Profit is not cash:</strong> the semester earns 1,064 but cash grows by $16{,}300 - 1{,}200 = 15{,}100$, mostly because of the capital increase (10,000), the credit (2,000) and the many expenses still unpaid (suppliers, renting, overheads, interest, taxes). In the opposite direction, 8,000 of cash went into a machine whose expense this semester is only 1,250.</li><li><strong>Limiting case:</strong> if June's sales had been collected, cash would be 3,880 higher and receivables zero, but net income would not change: revenue is recognised when the sale is made [Slide 8].</li><li><strong>Exam traps:</strong> (1) the 1/07 debts are liabilities at 30/06, not payments; (2) the 300 deferred to July 2016 is non-current; (3) machine 2 is depreciated 5 months and the patent 3 months, not 6; (4) the life of machine 1 must be deduced from the opening balance.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Official solution, <code>T3_Additional_SOLUTIONS.pdf</code>, pp. 6-8</div><p>The income statement and the cash account coincide with the key: net sales 23,280; cost of goods sold 14,400; personnel 3,000; general expenses 1,800; renting 1,200; EBITDA 2,880; depreciation and amortisation 1,450; EBIT 1,430; interest 100; income before taxes 1,330; tax 266; net income 1,064; collections 34,100; cash 16,300; total 33,880. The key writes the depreciation of machine 1 as $(500 - 100)/8$, that is, 8 half-years, the same 4-year life deduced above.</p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout-warning\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Discrepancy with the official solution (the 300 deferred to July 2016)</div><p>The key shows accounts receivable of 4,180, which includes the 300 due in July 2016, so it counts them as a current asset (current assets 20,480; non-current assets 13,400). Here they are a non-current receivable, because they fall due more than one year after 30/06/2015 (Slide 15), so receivables are 3,880 + 300 (current assets 20,180; non-current assets 13,700). Total assets, liabilities and equity are the same in both.</p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout-warning\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Erratum in the official solution</div><p>The key's payments column adds to 16,800, but its items add to $450 + 3{,}000 + 1{,}200 + 4{,}800 + 350 + 8{,}000 = 17{,}800$, which gives the cash of 16,300 that it does show. The capital increase in the equity block reads 1,000 instead of 10,000 (only 10,000 gives equity of 16,114), and the labels of the interest payable (100) and the tax payable (266) are swapped.</p></div>"}, {"id": "P08", "category": "extra_practice_problem", "archetype": "year-on-year-cash", "theme": "year-on-year-cash", "unit": 3, "title": "T3 Additional 2: INFOPYMES statements 2016-2017 and ROA/ROE", "difficulty": 3, "points": 4.0, "time_minutes": 45, "tags": ["official-exercise", "roa", "roe", "cash", "balance-sheet"], "official": true, "source": "T3_Additional_EXERCISES.pdf, Exercise 2 (official exercise, solved in Problem - T3 Extra 2 - Infopymes Full Cycle Financial Statements and ROA ROE)", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank P08 - T3 Extra 2 Infopymes Statements and ROA ROE.md", "page": "subjects/business-management/problemas/topic-3-extra-exam-problems.html#problem-2", "statement_html": "<p>Three entrepreneurs established INFOPYMES on 1/01/2016 to provide on-line information services for small and medium enterprises, start-ups and potential entrepreneurs.</p>\n<ul><li>The computer equipment, worth 100,000 €, was acquired on 1/01/2016 and will be paid to suppliers after three months, on 1/04/2016. Useful life 5 years (until 31/12/2020), linear depreciation, no residual value.</li><li>On-line consultations brought revenues of 300,000 € in 2016.</li><li>Rented premises: 1,000 €/month from 1/01/2016. Salaries and Social Security of the permanent employees: 5,000 €/month. Electricity, phone, etc.: 500 €/month.</li><li>Variable wages of the additional staff for the on-line consultations: 174,000 € in 2016.</li><li>The partners contributed an initial capital of 100,000 €. A bank granted a loan of 100,000 € at 10% yearly interest for 5 years (1/01/2016 to 31/12/2020). Each 31 December the company returns 20,000 € and pays the interest (10%) on the loan value at the beginning of each year.</li><li>Results are declared at 23:59 on 31 December (after all payments and receipts, including interest, wages, Social Security, etc.), and the corporation tax of 30% on the profits is paid immediately. Dividends of 40% of the profits after taxes are distributed, and the final balance is drawn at 24:00.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Required:</strong> (1) the income statement for 2016; (2) assuming the tax has been paid and the dividends distributed, the balance sheet at 24:00 on 31/12/2016; (3) the balance sheet at 24:00 on 31/12/2017, assuming no new investments in 2017 and that the retained earnings and the cash balance at the end of 2017 remained unchanged with respect to 2016; (4) the ROA and ROE ratios in 2017.</p>", "setup_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Capital</td><td>100,000 € (1/01/2016)</td></tr><tr><td>Loan</td><td>100,000 € at 10%; 20,000 € returned each 31/12; interest on the balance at the start of each year</td></tr><tr><td>Equipment</td><td>100,000 €, bought 1/01/2016, paid 1/04/2016, 5 years, no residual value</td></tr><tr><td>Revenues 2016</td><td>300,000 €</td></tr><tr><td>Fixed monthly costs</td><td>Rent 1,000; permanent staff 5,000; utilities 500 (€/month)</td></tr><tr><td>Variable wages 2016</td><td>174,000 €</td></tr><tr><td>Tax</td><td>30% of profits, paid at 23:59 on 31/12</td></tr><tr><td>Dividends</td><td>40% of profit after taxes, paid at 23:59 on 31/12</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Hypotheses:</strong></p>\n<ol><li>The statement says the results are declared \"after having made all payments and receipts\", so the 300,000 € of revenues are <strong>collected</strong> and all 2016 costs are <strong>paid</strong> within 2016.</li><li>The balance is drawn at 24:00, <strong>after</strong> paying tax, interest, the 20,000 € instalment and dividends: none of them remains as a liability.</li><li>For 2017 the statement gives no operating data; we assume the <strong>same revenues and operating costs as in 2016</strong>, which is the only reading that lets requirement 4 be computed. With retained earnings unchanged, the whole 2017 profit must have been paid out as dividends (shown in Phase 3).</li></ol>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Income statement 2016</th><th>Cash 2016</th><th>Open at 24:00 on 31/12/2016</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Capital</td><td>—</td><td>+100,000</td><td>Capital 100,000</td></tr><tr><td>Loan</td><td>—</td><td>+100,000</td><td>Loan 80,000 (after the instalment)</td></tr><tr><td>Equipment</td><td>Depreciation 20,000</td><td>−100,000 (on 1/04)</td><td>Equipment 100,000 − 20,000</td></tr><tr><td>Revenues</td><td>300,000</td><td>+300,000</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>Rent, staff, utilities, variable wages</td><td>252,000</td><td>−252,000</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>Interest</td><td>10,000</td><td>−10,000</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>Instalment</td><td>—</td><td>−20,000</td><td>(reduces the loan)</td></tr><tr><td>Tax</td><td>5,400</td><td>−5,400</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>Dividends</td><td>— (distribution)</td><td>−5,040</td><td>Retained earnings 7,560</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The equipment is bought on credit on 1/01 and paid on 1/04: between those dates it is a debt to the supplier, but at 31/12 that debt no longer exists.</p>", "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Prepare the income statement for 2016</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Interest is computed on the loan balance at the beginning of the year; the equipment is depreciated over 5 years.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Depreciation 20,000; interest 10,000 €</li><li>Income from operations 28,000; net income 12,600 €</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Depreciation and interest 2016</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$D = \\frac{100{,}000 - 0}{5} \\times \\frac{12}{12} = 20{,}000 \\text{ €/year}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$I_{2016} = 100{,}000 \\times 0.10 = 10{,}000 \\text{ €} \\quad \\text{(loan value at the beginning of 2016)}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Income statement 2016 (requirement 1)</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Computation</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">€</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Revenues (on-line consultations)</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">300,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Rent</td><td>$12 \\times 1{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">12,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Permanent staff and Social Security</td><td>$12 \\times 5{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">60,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Electricity, phone, etc.</td><td>$12 \\times 500$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">6,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Variable wages</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">174,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Depreciation</td><td>Step 1</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">20,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income from operations</strong></td><td>$300{,}000 - 272{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>28,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Interest expense</td><td>Step 1</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">10,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income before taxes</strong></td><td>$28{,}000 - 10{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>18,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Income taxes (30%)</td><td>$0.30 \\times 18{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">5,400</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Net income</strong></td><td>$18{,}000 - 5{,}400$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>12,600</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Distribution [Slide 23]: dividends $= 0.40 \\times 12{,}600 = 5{,}040$ €; retained earnings (self-financing) $= 12{,}600 - 5{,}040 = 7{,}560$ €.</p>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the cash and prepare the balance sheet at 24:00 on 31/12/2016</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Tax, interest, the 20,000 instalment and the dividend are all paid before 24:00. Split the remaining loan by maturity.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Cash 107,560 €</li><li>Total 187,560 € on both sides</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 3. Cash at 24:00 on 31/12/2016</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Inflows</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">€</th><th>Outflows</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">€</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">100,000</td><td>Equipment (1/04)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">100,000</td></tr><tr><td>Loan</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">100,000</td><td>Rent + staff + utilities</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">78,000</td></tr><tr><td>Revenues collected</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">300,000</td><td>Variable wages</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">174,000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Interest</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">10,000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Loan instalment</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">20,000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Tax</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">5,400</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Dividends</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">5,040</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>500,000</strong></td><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>392,440</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Cash}_{2016} = 500{,}000 - 392{,}440 = 107{,}560 \\text{ €}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 4. Balance sheet at 24:00 on 31/12/2016 (requirement 2)</h4>\n<p>The loan after the first instalment is $100{,}000 - 20{,}000 = 80{,}000$ €. The instalment due on 31/12/2017 (20,000 €) is within one year, so it is a <strong>current liability</strong>; the other 60,000 € are long-term [Slide 16].</p>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Assets</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">€</th><th>Liabilities and equity</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">€</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Equipment</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">100,000</td><td>Capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">100,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Accumulated depreciation</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">(20,000)</td><td>Retained earnings</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">7,560</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Non-current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>80,000</strong></td><td><strong>Equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>107,560</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">107,560</td><td>Long-term bank loan</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">60,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>107,560</strong></td><td>Short-term bank loan (due 31/12/2017)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">20,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>187,560</strong></td><td><strong>Total liabilities and equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>187,560</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "c", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Prepare the balance sheet at 24:00 on 31/12/2017</p>", "hint_html": "<p>No new investment: one more year of depreciation and one more instalment; cash and retained earnings unchanged.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Total 167,560 € on both sides</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 5. Balance sheet at 24:00 on 31/12/2017 (requirement 3)</h4>\n<p>Item by item:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Equipment:</strong> no new investment; two years of depreciation, $2 \\times 20{,}000 = 40{,}000$; net value $100{,}000 - 40{,}000 = 60{,}000$ €.</li><li><strong>Loan:</strong> second instalment paid, $80{,}000 - 20{,}000 = 60{,}000$ €: 20,000 € current (due 31/12/2018) and 40,000 € long-term.</li><li><strong>Cash and retained earnings:</strong> unchanged by statement: 107,560 € and 7,560 €.</li><li><strong>Capital:</strong> unchanged, 100,000 €.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Assets</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">€</th><th>Liabilities and equity</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">€</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Equipment</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">100,000</td><td>Capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">100,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Accumulated depreciation</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">(40,000)</td><td>Retained earnings</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">7,560</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Non-current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>60,000</strong></td><td><strong>Equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>107,560</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">107,560</td><td>Long-term bank loan</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">40,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>107,560</strong></td><td>Short-term bank loan (due 31/12/2018)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">20,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>167,560</strong></td><td><strong>Total liabilities and equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>167,560</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Is the statement consistent?</strong> With the 2016 operations repeated (hypothesis 3), 2017 gives:</p>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$I_{2017} = 80{,}000 \\times 0.10 = 8{,}000, \\quad \\text{IBT} = 28{,}000 - 8{,}000 = 20{,}000, \\quad T = 0.30 \\times 20{,}000 = 6{,}000, \\quad \\text{NI} = 14{,}000$$</div>\n<p>Retained earnings unchanged means dividends $= 14{,}000$ € (a 100% pay-out). Cash then moves by $+300{,}000 - 252{,}000 - 8{,}000 - 20{,}000 - 6{,}000 - 14{,}000 = 0$, so the cash is indeed unchanged: the two conditions of the statement are compatible.</p>"}, {"label": "d", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the ROA and the ROE of 2017</p>", "hint_html": "<p>The course decks do not define them: state the definitions you use (extension).</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>ROA 16.71%; ROE 13.02% (income from operations / total assets; net income / equity)</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 6. ROA and ROE in 2017 (requirement 4)</h4>\n<div class=\"callout callout-warning\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Definitions not in the Topic 3 slides</div><p>The Topic 3 deck in <code>sources/</code> does not define ROA and ROE, but the problem asks for them. The standard definitions used here are $\\text{ROA} = \\dfrac{\\text{Income from operations}}{\\text{Total assets}}$ (the return of the economic structure) and $\\text{ROE} = \\dfrac{\\text{Net income}}{\\text{Equity}}$ (the return to the owners). Check them against your class notes: if your lecturer defines ROA with net income instead, the value is $14{,}000 / 167{,}560 = 8.36\\%$.</p></div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{ROA}_{2017} = \\frac{28{,}000}{167{,}560} = 0.1671 = 16.71\\%$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{ROE}_{2017} = \\frac{14{,}000}{107{,}560} = 0.1302 = 13.02\\%$$</div>"}], "interpretation_html": "<ul><li><strong>Balances:</strong> $80{,}000 + 107{,}560 = 187{,}560 = 107{,}560 + 60{,}000 + 20{,}000$ (2016) and $60{,}000 + 107{,}560 = 167{,}560 = 107{,}560 + 40{,}000 + 20{,}000$ (2017).</li><li><strong>What changes between the two years:</strong> both sides shrink by 20,000 €: the asset loses 20,000 € of value through depreciation, and the loan falls by the 20,000 € instalment, which in cash terms was financed by the cash generated (depreciation is an expense that is not a payment).</li><li><strong>Leverage</strong> [Slide 36]: $L/E = 80{,}000/107{,}560 = 0.74$ in 2016 and $60{,}000/107{,}560 = 0.56$ in 2017; the company becomes less indebted each year.</li><li><strong>ROE &lt; ROA here:</strong> the return to the owners is lower than the return of the assets because interest and taxes are deducted from the numerator; the equity base being smaller than total assets is not enough to compensate.</li><li><strong>Exam traps:</strong> (1) the loan instalment is a payment, never an expense; (2) at 24:00 no tax or dividend liability remains; (3) the current part of the loan (next instalment) must be separated from the long-term part.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Official solution, <code>T3_Additional_SOLUTIONS.pdf</code>, pp. 12-14</div><p>Requirements 1 and 2 coincide with the key in every figure: EBIT 28,000; interest 10,000; income before taxes 18,000; tax 5,400; net income 12,600; dividends 5,040; retained earnings 7,560; cash 107,560; equipment net 80,000; total 187,560; bank loan 20,000 current and 60,000 long term; equity 107,560.</p>\n<p>The key does not solve requirement 3 (balance sheet at 31/12/2017) nor requirement 4 (ROA and ROE), so Steps 5 and 6 above are not covered by it.</p></div>"}, {"id": "P09", "category": "extra_practice_problem", "archetype": "narrative-case", "theme": "narrative-case", "unit": 3, "title": "T3 Additional 3: WIFINET accounting cycle and two ways of paying a dividend", "difficulty": 3, "points": 4.0, "time_minutes": 50, "tags": ["official-exercise", "dividends", "balance-sheet", "losses"], "official": true, "source": "T3_Additional_EXERCISES.pdf, Exercise 3 (official exercise, solved in Problem - T3 Extra 3 - Wifinet Internet Provider Accounting Cycle and Dividends)", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank P09 - T3 Extra 3 Wifinet Accounting Cycle and Dividends.md", "page": "subjects/business-management/problemas/topic-3-extra-exam-problems.html#problem-3", "statement_html": "<p>Wifinet S.L. was incorporated on 1/01/2016 with a registered capital of 6,003 m.u., to provide wireless Internet on the Alicante coast.</p>\n<p><strong>Fiscal year 2016.</strong> It took a bank loan of 2,000 m.u. at 5% annual interest (payable at the end of each year), the principal being repayable in 2019. It invested 5,371 m.u. in technical equipment with a life of 20 years; at the end of 2016 it recorded an accumulated depreciation of 26 m.u. for those assets. It also invested 206 m.u. in licence fees by 31/12/2016, to use certain technologies for 5 years. It spent 636 m.u. on financial shares of other companies. Most of 2016 was devoted to building the network and business started only in the last two months. At the end of 2016 the balance sheet reflected a <strong>loss of 340 m.u.</strong> and <strong>cash in hand and bank of 1,476 m.u.</strong></p>\n<p>In 2017 the results were positive, but the company decided not to distribute dividends and to retain all its profits to reduce the loss of the previous year.</p>\n<p><strong>Fiscal year 2017.</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Internet and VOIP: 5,000 customers on average, with an average revenue of 0.032 m.u./month per client in subscription fees. Additionally, 46 m.u. of on-line sales of other products. At the end of 2017 customers owed 164 m.u.</li><li>Stocks were exhausted at the end of 2016. Purchases of materials to be installed in customers' homes: 680 m.u., of which 560 m.u. paid immediately and the rest deferred to the following year. The 2017 sales consumed half of the purchased goods.</li><li>Staff costs: 559 m.u., paid in full in 2017. Supplies and outside services: 312 m.u., paid.</li><li>Other administrative expenses: 300 m.u., 60% of them deferred to the following year.</li><li>Corporation tax: 30%, payable in the first quarter of 2018.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Required:</strong> (1) the income statement for 2017; (2) the balance sheet at 31/12/2017; (3) which balance-sheet items change, and by how much, if a dividend of 20% of net profit is paid at 24:00 on 31/12/2017; (4) the same if the dividend of 20% is decided before the end of 2017 and paid on 30/06/2018.</p>", "setup_html": "<p><strong>Period:</strong> calendar year 2017. <strong>Closing:</strong> 31/12/2017. The 2016 data serve only to build the <strong>opening balance</strong> of 2017.</p>\n<p><strong>Hypotheses:</strong></p>\n<ol><li>The licences were bought at the very end of 2016, so they start to be amortised in 2017 (no 2016 amortisation is given).</li><li>The equipment was depreciated only 26 m.u. in 2016 (part of the year); in 2017 it is used the whole year.</li><li>Interest is paid at the end of each year, so no interest payable remains; the loan is due in 2019, more than one year after 31/12/2017: <strong>long-term</strong> [Slide 16].</li><li>The financial shares are a long-term investment (non-current asset) [Slide 15]; they produce no revenue in the data.</li></ol>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Opening balance at 31/12/2016 (rebuilt from the data)</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Assets</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th><th>Liabilities and equity</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Technical equipment ($5{,}371 - 26$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">5,345</td><td>Capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">6,003</td></tr><tr><td>Licences</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">206</td><td>Loss of 2016</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">(340)</td></tr><tr><td>Financial shares</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">636</td><td><strong>Equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>5,663</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,476</td><td>Bank loan (due 2019)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>7,663</strong></td><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>7,663</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>It balances ($7{,}663 = 5{,}663 + 2{,}000$), which confirms that no other 2016 item is missing.</p>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Operations of 2017</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Income statement 2017</th><th>Cash 2017</th><th>Open at 31/12/2017</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Subscriptions + on-line sales</td><td>Revenue 1,966</td><td>+1,802</td><td>Receivables 164</td></tr><tr><td>Purchases of materials (680)</td><td>COGS 340</td><td>−560</td><td>Inventory 340; suppliers 120</td></tr><tr><td>Staff</td><td>559</td><td>−559</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>Supplies and outside services</td><td>312</td><td>−312</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>Administrative expenses</td><td>300</td><td>−120</td><td>Other creditors 180</td></tr><tr><td>Equipment depreciation</td><td>268.55</td><td>0</td><td>Accumulated depreciation 294.55</td></tr><tr><td>Licence amortisation</td><td>41.20</td><td>0</td><td>Licences 164.80</td></tr><tr><td>Interest</td><td>100</td><td>−100</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>Tax</td><td>13.575</td><td>0</td><td>Taxes payable 13.575</td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.5, "prompt_html": "<p>Prepare the income statement for 2017</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Compute revenues, consumption, operating expenses, depreciation and interest first; check whether earlier losses reduce the tax.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Income from operations 145.25 m.u.</li><li>Net income 31.675 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Revenues and receivables</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Subscriptions} = 5{,}000 \\text{ clients} \\times 0.032 \\tfrac{\\text{m.u.}}{\\text{client} \\cdot \\text{month}} \\times 12 \\text{ months} = 1{,}920 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Revenues} = 1{,}920 + 46 = 1{,}966 \\text{ m.u.}, \\qquad \\text{Collected} = 1{,}966 - 164 = 1{,}802 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Purchases, consumption and inventory</h4>\n<p>Opening stock 0 (\"stocks were exhausted at the end of 2016\"); purchases 680; half consumed:</p>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{COGS} = 0 + 680 - 340 = 340 \\text{ m.u.}, \\qquad \\text{Ending inventory} = 340 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<p>Payment: 560 paid; $680 - 560 = 120$ owed to suppliers.</p>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 3. Operating expenses</h4>\n<ul><li>Staff 559 and supplies and outside services 312, all paid.</li><li>Administrative expenses 300: 60% deferred, $0.60 \\times 300 = 180$ owed; $300 - 180 = 120$ paid.</li></ul>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 4. Depreciation and amortisation</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$D_{\\text{equipment}} = \\frac{5{,}371}{20} = 268.55 \\text{ m.u.}, \\qquad A_{\\text{licences}} = \\frac{206}{5} = 41.20 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<p>Accumulated depreciation of the equipment: $26 + 268.55 = 294.55$; net value $5{,}371 - 294.55 = 5{,}076.45$. Licences: $206 - 41.20 = 164.80$.</p>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 5. Interest</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$I = 2{,}000 \\times 0.05 = 100 \\text{ m.u.} \\quad \\text{(paid at the end of 2017)}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 6. Income statement 2017 (requirement 1)</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Computation</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Revenues</td><td>$1{,}920 + 46$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,966.000</td></tr><tr><td>− Cost of goods sold (materials consumed)</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">340.000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Gross profit</strong></td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>1,626.000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Staff</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">559.000</td></tr><tr><td>− Supplies and outside services</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">312.000</td></tr><tr><td>− Administrative expenses</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">300.000</td></tr><tr><td>− Depreciation and amortisation</td><td>$268.55 + 41.20$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">309.750</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income from operations</strong></td><td>$1{,}626 - 1{,}480.75$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>145.250</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Interest expense</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">100.000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income before taxes</strong></td><td>$145.25 - 100$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>45.250</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Income taxes (30%)</td><td>$0.30 \\times 45.25$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">13.575</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Net income</strong></td><td>$45.25 - 13.575$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>31.675</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.5, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the cash and prepare the balance sheet at 31/12/2017</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Start from the opening cash of 1,476 m.u.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Cash 1,627 m.u.</li><li>Total 8,008.25 m.u. on both sides</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 7. Cash at 31/12/2017</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Cash} = 1{,}476 + 1{,}802 - 560 - 559 - 312 - 120 - 100 = 1{,}627 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 8. Balance sheet at 31/12/2017 (requirement 2)</h4>\n<p>All the profit is retained, so it stays in equity and reduces the accumulated loss: $-340 + 31.675 = -308.325$.</p>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Assets</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th><th>Liabilities and equity</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Technical equipment</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">5,371.000</td><td>Capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">6,003.000</td></tr><tr><td>− Accumulated depreciation</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">(294.550)</td><td>Loss of 2016</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">(340.000)</td></tr><tr><td>Licences (net)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">164.800</td><td>Net income 2017</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">31.675</td></tr><tr><td>Financial shares</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">636.000</td><td><strong>Equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>5,694.675</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Non-current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>5,877.250</strong></td><td>Bank loan (due 2019)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2,000.000</td></tr><tr><td>Inventory</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">340.000</td><td><strong>Long-term liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>2,000.000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Accounts receivable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">164.000</td><td>Suppliers</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">120.000</td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,627.000</td><td>Other creditors (administration)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">180.000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>2,131.000</strong></td><td>Taxes payable (Q1 2018)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">13.575</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td><strong>Current liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>313.575</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>8,008.250</strong></td><td><strong>Total liabilities and equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>8,008.250</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "c", "points": 0.5, "prompt_html": "<p>Which items change if a 20% dividend is paid at 24:00 on 31/12/2017?</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Cash leaves the company and the profit kept in equity falls by the same amount.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Dividend 6.335 m.u.: cash and equity −6.335; total 8,001.915 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 9. Dividend paid at 24:00 on 31/12/2017 (requirement 3)</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Dividend} = 0.20 \\times 31.675 = 6.335 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<p>Paying it at 24:00 means cash leaves the company and the profit kept in equity falls by the same amount:</p>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Before</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Change</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">After</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,627.000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−6.335</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,620.665</td></tr><tr><td>Equity (net income retained)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">5,694.675</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−6.335</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">5,688.340</td></tr><tr><td>Total assets = total liabilities and equity</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">8,008.250</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−6.335</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">8,001.915</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "d", "points": 0.5, "prompt_html": "<p>And if the dividend is decided in 2017 but paid on 30/06/2018?</p>", "hint_html": "<p>The dividend is owed but not paid: what appears among the liabilities?</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Equity −6.335; dividends payable +6.335 (current); total unchanged at 8,008.25 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 10. Dividend decided in 2017, paid on 30/06/2018 (requirement 4)</h4>\n<p>The dividend is already owed to the shareholders at 31/12/2017 but no cash has left: equity falls and a <strong>current liability</strong> (dividends payable, due within the year) appears.</p>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Before</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Change</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">After</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Equity</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">5,694.675</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−6.335</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">5,688.340</td></tr><tr><td>Dividends payable (current liability)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+6.335</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">6.335</td></tr><tr><td>Current liabilities</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">313.575</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+6.335</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">319.910</td></tr><tr><td>Cash and total assets</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">unchanged</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">8,008.250</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}], "interpretation_html": "<ul><li><strong>Balance:</strong> $5{,}877.25 + 2{,}131 = 8{,}008.25 = 5{,}694.675 + 2{,}000 + 313.575$; requirement 3: $8{,}001.915 = 5{,}688.340 + 2{,}313.575$; requirement 4: $8{,}008.25 = 5{,}688.34 + 2{,}000 + 319.91$.</li><li><strong>Requirement 3 vs 4:</strong> both reduce equity by the same 6.335; the difference is only on the other side: in 3 an <strong>asset</strong> (cash) falls, in 4 a <strong>liability</strong> rises. A dividend is never an expense, so net income is 31.675 in all three cases.</li><li><strong>Why the company chose not to pay:</strong> after 2017 the equity is still below the capital (accumulated loss $-308.325$); retaining the profit is self-financing [Slide 23] that rebuilds the equity.</li><li><strong>Exam traps:</strong> (1) the 2016 data are only the opening balance; (2) inventory is half of the purchases; (3) the licences bought at the end of 2016 are amortised from 2017; (4) the loan due in 2019 is long-term.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Official solution, <code>T3_Additional_SOLUTIONS.pdf</code>, pp. 16-18</div><p>Requirements 1 and 2 coincide with the key: revenues 1,966; cost of goods sold 340; operating expenses 1,511; EBITDA 455; depreciation and amortisation 309.75; EBIT 145.25; interest 100; income before taxes 45.25; tax 13.575; net income 31.675; cash 1,627; total assets 8,008.25; equity 5,694.675. The key rounds to one decimal (309.8; 145.3; 45.3; 13.6; 31.7), which only shifts the last digit.</p>\n<p>The key does not answer requirements 3 and 4 (20 % dividend): it only shows \"Dividends (@0%)\", so Steps 9 and 10 above are not covered by it.</p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout-warning\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Erratum in the official solution</div><p>The key gives total liabilities of 2,325.935, but its own items add to $120 + 180 + 13.575 + 2{,}000 = 2{,}313.575$ (2,313.6 rounded), which together with equity of 5,694.7 gives the total of 8,008.3 that it does show. The accumulated depreciation mentions \"2009\"; the 26 m.u. belong to 2016.</p></div>"}, {"id": "P10", "category": "extra_practice_problem", "archetype": "narrative-case", "theme": "narrative-case", "unit": 3, "title": "T3 Additional 4: CEMED statements and a 5% prompt-payment discount", "difficulty": 3, "points": 4.0, "time_minutes": 50, "tags": ["official-exercise", "supplier-discount", "balance-sheet", "cash"], "official": true, "source": "T3_Additional_EXERCISES.pdf, Exercise 4 (official exercise, solved in Problem - T3 Extra 4 - Cemed Mediterranean Pottery Manufacturing and Supplier Discount)", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank P10 - T3 Extra 4 Cemed Statements and Supplier Discount.md", "page": "subjects/business-management/problemas/topic-3-extra-exam-problems.html#problem-4", "statement_html": "<p>CERÁMICAS DEL MEDITERRÁNEO (CEMED) manufactures and distributes ceramic tiles for construction in Valencia.</p>\n<ul><li>CEMED was created on 1/01/2016 by three partners, each contributing 7 m.u., fully in cash.</li><li>On 1/02/2016 it bought a piece of land worth 10 m.u., paying 50% in cash and leaving 50% to be paid one year later. On the same date it bought the factory building, paying 6 m.u. in cash and financing this amount with a mortgage at 5% annual interest for ten years. The factory is amortised over 20 years, linearly, with zero residual value. Each 31 December the company returns 10% of the mortgage principal plus the interest accrued in the year.</li><li>On 1/04/2016 it obtained an additional bank loan of 5 m.u. at 6% annual interest, returning 20% of the principal each semester for the next 2½ years (first repayment on 1/10/2016, second on 1/04/2017, and so on). Interest is paid semiannually, calculated on the amount actually due in that semester, the day after the end of the semester.</li><li>With this loan, on the same date, it bought machinery for 3 m.u. with 60 days payment delay; useful life 10 years, straight-line, zero residual value.</li><li>On 1/07/2016 it acquired 150 tons of raw material at 0.03 m.u./ton. Half of it was used in producing the products sold. 60% of the total cost is paid to the supplier at 90 days; the other 40% remains unpaid at the end of the year.</li><li>Sales revenue: 50 m.u., of which only 70% had been collected at 31/12/2016.</li><li>Personnel plus Social Security: 0.8 m.u., paid in full in 2016.</li><li>Corporation tax: 30%, settled by calendar year and paid on 15/01/2017.</li><li>The company keeps 40% of annual net profits as reserves; the dividends are paid on 31/12/2016.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Required:</strong> (1) the income statement and the balance sheet at 24:00 on 31/12/2016, explaining the intermediate calculations; (2) the changes in the income statement and the balance sheet if the company had negotiated a 5% discount on the total price of the raw materials in exchange for paying in cash the 60% agreed at 90 days. In which case is the profit higher, and why?</p>", "setup_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>Event</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Cash</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1/01</td><td>Capital $3 \\times 7 = 21$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+21</td></tr><tr><td>1/02</td><td>Land 10: 50% paid, 50% due 1/02/2017</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−5</td></tr><tr><td>1/02</td><td>Building 6, financed by a mortgage of 6</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+6 −6</td></tr><tr><td>1/04</td><td>Bank loan 5 at 6%</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+5</td></tr><tr><td>1/04</td><td>Machinery 3 bought, paid 60 days later (31/05)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−3 (on 31/05)</td></tr><tr><td>1/07</td><td>Raw material $150 \\times 0.03 = 4.5$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">—</td></tr><tr><td>29/09</td><td>60% of the raw material paid (90 days)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−2.7</td></tr><tr><td>1/10</td><td>Loan: first instalment (20% of 5) and interest of the first semester</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−1 −0.15</td></tr><tr><td>31/12</td><td>Mortgage: 10% of principal and interest of the year</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−0.6 −0.275</td></tr><tr><td>31/12</td><td>Dividends (60% of net income)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−19.3053</td></tr><tr><td>15/01/2017</td><td>Tax paid (after the closing)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">—</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Hypotheses:</strong></p>\n<ol><li>The building is used from 1/02 (11 months) and the machinery from 1/04 (9 months). Land is not depreciated.</li><li>The mortgage interest of 2016 runs from 1/02 to 31/12 (11 months) on the 6 m.u. outstanding.</li><li>Loan semesters: 1/04-30/09 (principal 5) and 1/10-31/03 (principal 4). The interest of the second semester is paid on 1/04/2017, so the part from 1/10 to 31/12 is an <strong>accrued, unpaid</strong> expense of 2016.</li><li>Results to four decimals; exact fractions were used and the last digit is rounded.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Income statement 2016</th><th>Cash 2016</th><th>Open at 31/12/2016</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Capital</td><td>—</td><td>+21</td><td>Capital 21</td></tr><tr><td>Land</td><td>—</td><td>−5</td><td>Land 10; land payable 5 (current)</td></tr><tr><td>Building / mortgage</td><td>Depreciation 0.275; interest 0.275</td><td>+6 −6 −0.6 −0.275</td><td>Building $6 - 0.275$; mortgage 5.4 (0.6 current, 4.8 long-term)</td></tr><tr><td>Bank loan</td><td>Interest $0.15 + 0.06$</td><td>+5 −1 −0.15</td><td>Loan 4 (2 current, 2 long-term); interest payable 0.06</td></tr><tr><td>Machinery</td><td>Depreciation 0.225</td><td>−3</td><td>Machinery $3 - 0.225$</td></tr><tr><td>Raw material</td><td>COGS 2.25</td><td>−2.7</td><td>Inventory 2.25; suppliers 1.8</td></tr><tr><td>Sales</td><td>Revenue 50</td><td>+35</td><td>Receivables 15</td></tr><tr><td>Personnel + SS</td><td>0.8</td><td>−0.8</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>Tax</td><td>13.7895</td><td>0</td><td>Taxes payable 13.7895</td></tr><tr><td>Dividends</td><td>— (distribution)</td><td>−19.3053</td><td>Reserves 12.8702</td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.5, "prompt_html": "<p>Prepare the income statement for 2016</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Work out depreciation, the mortgage and loan interest, the raw material consumed and the sales before building the statement.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Income from operations 46.45 m.u.</li><li>Net income 32.1755 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Depreciation</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$D_{\\text{building}} = \\frac{6 - 0}{20} \\times \\frac{11}{12} = 0.3 \\times 0.9167 = 0.2750 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$D_{\\text{machinery}} = \\frac{3 - 0}{10} \\times \\frac{9}{12} = 0.3 \\times 0.75 = 0.2250 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<p>Total depreciation: $0.275 + 0.225 = 0.5$ m.u.</p>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Mortgage</h4>\n<p>Interest of 2016 (1/02 to 31/12, principal 6):</p>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$I_{\\text{mortgage}} = 6 \\times 0.05 \\times \\frac{11}{12} = 0.2750 \\text{ m.u.} \\quad \\text{(paid on 31/12)}$$</div>\n<p>Principal returned on 31/12: $0.10 \\times 6 = 0.6$; outstanding $6 - 0.6 = 5.4$. The next 0.6, due 31/12/2017, is current; the remaining $5.4 - 0.6 = 4.8$ is long-term.</p>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 3. Bank loan</h4>\n<p>Each instalment is $0.20 \\times 5 = 1$ m.u. (five instalments: 1/10/2016, 1/04/2017, 1/10/2017, 1/04/2018, 1/10/2018).</p>\n<ul><li>First semester (1/04-30/09, principal 5): $I_1 = 5 \\times 0.06 \\times \\frac{6}{12} = 0.15$, paid on 1/10/2016 together with the first instalment.</li><li>Second semester (1/10/2016-31/03/2017, principal 4): the 2016 part is October-December, $I_2 = 4 \\times 0.06 \\times \\frac{3}{12} = 0.06$, <strong>accrued but unpaid</strong> (it will be paid on 1/04/2017).</li></ul>\n<p>Interest of the loan in 2016: $0.15 + 0.06 = 0.21$. Principal outstanding at 31/12: $5 - 1 = 4$, of which the instalments of 1/04/2017 and 1/10/2017 (2) are current and those of 2018 (2) are long-term.</p>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 4. Raw material, cost of goods sold and suppliers</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Purchase} = 150 \\times 0.03 = 4.5 \\text{ m.u.}, \\qquad \\text{COGS} = \\frac{4.5}{2} = 2.25, \\qquad \\text{Inventory} = 2.25$$</div>\n<p>Paid at 90 days: $0.60 \\times 4.5 = 2.7$; unpaid: $0.40 \\times 4.5 = 1.8$ (suppliers, current).</p>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 5. Sales</h4>\n<p>Collected $0.70 \\times 50 = 35$; receivables $50 - 35 = 15$.</p>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 6. Income statement 2016</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Computation</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sales revenue</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">50.0000</td></tr><tr><td>− Cost of goods sold</td><td>Step 4</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2.2500</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Gross profit</strong></td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>47.7500</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Personnel and Social Security</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0.8000</td></tr><tr><td>− Depreciation</td><td>Step 1</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0.5000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income from operations</strong></td><td>$47.75 - 1.3$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>46.4500</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Interest (mortgage 0.275; loan 0.21)</td><td>Steps 2-3</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0.4850</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income before taxes</strong></td><td>$46.45 - 0.485$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>45.9650</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Income taxes (30%)</td><td>$0.30 \\times 45.965$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">13.7895</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Net income</strong></td><td>$45.965 - 13.7895$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>32.1755</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Distribution: reserves $= 0.40 \\times 32.1755 = 12.8702$; dividends $= 0.60 \\times 32.1755 = 19.3053$, paid on 31/12.</p>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the cash and prepare the balance sheet at 24:00 on 31/12/2016</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Split the land debt and the loan by maturity.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Cash 28.1697 m.u.</li><li>Total 63.9197 m.u. on both sides</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 7. Cash at 24:00 on 31/12/2016</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Inflows</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th><th>Outflows</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">21.0000</td><td>Land (50%)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">5.0000</td></tr><tr><td>Mortgage</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">6.0000</td><td>Building</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">6.0000</td></tr><tr><td>Bank loan</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">5.0000</td><td>Machinery</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3.0000</td></tr><tr><td>Sales collected</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">35.0000</td><td>Raw material (60%)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2.7000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Personnel</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0.8000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Mortgage principal + interest</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0.8750</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Loan instalment + interest</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.1500</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Dividends</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">19.3053</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>67.0000</strong></td><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>38.8303</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Cash} = 67 - 38.8303 = 28.1697 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 8. Balance sheet at 24:00 on 31/12/2016</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Assets</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th><th>Liabilities and equity</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Land</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">10.0000</td><td>Capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">21.0000</td></tr><tr><td>Building ($6 - 0.275$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">5.7250</td><td>Reserves (40% of net income)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">12.8702</td></tr><tr><td>Machinery ($3 - 0.225$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2.7750</td><td><strong>Equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>33.8702</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Non-current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>18.5000</strong></td><td>Mortgage, long-term part</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">4.8000</td></tr><tr><td>Inventory (raw material)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2.2500</td><td>Bank loan, long-term part</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2.0000</td></tr><tr><td>Accounts receivable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">15.0000</td><td><strong>Long-term liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>6.8000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">28.1697</td><td>Land payable (1/02/2017)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">5.0000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>45.4197</strong></td><td>Mortgage, current part</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0.6000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Bank loan, current part</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2.0000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Interest payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0.0600</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Suppliers</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.8000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Taxes payable (15/01/2017)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">13.7895</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td><strong>Current liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>23.2495</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>63.9197</strong></td><td><strong>Total liabilities and equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>63.9197</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "c", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Changes if the company had obtained a 5% discount for paying 60% in cash; in which case is the profit higher?</p>", "hint_html": "<p>A lower purchase price lowers the cost of the material consumed; the unconsumed part stays in inventory at the lower price.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Net income 32.2543 m.u. (+0.0788, the after-tax saving)</li><li>Total 63.8950 m.u.; cash 28.2575 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 9. New purchase price and payments</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Price} = 4.5 \\times (1 - 0.05) = 4.275 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<ul><li>Paid in cash on 1/07: $0.60 \\times 4.275 = 2.565$ (instead of 2.7 at 90 days).</li><li>Unpaid at 31/12: $0.40 \\times 4.275 = 1.71$ (instead of 1.8).</li><li>Consumed (half): $\\text{COGS} = 4.275 / 2 = 2.1375$; inventory 2.1375 (both instead of 2.25).</li></ul>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 10. New income statement</h4>\n<p>Only the cost of goods sold changes before taxes: $\\Delta\\text{COGS} = 2.1375 - 2.25 = -0.1125$.</p>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Line</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Requirement 1</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Requirement 2</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Change</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cost of goods sold</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2.2500</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2.1375</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−0.1125</td></tr><tr><td>Income before taxes</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">45.9650</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">46.0775</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+0.1125</td></tr><tr><td>Income taxes (30%)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">13.7895</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">13.8233</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+0.0338</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Net income</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>32.1755</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>32.2543</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>+0.0788</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Dividends (60%)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">19.3053</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">19.3526</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+0.0473</td></tr><tr><td>Reserves (40%)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">12.8702</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">12.9017</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+0.0315</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The net change is the after-tax saving: $0.1125 \\times (1 - 0.30) = 0.07875$.</p>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 11. New balance sheet items</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Change</th><th>Reason</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Inventory</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−0.1125</td><td>Half of the material is still in stock, valued at the lower price</td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+0.0878</td><td>Pays 2.565 instead of 2.7 (+0.135) but pays 0.0473 more dividends</td></tr><tr><td>Suppliers</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−0.0900</td><td>40% of a lower price (1.71 instead of 1.8)</td></tr><tr><td>Taxes payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+0.0338</td><td>Higher profit</td></tr><tr><td>Reserves</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+0.0315</td><td>40% of the higher net income</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>−0.0248</strong></td><td>$-0.1125 + 0.08775$</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total liabilities and equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>−0.0248</strong></td><td>$-0.09 + 0.03375 + 0.0315$</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>New totals: $63.9197 - 0.0248 = 63.8950$ on both sides (cash 28.2575).</p>"}], "interpretation_html": "<ul><li><strong>Balance:</strong> $18.5 + 45.4197 = 63.9197 = 33.8702 + 6.8 + 23.2495$; requirement 2: both sides fall by 0.02475.</li><li><strong>In which case is the profit higher?</strong> With the <strong>discount</strong>. The discount lowers the cost of the material; the price paid for it is giving up 90 days of <strong>trade credit</strong> [Slide 35], and trade credit has no explicit interest in this problem, so losing it costs nothing in the income statement: it only advances a payment that in both cases falls inside 2016.</li><li><strong>Why only half of the saving reaches the profit:</strong> only half of the material was consumed. The other half of the saving is in the lower value of the inventory and will reach the income statement when that stock is used.</li><li><strong>Debt structure</strong> [Slide 36]: liabilities $6.8 + 23.2495 = 30.0495$, equity 33.8702, so $L/E = 0.89$.</li><li><strong>Exam traps:</strong> (1) the mortgage finances the building: cash +6 and −6 on the same day; (2) the loan interest of October-December is accrued but unpaid; (3) the current part of each loan is what is due before 31/12/2017; (4) the land is not depreciated; (5) taxes are payable on 15/01/2017, so they are a liability, while dividends were paid.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Official solution, <code>T3_Additional_SOLUTIONS.pdf</code>, pp. 19-25</div><p>Requirement 1 coincides with the key: cost of goods sold 2.25; personnel 0.8; depreciation 0.5; EBIT 46.45; interest 0.485; income before taxes 45.965; tax 13.79; net income 32.18; dividends 19.31; reserves 12.87; cash 28.17; total 63.92. Requirement 2 also coincides: cost of goods sold 2.14 (2.1375, rounded in the key); net income 32.25; dividends 19.35; cash 28.26; suppliers 1.71; total 63.89. The key's figures show net income rising from 32.18 to 32.25, but the key states no conclusion to the question \"in which case are profits higher?\"; the reasoning in Phase 4 is ours.</p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout-warning\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Discrepancy with the official solution (current part of the debt)</div><p>The key leaves the whole bank loan (4.0) and the whole mortgage (5.4) as long-term debt, so its current liabilities are 20.65 and its long-term liabilities 9.4. Here the instalments due within twelve months of 31/12/2016 are shown as current: 2.0 of the loan (1/04/2017 and 1/10/2017) and 0.6 of the mortgage (31/12/2017), following the definition of Slide 16. Current liabilities are therefore 23.25 and long-term liabilities 6.8; total liabilities (30.05), equity and total assets are the same.</p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout-warning\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Differences in the wording of the key</div><p>The key's statement dates the company in 2010, while the official sheet in <code>sources/</code> says 2016, which is the year used here. In the key's part 2 the interest formula writes 0.85 where 0.80 is meant (the loan balance of 4 over 5); its value, 0.06, is unchanged.</p></div>"}, {"id": "P11", "category": "extra_practice_problem", "archetype": "reverse-engineering-ratios", "theme": "reverse-engineering-ratios", "unit": 3, "title": "FRIGOLEV: an incomplete 2017 balance, a cooperative discount and the 2018 statements", "difficulty": 3, "points": 4.0, "time_minutes": 60, "tags": ["extra-practice", "exam-style", "accrual-vs-cash", "bad-debts", "capital-increase", "roa", "roe"], "official": false, "source": "Exam-style practice exercise supplied by the student and solved for the problem bank (not an official exercise), in the style of T3_Additional_EXERCISES.pdf", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank P11 - FRIGOLEV Incomplete Balance and Cooperative Discount.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>The newly appointed financial director of <strong>LOGÍSTICA FRIGORÍFICA DEL LEVANTE S.L. (FRIGOLEV)</strong>, a company located in the port area of Castellón and dedicated to the manufacturing of isothermal containers and cold-chain storage equipment for agricultural exporters, is working on the closing of its financial statements (balance sheet and Income Statement) as of 31 December 2018 (31/12/2018).</p>\n<p>The company was originally incorporated on 1 January 2016 (01/01/2016) by four partners, with an initial contribution of each of 1.500 m.u. disbursed fully in cash at that time, and no further capital modifications took place until fiscal year 2018. Due to the sudden departure of the previous chief accountant, the financial director only has an incomplete balance of the company as of 31 December 2017 (31/12/2017), which is included below with certain missing figures marked as (?), along with the audit notes detailing the operations that have taken place during the period from 1 January 2018 (01/01/2018) to 31 December 2018 (31/12/2018):</p>\n<p><strong>I.</strong> On 31 December 2017 (31/12/2017), the company had as depreciable fixed assets only the industrial refrigeration chambers purchased on a certain date during 2016, which have a useful life of 10 years and are amortized linearly with a residual value of 10% of their acquisition cost. In addition, the balance sheet at 31 December 2017 (31/12/2017) reflected an industrial plot of land acquired in 2016 and financial shares of other companies acquired as a permanent investment.</p>\n<p><strong>II.</strong> During the year 2018, the company has sold 3.600 units of isothermal containers at a catalog price of 25 m.u. / unit (every month the same amount, 300 u.). The general payment condition agreed with the customers implies that this will occur 60 days after the sale. However, a very important agricultural cooperative (representing 25% of the physical units sold each month), which has bought an equal amount every month, has been given a discount of 8% on the catalog price of their purchases in exchange for paying strictly in cash in the same month of the sale.</p>\n<p><strong>III.</strong> At 31 December 2017 (31/12/2017) there was a stock (stock in stock) of 400 tons of insulating raw material valued at 2,00 m.u. / ton. On 1 April 2018 (01/04/2018), the company purchased 5.000 tons of raw material at a cost of 2,00 m.u. / ton. The payment condition agreed with the suppliers was 40% paid in cash on the date of purchase, 30% payable to 90 days, and the remaining 30% deferred until 1 February 2019 (01/02/2019). At 31 December 2018 (31/12/2018), after conducting the warehouse count, there remains a final stock (stock in stock) of 900 tons.</p>\n<p><strong>IV.</strong> During the first quarter of 2018, the unpaid taxes and the outstanding supplier balances from the previous year were paid in full. Regarding the customer debt outstanding from 31 December 2017 (31/12/2017), 75% was charged in cash during the first semester of 2018, except 200 m.u. from a customer who requested and was granted a deferral of payment until 15 March 2019 (15/03/2019), and 100 m.u. corresponding to a client declared definitively insolvent on 1 June 2018 (01/06/2018), which must be recorded as an unrecoverable bad debt loss for the period.</p>\n<p><strong>V.</strong> Fixed personnel expenses have been 2.200 m.u. / month (to which 600 m.u. / month must be added for Social Security) from 1 January 2018 (01/01/2018). To respond to seasonal peaks in container assembly, production staff also received variable wages that amounted in 2018 to 3.620 m.u. Fixed and variable wages are paid in the month in which they are produced, whereas Social Security contributions (starting from January 2018) are paid on the 15th day of the following month. General expenses and utilities (electricity, phone, insurance, etc.) amounted to 1.100 m.u. / month and are paid two months after being made.</p>\n<p><strong>VI.</strong> The company carried out a renting contract on 1 March 2018 (01/03/2018) (with a duration of 2 years) with a specialized company for electric warehouse forklifts, for a value of 350 m.u. monthly. The contract establishes a condition of payment to 90 days (payment at the end of each month).</p>\n<p><strong>VII.</strong> On 1 April 2018 (01/04/2018), the company made a capital increase by issuing 1.000 new shares of 2,00 m.u. nominal value each, fully disbursed in cash and with an issue premium of 50% over the nominal value. On the same date, 1 April 2018 (01/04/2018), it bought two refrigerated delivery vehicles that cost a total of 4.800 m.u., of which it paid 50% in cash, postponing the payment of the remainder in two equal installments: half on 1 October 2018 (01/10/2018) and the other half on 1 April 2019 (01/04/2019). These vehicles will be amortized linearly in 6 years with a total residual value of 480 m.u. Furthermore, on 1 October 2018 (01/10/2018), it bought a software license for cold-chain tracking for a value of 1.600 m.u., payable to 120 days, which is amortized linearly in 4 years with residual value zero.</p>\n<p><strong>VIII.</strong> The long-term bank loan appearing on the balance sheet at 31 December 2017 (31/12/2017) was granted on 1 January 2017 (01/01/2017) at an annual interest rate of 6%. Each 31 December, the company must pay the accrued annual interest calculated from the loan value at the beginning of each year, and starting on 31 December 2018 (31/12/2018) it must also return to the bank an annual principal amount of 500 m.u. until completing the repayment on 31 December 2021 (31/12/2021). Additionally, on 1 July 2018 (01/07/2018), the company obtained a new short-term bank credit of 1.500 m.u. at 8% annual interest, agreeing to return 50% of the principal each semester for the next year. Both the principal installment and the semiannual interest calculated on the amount actually due in that semester will be paid the day after the end of each semester (i.e., on 1 January 2019 (01/01/2019) and 1 July 2019 (01/07/2019)).</p>\n<p><strong>IX.</strong> The financial results of the company are declared at 23:59 hrs on 31 December 2018 (31/12/2018). The applicable corporation tax rate is 25% on the Profits obtained, which is settled by calendar year and will be paid to the Treasury on 20 January 2019 (20/01/2019) (counted in the balance at 31 December 2018 (31/12/2018) as \"unpaid taxes\"). From the net profit after taxes, the company first offsets in full the losses from previous years, and of the remaining net profit, it retains 60% as reserves and distributes the other 40% as dividends to the shareholders, which are paid in cash before carrying out the final balance at 24:00 on 31 December 2018 (31/12/2018).</p>\n<p><strong>BALANCE 31 December 2017 (31/12/2017)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>ASSETS</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th><th>LIABILITIES</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>CURRENT ASSETS</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>2.940</strong></td><td><strong>CURRENT LIABILITIES</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>900</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">(?)</td><td>Unpaid taxes</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">250</td></tr><tr><td>Accounts receivable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.200</td><td>Accounts payable (suppliers)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">650</td></tr><tr><td>Inventory (stock)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">800</td><td><strong>NON-CURRENT LIABILITIES</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>2.000</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>NON-CURRENT ASSETS</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>5.560</strong></td><td>Long-term bank loan</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2.000</td></tr><tr><td>Land</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.500</td><td><strong>OWNERS EQUITY</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>(?)</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Refrigeration chambers (Equipment)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">4.000</td><td>Paid-in capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">(?)</td></tr><tr><td>Accum. depreciation</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">(540)</td><td>Losses from previous years</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">(400)</td></tr><tr><td>Financial shares</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">600</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total Assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>(?)</strong></td><td><strong>Total Liabilities + OE</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>(?)</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Required:</strong></p>\n<ol><li>Determine and justify the missing figures (?) in the balance sheet as of 31 December 2017 (31/12/2017) as well as the exact date in 2016 on which the industrial refrigeration chambers were purchased, and construct the Income Statement (Profit and Loss Account) of FRIGOLEV for the period ending 31 December 2018 (31/12/2018).</li><li>Assuming that the company has offset the prior losses and paid the dividends as indicated, construct and justify the balance sheet at 24:00 on 31 December 2018 (31/12/2018), detailing all intermediate calculations.</li><li>Determine the values of the ROA and ROE ratios in 2018, and indicate which items of the balance sheet at 24:00 on 31 December 2018 (31/12/2018) would be affected, and the exact amount of the effect, if the decision was made before the end of 2018 to distribute the same dividend, but to pay it on 15 April 2019 (15/04/2019).</li><li>State and explain what changes would occur on the various items of the Income Statement and the balance sheet at 31 December 2018 (31/12/2018) if the company had eliminated the 8% discount granted to the agricultural cooperative, subjecting this customer to the general 60-day payment condition at the full price of 25 m.u. / unit, which in turn would have caused the cooperative to reduce the physical units bought each month by 10%. In which case is the net profit higher, and in which case is the final cash balance higher? Discuss reasoned, why.</li></ol>", "setup_html": "<p><strong>Hypotheses</strong> (readings of the statement that the solution depends on):</p>\n<ol><li>Item IV: 75% of the 1.200 m.u. (900 m.u.) is collected; the remaining 25% (300 m.u.) is exactly the 200 m.u. deferred plus the 100 m.u. written off.</li><li>Item IX: the 25% tax is applied to the whole profit before taxes of 2018; the losses of previous years are offset afterwards, against the net profit, as the statement orders.</li><li>Item VI: \"payable to 90 days (payment at the end of each month)\" means each month's fee is paid at the end of the third month after it (March is paid at the end of June); October, November and December are unpaid at 31/12/2018.</li><li>Requirement 4: the containers consume raw material in proportion to the units sold, and no finished containers are kept in stock.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"callout callout-warning\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Inconsistency in the given balance</div><p>Item VIII says that 500 m.u. of the loan are repaid on 31/12/2018, so at 31/12/2017 those 500 m.u. were due within one year and should have been a current liability (current liabilities 1.400, non-current 1.500). The totals do not change, so the solution keeps the given balance as stated and splits the loan correctly at 31/12/2018.</p></div>\n<p><strong>Timeline of the cash-relevant dates in 2018</strong></p>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>Event</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Q1 2018</td><td>Unpaid taxes 250 and suppliers 650 of 2017 paid</td></tr><tr><td>H1 2018</td><td>900 of the 2017 receivables collected; 100 written off on 01/06</td></tr><tr><td>01/04/2018</td><td>Raw material 10.000 (4.000 cash); capital increase 3.000; vehicles 4.800 (2.400 cash)</td></tr><tr><td>01/07/2018</td><td>Second 30% of raw material paid (3.000); short-term credit received 1.500</td></tr><tr><td>01/10/2018</td><td>Second vehicle instalment 1.200; software 1.600 bought payable to 120 days</td></tr><tr><td>31/12/2018</td><td>Loan interest 120 and principal 500 paid; tax accrued; dividends paid at 24:00</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Extension (not defined in the course decks).</strong> $\\text{ROA} = \\text{Income from operations}/\\text{Total assets}$ and $\\text{ROE} = \\text{Net income}/\\text{Owners equity}$, both on the balance at 24:00 on 31/12/2018, as in the solved INFOPYMES exercise.</p>", "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Find the missing figures of the 2017 balance and the purchase date of the chambers, and prepare the 2018 Income Statement</p>", "hint_html": "<p>The current-assets subtotal and the four partners' contributions close the 2017 balance. For the date: how many years of depreciation are inside 540 m.u.? For 2018, separate what is consumed (raw material, the cooperative discount, the bad debt) from what is paid.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Cash 940; total 8.500; paid-in capital 6.000; owners equity 5.600 m.u.</li><li>Chambers depreciate 360 m.u./year: bought on 1 July 2016 (01/07/2016).</li><li>Revenue 88.200; raw material consumed 9.000; depreciation 1.000 m.u.</li><li>Income from operations 24.180; financial expenses 180; net profit 18.000 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. The missing figures of 31/12/2017</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Cash} = 2.940 - 1.200 - 800 = 940 \\text{ m.u.}, \\qquad \\text{Total assets} = 2.940 + 5.560 = 8.500 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<p>Check of the non-current block: $1.500 + 4.000 - 540 + 600 = 5.560$ ✓.</p>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Paid-in capital} = 4 \\times 1.500 = 6.000, \\qquad \\text{Owners equity} = 6.000 - 400 = 5.600 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Total liabilities + OE} = 900 + 2.000 + 5.600 = 8.500 \\text{ m.u.} \\;\\checkmark$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Purchase date of the refrigeration chambers</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$D_{\\text{year}} = \\frac{4.000 - 0{,}10 \\times 4.000}{10} = \\frac{3.600}{10} = 360 \\text{ m.u./year}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\frac{540}{360} = 1{,}5 \\text{ years before } 31/12/2017 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\text{bought on 1 July 2016 (01/07/2016)}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 3. Revenue and its collection (item II)</h4>\n<p>Each month 300 units are sold: 75 to the cooperative (25%) at $25 \\times 0{,}92 = 23$ m.u. and 225 to the other customers at 25 m.u.</p>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Cooperative} = 75 \\times 23 = 1.725 \\text{ m.u./month}, \\qquad \\text{Others} = 225 \\times 25 = 5.625 \\text{ m.u./month}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Revenue 2018} = 12 \\times (1.725 + 5.625) = 12 \\times 7.350 = 88.200 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<p>The cooperative pays in the same month (20.700 collected); the others pay at 60 days, so November and December are pending: $2 \\times 5.625 = 11.250$ m.u. receivable.</p>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 4. Raw material (item III)</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Consumption} = 800 + 5.000 \\times 2{,}00 - 900 \\times 2{,}00 = 800 + 10.000 - 1.800 = 9.000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<p>Payments: 40% on 01/04 (4.000) and 30% at 90 days, on 01/07 (3.000); the last 30% (3.000) is owed until 01/02/2019.</p>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 5. Personnel, general expenses and renting (items V, VI)</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Personnel} = 12 \\times 2.200 + 12 \\times 600 + 3.620 = 26.400 + 7.200 + 3.620 = 37.220 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<ul><li>Social Security of December (600) is paid on 15/01/2019; 11 months paid (6.600).</li><li>General expenses: $12 \\times 1.100 = 13.200$ m.u. of expense; paid two months later, so November and December (2.200) are owed and 11.000 paid.</li><li>Renting: March to December, $10 \\times 350 = 3.500$ m.u. of expense; March to September paid ($7 \\times 350 = 2.450$), October to December owed (1.050).</li></ul>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 6. Depreciation and amortization (items I, VII)</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$D_{\\text{chambers}} = 360, \\qquad D_{\\text{vehicles}} = \\frac{4.800 - 480}{6} \\times \\frac{9}{12} = 720 \\times \\frac{9}{12} = 540, \\qquad D_{\\text{software}} = \\frac{1.600}{4} \\times \\frac{3}{12} = 100$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$D_{2018} = 360 + 540 + 100 = 1.000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 7. Financial expenses (item VIII)</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$I_{\\text{loan}} = 0{,}06 \\times 2.000 = 120 \\text{ m.u. (paid 31/12/2018)}, \\qquad I_{\\text{credit}} = 1.500 \\times 0{,}08 \\times \\frac{6}{12} = 60 \\text{ m.u. (paid 01/01/2019)}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 8. Income Statement 2018</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Computation</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sales revenue</td><td>Step 3</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">88.200</td></tr><tr><td>− Raw material consumed</td><td>Step 4</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">9.000</td></tr><tr><td>− Personnel (wages and Social Security)</td><td>Step 5</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">37.220</td></tr><tr><td>− General expenses and utilities</td><td>$12 \\times 1.100$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">13.200</td></tr><tr><td>− Renting of forklifts</td><td>$10 \\times 350$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3.500</td></tr><tr><td>− Bad debt loss</td><td>item IV</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">100</td></tr><tr><td>− Depreciation and amortization</td><td>Step 6</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income from operations</strong></td><td>$88.200 - 64.020$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>24.180</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Financial expenses</td><td>$120 + 60$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">180</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Profit before taxes</strong></td><td>$24.180 - 180$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>24.000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Corporation tax (25%)</td><td>$0{,}25 \\times 24.000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">6.000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Net profit</strong></td><td>$24.000 - 6.000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>18.000</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Prepare the balance sheet at 24:00 on 31 December 2018</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Distribute first: offset the 400 of losses, then 60/40. Build the cash account from the 940 of 2017, and list every amount \"still open\" at 31/12 as a receivable or a payable.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Remaining profit 17.600; reserves 10.560; dividends 7.040 m.u.</li><li>Cash at 24:00 on 31/12/2018: 14.060 m.u.</li><li>Owners equity 19.560; current liabilities 17.710; total 38.270 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Distribution of the profit [Slide 23]</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Remaining profit} = 18.000 - 400 = 17.600, \\qquad \\text{Reserves} = 0{,}60 \\times 17.600 = 10.560, \\qquad \\text{Dividends} = 0{,}40 \\times 17.600 = 7.040 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Capital increase (item VII)</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$1.000 \\times 2{,}00 = 2.000 \\text{ (capital)}, \\qquad 0{,}50 \\times 2.000 = 1.000 \\text{ (issue premium)}, \\qquad \\text{cash in} = 3.000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 3. Cash account 2018</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Inflows</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th><th>Outflows</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cooperative sales (cash)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">20.700</td><td>Unpaid taxes 2017</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">250</td></tr><tr><td>Other customers, January–October ($10 \\times 5.625$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">56.250</td><td>Suppliers 2017</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">650</td></tr><tr><td>Receivables of 2017 (75%)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">900</td><td>Raw material ($4.000 + 3.000$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">7.000</td></tr><tr><td>Capital increase</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3.000</td><td>Fixed wages</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">26.400</td></tr><tr><td>Short-term bank credit</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.500</td><td>Variable wages</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3.620</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Social Security (11 months)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">6.600</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>General expenses (10 months)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">11.000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Renting (7 months)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2.450</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Vehicles ($2.400 + 1.200$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3.600</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Loan interest</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">120</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Loan principal</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">500</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Dividends</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">7.040</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>82.350</strong></td><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>69.230</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Cash}_{24:00,\\,31/12/2018} = 940 + 82.350 - 69.230 = 14.060 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 4. Balance sheet at 24:00 on 31/12/2018</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Assets</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th><th>Liabilities and owners equity</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Land</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.500</td><td>Paid-in capital ($6.000 + 2.000$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">8.000</td></tr><tr><td>Refrigeration chambers ($4.000 - 900$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3.100</td><td>Issue premium</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.000</td></tr><tr><td>Vehicles ($4.800 - 540$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">4.260</td><td>Reserves</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">10.560</td></tr><tr><td>Software license ($1.600 - 100$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.500</td><td>Losses from previous years (offset)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0</td></tr><tr><td>Financial shares</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">600</td><td><strong>Owners equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>19.560</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Non-current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>10.960</strong></td><td>Long-term bank loan (2020–2021)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.000</td></tr><tr><td>Inventory (900 t × 2,00)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.800</td><td><strong>Non-current liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>1.000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Accounts receivable ($11.250 + 200$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">11.450</td><td>Bank loan, instalment 31/12/2019</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">500</td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">14.060</td><td>Short-term bank credit</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.500</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Interest payable (credit)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">60</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Suppliers of raw material</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3.000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Suppliers of vehicles</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.200</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Supplier of software</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.600</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Social Security payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">600</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>General expenses payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2.200</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Renting payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.050</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Unpaid taxes</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">6.000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>27.310</strong></td><td><strong>Current liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>17.710</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>38.270</strong></td><td><strong>Total liabilities and equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>38.270</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Accumulated depreciation of the chambers: $540 + 360 = 900$. The 200 m.u. deferred to 15/03/2019 is due within one year, so it stays current; the loan splits into the 500 due on 31/12/2019 (current) and 1.000 due in 2020–2021.</p>"}, {"label": "c", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute ROA and ROE of 2018, and the effect of paying the same dividend on 15 April 2019</p>", "hint_html": "<p>A dividend decided but not paid is owed to the shareholders: which line appears, and which line no longer goes down?</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>ROA = 63,18%; ROE = 92,02% (extension).</li><li>Paid on 15/04/2019: cash +7.040 (21.100); new current liability \"dividends payable\" 7.040; totals 45.310 m.u.; owners equity unchanged.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Ratios (extension)</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{ROA} = \\frac{24.180}{38.270} = 63{,}18\\%, \\qquad \\text{ROE} = \\frac{18.000}{19.560} = 92{,}02\\%$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Dividend decided in 2018, paid on 15/04/2019</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Paid at 24:00</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Paid 15/04/2019</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Change</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">14.060</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">21.100</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+7.040</td></tr><tr><td>Dividends payable (current liability)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">7.040</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+7.040</td></tr><tr><td>Reserves and owners equity</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">19.560</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">19.560</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0</td></tr><tr><td>Total assets = total liabilities and equity</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">38.270</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">45.310</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+7.040</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The decision already reduces the profit kept in the company, so equity does not change; only the moment the money leaves changes.</p>"}, {"label": "d", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Effect of eliminating the 8% discount to the cooperative; which case gives more profit and which more cash?</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Recompute only what the cooperative touches: its units (−10%), its price (25), its collection (60 days), the raw material its containers consume, and then tax, distribution and cash.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Cooperative: 67,5 u./month at 25 m.u. = 1.687,5 m.u./month; revenue 87.750 (−450).</li><li>Raw material consumed −225 (90 fewer units × 2,50); net profit 17.831,25 (−168,75).</li><li>Receivables +3.375; inventory +225; cash 10.302,50 (−3.757,50); total assets 38.112,50.</li><li>Profit and cash are both higher with the discount.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. New sales of the cooperative</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$75 \\times 0{,}90 = 67{,}5 \\text{ u./month}, \\qquad 67{,}5 \\times 25 = 1.687{,}5 \\text{ m.u./month}, \\qquad 12 \\times 1.687{,}5 = 20.250 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\Delta\\text{Revenue} = 20.250 - 20.700 = -450 \\text{ m.u.} \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\text{Revenue} = 87.750 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Raw material (hypothesis 4)</h4>\n<p>In the base case 4.500 tons were consumed for 3.600 units: $4.500 / 3.600 = 1{,}25$ t/unit $= 2{,}50$ m.u./unit. Selling $12 \\times 7{,}5 = 90$ units fewer:</p>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\Delta\\text{Consumption} = -90 \\times 2{,}50 = -225 \\text{ m.u.} \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\text{final stock} = 1.800 + 225 = 2.025 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 3. New results</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Profit before taxes} = 24.000 - 450 + 225 = 23.775, \\qquad \\text{Tax} = 0{,}25 \\times 23.775 = 5.943{,}75$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Net profit} = 23.775 - 5.943{,}75 = 17.831{,}25, \\qquad \\text{Remaining} = 17.431{,}25$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Reserves} = 0{,}60 \\times 17.431{,}25 = 10.458{,}75, \\qquad \\text{Dividends} = 0{,}40 \\times 17.431{,}25 = 6.972{,}50$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 4. New cash</h4>\n<p>The cooperative now pays at 60 days: January to October collected, $10 \\times 1.687{,}5 = 16.875$ instead of 20.700 (−3.825). The dividend paid falls by 67,50; the tax is not paid in 2018.</p>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Cash} = 14.060 - 3.825 + 67{,}50 = 10.302{,}50 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 5. Changes in the statements</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Base</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Without discount</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Change</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Revenue</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">88.200</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">87.750</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−450</td></tr><tr><td>Raw material consumed</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">9.000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">8.775</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−225</td></tr><tr><td>Net profit</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">18.000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">17.831,25</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−168,75</td></tr><tr><td>Inventory</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.800</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2.025</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+225</td></tr><tr><td>Accounts receivable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">11.450</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">14.825</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+3.375</td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">14.060</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">10.302,50</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−3.757,50</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">38.270</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">38.112,50</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−157,50</td></tr><tr><td>Reserves</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">10.560</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">10.458,75</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−101,25</td></tr><tr><td>Unpaid taxes</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">6.000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">5.943,75</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−56,25</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total liabilities and equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">38.270</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">38.112,50</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−157,50</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Discussion.</strong> The <strong>profit is higher with the discount</strong> (18.000 against 17.831,25): removing the discount gains $2$ m.u. on each of the 810 units the cooperative still buys ($810 \\times 2 = 1.620$ m.u.), but the 90 units it stops buying carried a margin over raw material of $23 - 2{,}50 = 20{,}50$ m.u. each ($90 \\times 20{,}50 = 1.845$ m.u.); the net effect before taxes is $1.620 - 1.845 = -225$ m.u. The <strong>cash is also higher with the discount</strong> (14.060 against 10.302,50): the cooperative pays in the same month, while under the general condition November and December stay as receivables. Keeping the discount wins on both counts.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": "<ul><li><strong>Dimensional check:</strong> units × m.u./unit and tons × m.u./ton give m.u.; m.u./year × months/12 gives m.u.; ratios are dimensionless.</li><li><strong>Why ROA and ROE are so high:</strong> the company's annual sales (88.200 m.u.) are ten times its 2017 assets; the ratios are measured on the year-end balance, which already includes most of the year's profit.</li><li><strong>Accrual vs cash:</strong> net profit 18.000 m.u., but cash grew by only 13.120 m.u. because 11.250 m.u. of sales are uncollected, although many expenses (tax, software, raw material, renting) are still unpaid.</li><li><strong>Typical mistakes:</strong> depreciating the vehicles for 12 months, forgetting the residual values, treating the issue premium as capital, and putting the whole short-term credit or the 200 m.u. deferred receivable outside current items.</li></ul>"}, {"id": "P12", "category": "extra_practice_problem", "archetype": "year-on-year-cash", "theme": "year-on-year-cash", "unit": 3, "title": "AGRODRON: the second year of a drone start-up that closed its first year with a loss", "difficulty": 2, "points": 4.0, "time_minutes": 50, "tags": ["extra-practice", "exam-style", "accrual-vs-cash", "losses-carried-forward", "depreciation", "roa", "roe"], "official": false, "source": "Exam-style practice exercise supplied by the student and solved for the problem bank (not an official exercise), in the style of T3_Additional_EXERCISES.pdf, Exercise 2", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank P12 - AGRODRON Second Year after a Start-Up Loss.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>The company <strong>AGRODRON LEVANTE S.L.</strong> was incorporated on 1 January 2017 (01/01/2017) by a group of agricultural engineers with a registered paid-in capital of 4.500 m.u., having an objective of providing precision crop-monitoring services by drone and distributing organic fertilizers in the region of Murcia.</p>\n<p><strong>The following information applies to fiscal year 2017:</strong></p>\n<p>To finance the operations of the company, on 1 January 2017 (01/01/2017) a bank granted the company a loan of 1.500 m.u. at 6% annual interest, with a duration of 5 years, i.e. until 31 December 2021 (31/12/2021). Each 31 December, the company must return to the bank an equal principal amount of 300 m.u. and should also pay the due annual interest (6%) calculated from the loan value at the beginning of each year.</p>\n<p>During 2017, the company invested in a fleet of agricultural drones worth 2.400 m.u. The useful life of the drones purchased was 8 years (amortized linearly, with no residual value) and at the end of 2017 the company recorded in accumulated depreciation an amount of 75 m.u. for those assets. Also, a further investment of 400 m.u. in mapping software licenses was made on 31 December 2017 (31/12/2017) to process aerial images. They use these licenses for 4 years (linear amortization with residual value zero). In addition, the company spent 800 m.u. to purchase a plot of rural land for its flight testing base. Most of 2017 was devoted to obtaining civil aviation permits and only the actual business work was started in the final months of the year. At 31 December 2017 (31/12/2017), its balance sheet reflected a loss of 220 m.u., there was no stock (stock in stock) nor pending customer or supplier balances, and the cash in hand and bank account was 1.955 m.u.</p>\n<p><strong>The following information applies to fiscal year 2018:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>For aerial crop monitoring, the average number of subscribed farms during 2018 was 400 with an average revenue of 0,45 m.u. / month per client in subscription fees. Additionally, the company billed 840 m.u. for the direct sales of organic fertilizers. At the end of 2018, AGRODRON had accounts receivable of 310 m.u. from their customers.</li><li>The company purchased supplies of organic fertilizers during 2018 which amounted to 500 m.u. Of these purchases, 70% was paid immediately in cash and the payment of the remaining 30% was deferred to the following year. Year 2018 sales consumed 80% of the purchased goods.</li><li>AGRODRON operates from rented hangars that have a monthly cost (from 1 January 2018 (01/01/2018)) of 25 m.u. / month, paid in cash. Fixed salaries and Social Security costs (contribution of the company to the Social Security) of the permanent employees amounted to 60 m.u. per month (from 1 January 2018 (01/01/2018)) and were paid in full in 2018. To respond to increased demand during the harvest campaign, the company hired external pilots whose variable wages amounted in 2018 to 192 m.u. (paid in cash).</li><li>Electricity, insurance, and other administrative expenditures totaled 180 m.u. in 2018. The company postponed the payment of 40% of this amount to the following year. Furthermore, on 1 July 2018 (01/07/2018), the company acquired thermal cameras for its drones for a value of 200 m.u., paying 50% in cash on that date and leaving the remainder payable to 9 months, i.e. on 1 April 2019 (01/04/2019). This equipment has a useful life of 5 years and will be amortized linearly with a residual value of 20%.</li><li>The financial results of the company are declared at 23:59 hrs on 31 December 2018 (31/12/2018). The applicable corporation tax rate was 25% on the Profits obtained and is payable in the first quarter of 2019. In 2018, the results were positive: after offsetting in full the loss of the previous year, the company decided to distribute 30% of the remaining net profit after taxes as dividends (paid at 24:00 on 31 December 2018 (31/12/2018)) and retain the rest in the company.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Required:</strong></p>\n<ol><li>Construct the Income Statement (Profit and Loss Account) of AGRODRON LEVANTE S.L. for the period ending 31 December 2018 (31/12/2018), and deduce the exact date in 2017 on which the fleet of agricultural drones was acquired.</li><li>Construct and justify the balance sheet at 24:00 on 31 December 2018 (31/12/2018), detailing all intermediate cash and accrual calculations.</li><li>Determine the values of the ROA and ROE ratios in 2018.</li><li>Indicate which items of the balance sheet at 31 December 2018 (31/12/2018) would be affected and the exact amount of the effect if the decision was made before the end of 2018 to distribute the same dividend, but to pay it on 30 June 2019 (30/06/2019), and assuming simultaneously that the company had decided not to offset the loss of 2017 before calculating the 30% dividend payout.</li></ol>", "setup_html": "<p><strong>Hypotheses:</strong></p>\n<ol><li>The tax of 25% is applied to the whole profit before taxes of 2018; the loss of 2017 is offset afterwards, against the net profit, as the statement orders (\"after offsetting in full the loss of the previous year, ... distribute 30% of the remaining net profit after taxes\").</li><li>The software licences were bought on the last day of 2017, so their amortization starts in 2018 (a full year).</li><li>The fertilizers not consumed (20% of the purchases) stay in stock at cost.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>The balance at 31/12/2017, rebuilt from the data</strong> (needed to start 2018):</p>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Assets</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th><th>Liabilities and owners equity</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Land</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">800</td><td>Paid-in capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">4.500</td></tr><tr><td>Drones ($2.400 - 75$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2.325</td><td>Losses of 2017</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">(220)</td></tr><tr><td>Software licences</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">400</td><td><strong>Owners equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>4.280</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.955</td><td>Bank loan due 2019–2021</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">900</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Bank loan due 31/12/2018</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">300</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>5.480</strong></td><td><strong>Total liabilities and equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>5.480</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The loan was 1.500 and 300 were repaid on 31/12/2017, so 1.200 remain at the start of 2018.</p>\n<p><strong>Extension (not defined in the course decks).</strong> $\\text{ROA} = \\text{Income from operations}/\\text{Total assets}$ and $\\text{ROE} = \\text{Net income}/\\text{Owners equity}$, on the balance at 24:00 on 31/12/2018.</p>", "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Prepare the 2018 Income Statement and deduce the purchase date of the drones</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Compute the yearly depreciation of the drones: how many months of it are 75 m.u.? In 2018 only 80% of the fertilizers are an expense, and the cameras work for six months.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Drones bought on 1 October 2017 (01/10/2017).</li><li>Revenue 3.000; fertilizers consumed 400; depreciation and amortization 416 m.u.</li><li>Income from operations 792; interest 72; profit before taxes 720; tax 180; net profit 540 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Purchase date of the drones</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$D_{\\text{drones}} = \\frac{2.400 - 0}{8} = 300 \\text{ m.u./year}, \\qquad \\frac{75}{300} \\times 12 = 3 \\text{ months} \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\text{bought on 1 October 2017 (01/10/2017)}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Revenue</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Subscriptions} = 400 \\times 0{,}45 \\times 12 = 2.160, \\qquad \\text{Revenue} = 2.160 + 840 = 3.000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 3. Fertilizers consumed</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Consumed} = 0{,}80 \\times 500 = 400, \\qquad \\text{Stock at } 31/12/2018 = 500 - 400 = 100 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 4. Depreciation and amortization</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$D_{\\text{drones}} = 300, \\qquad D_{\\text{software}} = \\frac{400}{4} = 100, \\qquad D_{\\text{cameras}} = \\frac{200 - 0{,}20 \\times 200}{5} \\times \\frac{6}{12} = 32 \\times \\frac{6}{12} = 16$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$D_{2018} = 300 + 100 + 16 = 416 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 5. Interest</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$I_{2018} = 0{,}06 \\times 1.200 = 72 \\text{ m.u.} \\quad \\text{(loan value at the beginning of 2018)}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 6. Income Statement 2018</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Computation</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Subscription fees</td><td>$400 \\times 0{,}45 \\times 12$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2.160</td></tr><tr><td>Sales of fertilizers</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">840</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Revenue</strong></td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>3.000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Fertilizers consumed</td><td>Step 3</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">400</td></tr><tr><td>− Hangar rent</td><td>$12 \\times 25$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">300</td></tr><tr><td>− Fixed salaries and Social Security</td><td>$12 \\times 60$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">720</td></tr><tr><td>− Variable wages of external pilots</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">192</td></tr><tr><td>− Electricity, insurance and administration</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">180</td></tr><tr><td>− Depreciation and amortization</td><td>Step 4</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">416</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income from operations</strong></td><td>$3.000 - 2.208$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>792</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Financial expenses (interest)</td><td>Step 5</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">72</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Profit before taxes</strong></td><td>$792 - 72$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>720</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Corporation tax (25%)</td><td>$0{,}25 \\times 720$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">180</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Net profit</strong></td><td>$720 - 180$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>540</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.5, "prompt_html": "<p>Prepare the balance sheet at 24:00 on 31 December 2018</p>", "hint_html": "<p>First the distribution (offset, then 30%), then the cash account starting from 1.955. Every unpaid amount of 2018 is a current liability; the loan keeps one instalment as current.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Remaining profit 320; dividends 96; reserves 224 m.u.</li><li>Cash 2.407 m.u.</li><li>Owners equity 4.724; current liabilities 802; total 6.126 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Distribution</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Remaining profit} = 540 - 220 = 320, \\qquad \\text{Dividends} = 0{,}30 \\times 320 = 96, \\qquad \\text{Reserves} = 320 - 96 = 224 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Collections and payments</h4>\n<ul><li>Customers: $3.000 - 310 = 2.690$ collected.</li><li>Fertilizers: $0{,}70 \\times 500 = 350$ paid, $150$ owed.</li><li>Administration: $0{,}60 \\times 180 = 108$ paid, $72$ owed.</li><li>Cameras: 100 paid, 100 owed until 01/04/2019.</li><li>Loan: interest 72 and principal 300 paid on 31/12/2018; it falls to $1.200 - 300 = 900$ (300 due on 31/12/2019, 600 later).</li></ul>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 3. Cash account 2018</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Inflows</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th><th>Outflows</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Collections from customers</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2.690</td><td>Fertilizers</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">350</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Hangar rent</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">300</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Fixed salaries and Social Security</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">720</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>External pilots</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">192</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Administration (60%)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">108</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Thermal cameras (50%)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">100</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Loan interest</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">72</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Loan principal</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">300</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Dividends</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">96</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>2.690</strong></td><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>2.238</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Cash}_{24:00,\\,31/12/2018} = 1.955 + 2.690 - 2.238 = 2.407 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 4. Balance sheet at 24:00 on 31/12/2018</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Assets</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th><th>Liabilities and owners equity</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Land</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">800</td><td>Paid-in capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">4.500</td></tr><tr><td>Drones ($2.400 - 375$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2.025</td><td>Losses of 2017 (offset)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0</td></tr><tr><td>Software licences ($400 - 100$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">300</td><td>Reserves</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">224</td></tr><tr><td>Thermal cameras ($200 - 16$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">184</td><td><strong>Owners equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>4.724</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Non-current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>3.309</strong></td><td>Bank loan due 2020–2021</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">600</td></tr><tr><td>Stock of fertilizers</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">100</td><td><strong>Non-current liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>600</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Accounts receivable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">310</td><td>Bank loan due 31/12/2019</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">300</td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2.407</td><td>Suppliers of fertilizers</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">150</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Creditors for administration expenses</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">72</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Supplier of thermal cameras</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">100</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Taxes payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">180</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>2.817</strong></td><td><strong>Current liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>802</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>6.126</strong></td><td><strong>Total liabilities and equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>6.126</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Accumulated depreciation of the drones: $75 + 300 = 375$.</p>"}, {"label": "c", "points": 0.5, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute ROA and ROE of 2018</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Use the income from operations for ROA and the net profit for ROE, on the year-end balance.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>ROA = 12,93%; ROE = 11,43% (extension).</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{ROA} = \\frac{792}{6.126} = 12{,}93\\%, \\qquad \\text{ROE} = \\frac{540}{4.724} = 11{,}43\\%$$</div>\n<p>The ROE is below the ROA because interest and taxes are deducted from the numerator, while the equity base (4.724) is not much smaller than the total assets (6.126).</p>"}, {"label": "d", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Effect of paying the dividend on 30 June 2019 and of not offsetting the 2017 loss before the 30% payout</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Two changes at once: the dividend is now 30% of the whole 540, and it becomes a debt instead of a payment. The 2017 loss stays in equity.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Dividend $0{,}30 \\times 540 = 162$ m.u., recorded as \"dividends payable\" (current).</li><li>Cash +96 (2.503); reserves 378; losses of 2017 (220) remain; owners equity 4.658 (−66).</li><li>Totals 6.222 m.u. (+96).</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. New distribution</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Dividend} = 0{,}30 \\times 540 = 162, \\qquad \\text{Reserves} = 540 - 162 = 378 \\text{ m.u.}, \\qquad \\text{the loss of 2017 } (-220) \\text{ is not offset}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Changes in the balance sheet at 31/12/2018</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Base case</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">New case</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Change</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cash (the 96 m.u. are no longer paid)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2.407</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2.503</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+96</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>6.126</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>6.222</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>+96</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Losses of 2017</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">(220)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−220</td></tr><tr><td>Reserves</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">224</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">378</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+154</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Owners equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>4.724</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>4.658</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>−66</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Dividends payable (current liability)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">162</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+162</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total liabilities and equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>6.126</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>6.222</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>+96</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Check: $-66 + 162 = +96$ ✓. The shareholders are promised more (162 instead of 96) because the dividend is computed before absorbing the loss; equity is lower by exactly the extra dividend ($162 - 96 = 66$), and the cash stays in the company until June.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": "<ul><li><strong>Dimensional check:</strong> farms × m.u./(month·farm) × months gives m.u.; m.u./year × months/12 gives m.u.</li><li><strong>Accrual vs cash:</strong> net profit 540 m.u., while cash grew by 452 m.u.: $540 + 416$ (depreciation, no cash) $- 310$ (uncollected sales) $- 100$ (stock) $+ 150 + 72 + 180$ (suppliers, administration and tax still owed) $- 100$ (cameras) $- 300$ (principal) $- 96$ (dividends) $= 452$.</li><li><strong>Typical mistakes:</strong> expensing all 500 m.u. of fertilizers, amortizing the software in 2017, forgetting the 20% residual value of the cameras, and computing the interest on the original 1.500 m.u.</li></ul>"}, {"id": "P13", "category": "extra_practice_problem", "archetype": "narrative-case", "theme": "narrative-case", "unit": 3, "title": "TAHSA: deducing the share of cash sales from the bank balance", "difficulty": 3, "points": 4.0, "time_minutes": 60, "tags": ["extra-practice", "exam-style", "accrual-vs-cash", "uncalled-capital", "bad-debts", "early-payment-discount"], "official": false, "source": "Exam-style practice exercise written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), in the style of T3_Additional_EXERCISES.pdf", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank P13 - TAHSA Deducing the Cash Sales from the Bank Balance.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>The external auditor of <strong>TALLERES AERONÁUTICOS DEL HENARES S.A. (TAHSA)</strong>, a company of Alcalá de Henares dedicated to the machining of titanium brackets and fittings for the aircraft structures industry, must issue the audit report on the financial statements (balance sheet and Income Statement) as of 31 December 2019 (31/12/2019). The auditor has the balance of the company as of 31 December 2018 (31/12/2018), which is included below, and the following operations that have taken place during the period from 1 January 2019 (01/01/2019) to 31 December 2019 (31/12/2019).</p>\n<p><strong>I.</strong> The company has sold during the year 2.400 brackets at a price of 8 m.u. / unit (every month the same amount, 200 u.). A part of the sales of each month, always the same percentage, is charged in cash in the moment of the sale; the rest is payable to 90 days. Unfortunately the commercial department has lost the contract that fixes this percentage.</p>\n<p><strong>II.</strong> During the first quarter of 2019, the unpaid taxes and the outstanding supplier balances of the previous year were paid. The customer debt outstanding from the previous year was charged in January, except 160 m.u. from an airline component maker declared in insolvency on 1 March 2019 (01/03/2019), which is definitively lost.</p>\n<p><strong>III.</strong> At 31 December 2018 (31/12/2018) there is a stock (stock in stock) of 800 kg of titanium bar valued at 1,50 m.u. / kg. On 1 February 2019 (01/02/2019) the company purchased 3.000 kg at 1,50 m.u. / kg, 50% payable to 60 days and the other 50% deferred until 1 February 2020 (01/02/2020). On 1 August 2019 (01/08/2019) it purchased 2.000 kg more at the same price, payable to 120 days. The count of 31 December 2019 (31/12/2019) shows 900 kg in the warehouse.</p>\n<p><strong>IV.</strong> Personnel expenses have been 400 m.u. / month (to which 120 m.u. / month must be added for Social Security); salaries are paid in the month in which they are produced and Social Security the month after. General expenses (electricity, insurance, cleaning) amount to 150 m.u. / month and are paid two months after being made.</p>\n<p><strong>V.</strong> The machinery of the balance of 2018 was bought on 1 January 2016 (01/01/2016) and is amortized linearly in 10 years with residual value zero. On 1 April 2019 (01/04/2019) the company bought a 5-axis CNC centre that cost 4.800 m.u., paid in full in cash that day. To finance it, the same day a bank granted a loan of 3.600 m.u. at 5% annual interest, to be returned in one single payment on 1 April 2022 (01/04/2022); the interest is paid every 1 April for the year that ends that day. The CNC centre will be amortized linearly in 8 years with a residual value of 480 m.u.</p>\n<p><strong>VI.</strong> The long-term loan of the balance of 2018 bears 4% annual interest. The company returns 300 m.u. of principal every 30 June and every 31 December, paying the same days the interest of the semester calculated on the debt at the beginning of the semester.</p>\n<p><strong>VII.</strong> On 1 October 2019 (01/10/2019) the shareholders subscribed a capital increase of 500 new shares of 2 m.u. nominal value, without issue premium, disbursing that day only 50%; the rest will be called in 2020. On 15 November 2019 (15/11/2019) the company received in cash a dividend of 80 m.u. from its financial shares in AEROPIEZAS S.A., a permanent investment.</p>\n<p><strong>VIII.</strong> The bank statement at 23:00 hrs on 31 December 2019 (31/12/2019), before any operation of the closing, shows a balance of 3.024 m.u. The results are declared at 23:59 hrs; the corporation tax of 30% is paid immediately, and 50% of the profit after taxes is paid as dividends before the final balance at 24:00 on 31 December 2019 (31/12/2019); the rest goes to reserves.</p>\n<p><strong>BALANCE 31 December 2018 (31/12/2018)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>ASSETS</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th><th>LIABILITIES</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>NON-CURRENT ASSETS</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>7.200</strong></td><td><strong>OWNERS EQUITY</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>8.100</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Land</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2.000</td><td>Paid-in capital (3.500 shares × 2 m.u.)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">7.000</td></tr><tr><td>Machinery</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">6.000</td><td>Reserves</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.100</td></tr><tr><td>Accum. depreciation</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">(1.800)</td><td><strong>NON-CURRENT LIABILITIES</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>1.200</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Financial shares in AEROPIEZAS S.A.</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.000</td><td>Long-term loan</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.200</td></tr><tr><td><strong>CURRENT ASSETS</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>3.700</strong></td><td><strong>CURRENT LIABILITIES</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>1.600</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Stock of titanium</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.200</td><td>Long-term loan, part due in 2019</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">600</td></tr><tr><td>Accounts receivable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.600</td><td>Suppliers</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">700</td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">900</td><td>Unpaid taxes</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">300</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total Assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>10.900</strong></td><td><strong>Total Liabilities + OE</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>10.900</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Required:</strong></p>\n<ol><li>Deduce, justifying it from the bank statement, the percentage of the sales that is charged in cash, and construct the Income Statement (Profit and Loss Account) of TAHSA for the year 2019.</li><li>Construct and justify the balance sheet at 24:00 on 31 December 2019 (31/12/2019), showing all the intermediate calculations.</li><li>Determine the values of the ROA and ROE ratios in 2019.</li><li>The commercial director proposes for 2020 that 50% of the sales be charged in cash, granting on those cash sales a discount of 4%. Indicate what changes would have occurred in the Income Statement, the cash and the balance sheet of 2019 if this policy had been applied from 1 January 2019 (01/01/2019). In which case is the profit higher, and in which case is the final cash balance higher? Discuss, reasoned, why.</li></ol>", "setup_html": "<p><strong>Unknown of the problem:</strong> the share $p$ of each month's sales charged in cash. Every other flow of cash can be computed, so the bank statement before the closing (3.024 m.u.) gives one equation in $p$.</p>\n<p><strong>Collections at 90 days:</strong> a sale of January is collected in April; the sales of October, November and December are still pending at 31/12/2019.</p>\n<p><strong>Uncalled capital:</strong> the shareholders owe the 50% not yet disbursed; it is shown as a deduction of the subscribed capital, as in the Company X exercise of the course.</p>\n<p><strong>Extension (not defined in the course decks).</strong> $\\text{ROA} = \\text{Income from operations}/\\text{Total assets}$ and $\\text{ROE} = \\text{Net profit}/\\text{Owners equity}$, on the balance at 24:00 on 31/12/2019.</p>", "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.5, "prompt_html": "<p>Deduce the percentage of cash sales and prepare the 2019 Income Statement</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Write the cash at 23:00 as initial cash + all inflows − all outflows, leaving the collections from 2019 sales as a function of $p$: $12 \\times 1.600\\,p + 9 \\times 1.600\\,(1-p)$.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Outflows before closing 19.336; inflows other than 2019 sales 5.620 m.u.</li><li>$p = 30\\%$; collections from 2019 sales 15.840 m.u.</li><li>Titanium consumed 7.350; depreciation 1.005; income from operations 2.645 m.u.</li><li>Financial income 80, financial expenses 201; profit before taxes 2.524; tax 757,20; net profit 1.766,80 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Outflows before the closing</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Outflow</th><th>Computation</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Suppliers 2018 and unpaid taxes 2018</td><td>$700 + 300$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.000</td></tr><tr><td>Titanium of 01/02, 50% at 60 days (01/04)</td><td>$0{,}50 \\times 3.000 \\times 1{,}50$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2.250</td></tr><tr><td>Titanium of 01/08, at 120 days (29/11)</td><td>$2.000 \\times 1{,}50$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3.000</td></tr><tr><td>Salaries</td><td>$12 \\times 400$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">4.800</td></tr><tr><td>Social Security, January–November</td><td>$11 \\times 120$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.320</td></tr><tr><td>General expenses, January–October</td><td>$10 \\times 150$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.500</td></tr><tr><td>CNC centre</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">4.800</td></tr><tr><td>Old loan: principal</td><td>$300 + 300$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">600</td></tr><tr><td>Old loan: interest</td><td>$1.800 \\times 0{,}04 \\times \\tfrac{6}{12} + 1.500 \\times 0{,}04 \\times \\tfrac{6}{12} = 36 + 30$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">66</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>19.336</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Inflows other than the 2019 sales</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\underbrace{1.600 - 160}_{\\text{2018 receivables}} + \\underbrace{3.600}_{\\text{new loan}} + \\underbrace{0{,}50 \\times 1.000}_{\\text{capital disbursed}} + \\underbrace{80}_{\\text{dividend received}} = 1.440 + 3.600 + 500 + 80 = 5.620 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 3. Equation for $p$</h4>\n<p>Monthly sales are $200 \\times 8 = 1.600$ m.u. The cash part of every month is collected (12 months); the credit part only for January to September (9 months):</p>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$900 + 5.620 + 12 \\times 1.600\\,p + 9 \\times 1.600\\,(1 - p) - 19.336 = 3.024$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$19.200\\,p + 14.400 - 14.400\\,p = 3.024 + 19.336 - 900 - 5.620 = 15.840$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$4.800\\,p = 15.840 - 14.400 = 1.440 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; p = \\frac{1.440}{4.800} = 0{,}30 = 30\\%$$</div>\n<p>Collections from 2019 sales: $19.200 \\times 0{,}30 + 9 \\times 1.120 = 5.760 + 10.080 = 15.840$ m.u.; pending at 31/12: $3 \\times 1.120 = 3.360$ m.u.</p>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 4. Titanium consumed</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Consumption} = 1.200 + 3.000 \\times 1{,}50 + 2.000 \\times 1{,}50 - 900 \\times 1{,}50 = 1.200 + 4.500 + 3.000 - 1.350 = 7.350 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 5. Depreciation</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$D_{\\text{machinery}} = \\frac{6.000}{10} = 600 \\quad \\left(\\text{check: } 3 \\text{ years} \\times 600 = 1.800 \\text{ at } 31/12/2018 \\;\\checkmark\\right)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$D_{\\text{CNC}} = \\frac{4.800 - 480}{8} \\times \\frac{9}{12} = 540 \\times \\frac{9}{12} = 405, \\qquad D_{2019} = 600 + 405 = 1.005 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 6. Financial items</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$I_{\\text{old loan}} = 66 \\text{ (Step 1)}, \\qquad I_{\\text{CNC loan}} = 3.600 \\times 0{,}05 \\times \\frac{9}{12} = 135 \\text{ (accrued, paid 01/04/2020)}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Financial expenses} = 66 + 135 = 201, \\qquad \\text{Financial income (dividend received)} = 80 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 7. Income Statement 2019</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Computation</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sales revenue</td><td>$2.400 \\times 8$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">19.200</td></tr><tr><td>− Titanium consumed</td><td>Step 4</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">7.350</td></tr><tr><td>− Personnel (salaries and Social Security)</td><td>$12 \\times (400 + 120)$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">6.240</td></tr><tr><td>− General expenses</td><td>$12 \\times 150$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.800</td></tr><tr><td>− Bad debt loss</td><td>item II</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">160</td></tr><tr><td>− Depreciation</td><td>Step 5</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.005</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income from operations</strong></td><td>$19.200 - 16.555$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>2.645</strong></td></tr><tr><td>+ Financial income</td><td>dividend from AEROPIEZAS</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">80</td></tr><tr><td>− Financial expenses</td><td>Step 6</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">201</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Profit before taxes</strong></td><td>$2.645 + 80 - 201$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>2.524</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Corporation tax (30%)</td><td>$0{,}30 \\times 2.524$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">757,20</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Net profit</strong></td><td>$2.524 - 757{,}20$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>1.766,80</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Prepare the balance sheet at 24:00 on 31 December 2019</p>", "hint_html": "<p>From the 3.024 m.u. of 23:00, only the tax and the dividend leave before 24:00. Split each debt by its due date; the new loan is repaid in 2022.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Dividends 883,40; reserves 1.983,40 m.u.; cash 1.383,40 m.u.</li><li>Owners equity 9.483,40 (subscribed 8.000, uncalled −500).</li><li>Non-current liabilities 4.200; current liabilities 3.405; total 17.088,40 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Closing operations</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Dividends} = 0{,}50 \\times 1.766{,}80 = 883{,}40, \\qquad \\text{to reserves} = 883{,}40 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\text{Reserves} = 1.100 + 883{,}40 = 1.983{,}40$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Cash}_{24:00} = 3.024 - 757{,}20 - 883{,}40 = 1.383{,}40 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Old loan after 2019</h4>\n<p>$1.800 - 600 = 1.200$ m.u.: 600 due in 2020 (current) and 600 in 2021 (non-current).</p>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 3. Balance sheet at 24:00 on 31/12/2019</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Assets</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th><th>Liabilities and owners equity</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Land</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2.000</td><td>Subscribed capital (4.000 shares × 2)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">8.000</td></tr><tr><td>Machinery ($6.000 - 2.400$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3.600</td><td>− Uncalled capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">(500)</td></tr><tr><td>CNC centre ($4.800 - 405$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">4.395</td><td>Reserves</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.983,40</td></tr><tr><td>Financial shares in AEROPIEZAS S.A.</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.000</td><td><strong>Owners equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>9.483,40</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Non-current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>10.995</strong></td><td>Bank loan for the CNC (01/04/2022)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3.600</td></tr><tr><td>Stock of titanium (900 kg × 1,50)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.350</td><td>Old loan, part due in 2021</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">600</td></tr><tr><td>Accounts receivable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3.360</td><td><strong>Non-current liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>4.200</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.383,40</td><td>Old loan, part due in 2020</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">600</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Interest payable (CNC loan)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">135</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Suppliers of titanium (01/02/2020)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2.250</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Social Security payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">120</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>General expenses payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">300</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>6.093,40</strong></td><td><strong>Current liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>3.405</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>17.088,40</strong></td><td><strong>Total liabilities and equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>17.088,40</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "c", "points": 0.5, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute ROA and ROE of 2019</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Year-end balance; income from operations for ROA, net profit for ROE.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>ROA = 15,48%; ROE = 18,63% (extension).</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{ROA} = \\frac{2.645}{17.088{,}40} = 15{,}48\\%, \\qquad \\text{ROE} = \\frac{1.766{,}80}{9.483{,}40} = 18{,}63\\%$$</div>"}, {"label": "d", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Effect of charging 50% in cash with a 4% discount; which case gives more profit and which more cash?</p>", "hint_html": "<p>The discount lowers revenue; the higher cash share brings collections forward. Then follow the change through the tax and the dividend, both paid at the closing.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Revenue 18.816 (−384); net profit 1.498 (−268,80); dividends 749 (−134,40).</li><li>Collections from 2019 sales 16.416 (+576); receivables 2.400 (−960).</li><li>Cash at 24:00: 2.209 (+825,60). Profit higher with the present policy; cash higher with the new policy.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. New revenue and collections</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Revenue} = 0{,}50 \\times 19.200 \\times 0{,}96 + 0{,}50 \\times 19.200 = 9.216 + 9.600 = 18.816 \\text{ m.u.} \\;(-384)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Collections} = 9.216 + 9 \\times 800 = 16.416 \\;(+576), \\qquad \\text{Receivables} = 3 \\times 800 = 2.400 \\;(-960)$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Results and closing</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Profit before taxes} = 2.524 - 384 = 2.140, \\qquad \\text{Tax} = 0{,}30 \\times 2.140 = 642, \\qquad \\text{Net profit} = 1.498$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Dividends} = 0{,}50 \\times 1.498 = 749, \\qquad \\text{Reserves} = 1.100 + 749 = 1.849$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Cash}_{24:00} = (3.024 + 576) - 642 - 749 = 2.209 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 3. Changes in the statements</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Present policy</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">New policy</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Change</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Revenue</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">19.200</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">18.816</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−384</td></tr><tr><td>Corporation tax</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">757,20</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">642</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−115,20</td></tr><tr><td>Net profit</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.766,80</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.498</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−268,80</td></tr><tr><td>Accounts receivable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3.360</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2.400</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−960</td></tr><tr><td>Cash at 24:00</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.383,40</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2.209</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+825,60</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>17.088,40</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>16.954</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>−134,40</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Reserves</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.983,40</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.849</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−134,40</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total liabilities and equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>17.088,40</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>16.954</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>−134,40</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Discussion.</strong> The <strong>profit is higher with the present policy</strong>: the discount is a real cost of 384 m.u. (269 m.u. after tax). The <strong>cash is higher with the new policy</strong>: 576 m.u. more are collected in 2019 because more of each sale is charged at once, and the lower profit also means less tax and fewer dividends paid at the closing. The discount buys liquidity at the price of profitability; it is worth it only if the company needs the cash or if the money collected earlier earns more than the discount costs.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": "<ul><li><strong>Dimensional check:</strong> units × m.u./unit, kg × m.u./kg and m.u./year × months/12 all give m.u.; $p$ and the ratios are dimensionless.</li><li><strong>Why the bank balance is enough:</strong> all the other flows are fixed by the terms of the contracts, so the cash balance determines the single unknown term, the share of cash sales.</li><li><strong>Uncalled capital:</strong> the 500 m.u. not disbursed are a debt of the shareholders to the company, not cash; showing them as a deduction keeps equity at the money actually contributed.</li><li><strong>Typical mistakes:</strong> paying the CNC loan interest in 2019, depreciating the CNC for 12 months, counting the dividend received as revenue from operations, and treating the bad debt as a collection.</li></ul>"}, {"id": "P14", "category": "extra_practice_problem", "archetype": "narrative-case", "theme": "narrative-case", "unit": 3, "title": "AVIEBRO: a half-year close with a volume discount, a mortgage and prior losses", "difficulty": 3, "points": 4.0, "time_minutes": 60, "tags": ["extra-practice", "exam-style", "half-year", "mortgage", "losses-carried-forward", "renting"], "official": false, "source": "Exam-style practice exercise written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), in the style of T3_Additional_EXERCISES.pdf, Exercise 1 (CIRCONSA)", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank P14 - AVIEBRO Half-Year with a Mortgage and Prior Losses.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p><strong>AVIÓNICA DEL EBRO S.L. (AVIEBRO)</strong>, a Zaragoza workshop dedicated to the repair and calibration of avionics equipment for regional airlines and flying schools, must present to the bank that finances it the financial statements (balance sheet and Income Statement) as of 30 June 2020 (30/06/2020). The financial director has the balance of 31 December 2019 (31/12/2019), included below, and the following operations of the first semester, from 1 January 2020 (01/01/2020) to 30 June 2020 (30/06/2020):</p>\n<p><strong>I.</strong> The company has invoiced repairs for 1.600 m.u. every month. The payment condition agreed with the customers is payable to 60 days. A regional airline, which represents 40% of the invoicing of every month, surpassed in March the annual volume agreed in its contract, and from 1 April 2020 (01/04/2020) it receives a discount of 5% on all its invoices.</p>\n<p><strong>II.</strong> The customer debt outstanding at 31 December 2019 (31/12/2019) was charged in January, except 250 m.u. from a flying school that has been granted to pay it on 30 September 2020 (30/09/2020). The suppliers of the previous year were paid in February. As the company closed 2019 with losses, there were no taxes to pay.</p>\n<p><strong>III.</strong> At 31 December 2019 (31/12/2019) there is a stock (stock in stock) of 300 spare parts valued at 2 m.u. / unit. On 1 February 2020 (01/02/2020) the company purchased 400 parts at 2 m.u., 50% paid in cash and 50% payable to 90 days; on 1 May 2020 (01/05/2020) it purchased 300 parts more at the same price, payable to 120 days. At 30 June 2020 (30/06/2020) there remain 250 parts in the warehouse.</p>\n<p><strong>IV.</strong> The technicians cost 600 m.u. / month, paid in the same month, to which 180 m.u. / month of Social Security must be added, paid the month after. The overheads (electricity, telephone, cleaning) are 100 m.u. / month and are paid two months after being made. On 1 March 2020 (01/03/2020) the company carried out a renting contract for a test bench of 120 m.u. monthly, paid at the end of each month.</p>\n<p><strong>V.</strong> The patent of the balance (a calibration procedure) is amortized linearly in 5 years with residual value zero; the computer equipment was bought on 1 January 2019 (01/01/2019) and is amortized linearly in 3 years with residual value zero.</p>\n<p><strong>VI.</strong> On 1 April 2020 (01/04/2020) AVIEBRO bought a small hangar next to the airport: the building is valued at 2.000 m.u. and the land at 1.000 m.u. It paid 20% in cash and the rest with a mortgage loan at 4% annual interest, to be returned in installments of 120 m.u. every 1 October and every 1 April, the first one on 1 October 2020 (01/10/2020); the same days it pays the interest of the semester calculated on the debt at the beginning of the semester. The building is amortized linearly in 50 years with residual value zero.</p>\n<p><strong>VII.</strong> The long-term loan of the balance bears 5% annual interest, which is paid every 31 December, and it is returned in one single payment on 31 December 2022 (31/12/2022).</p>\n<p><strong>VIII.</strong> The corporation tax is 25%. The taxable base of the semester is the profit before taxes minus the losses from previous years still pending to offset, and the resulting tax will be paid on 20 July 2020 (20/07/2020) (counted in the balance as \"taxes to pay\"). No dividends are distributed at the half-year.</p>\n<p><strong>BALANCE 31 December 2019 (31/12/2019)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>ASSETS</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th><th>LIABILITIES</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>NON-CURRENT ASSETS</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>1.900</strong></td><td><strong>OWNERS EQUITY</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>2.700</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Patent</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.200</td><td>Paid-in capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3.200</td></tr><tr><td>Accum. amortization of the patent</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">(300)</td><td>Losses from previous years</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">(500)</td></tr><tr><td>Computer equipment</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">900</td><td><strong>NON-CURRENT LIABILITIES</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>1.000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Accum. depreciation of computer equipment</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">(300)</td><td>Long-term loan (31/12/2022)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.000</td></tr><tr><td>Financial shares</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">400</td><td><strong>CURRENT LIABILITIES</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>500</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>CURRENT ASSETS</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>2.300</strong></td><td>Suppliers</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">500</td></tr><tr><td>Stock of spare parts</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">600</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td></tr><tr><td>Accounts receivable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.000</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">700</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total Assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>4.200</strong></td><td><strong>Total Liabilities + OE</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>4.200</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Required:</strong></p>\n<ol><li>Deduce the exact date on which the patent was acquired, and construct the Income Statement (Profit and Loss Account) of AVIEBRO for the first semester of 2020, explaining the intermediate calculations.</li><li>Calculate the cash and construct the balance sheet at 30 June 2020 (30/06/2020).</li><li>Determine the values of the ROA and ROE ratios of the semester, and indicate how the Income Statement and the balance sheet would change if the tax authority did not allow to offset the losses from previous years.</li><li>Instead of buying the hangar, the company could have rented it from 1 April 2020 (01/04/2020) for 50 m.u. monthly, paid at the end of each month. Indicate the changes in the Income Statement, the cash and the balance sheet at 30 June 2020 (30/06/2020). In which case is the profit higher, and in which case is the final cash balance higher? Discuss, reasoned, why.</li></ol>", "setup_html": "<p><strong>Time window:</strong> six months, 01/01/2020–30/06/2020. Depreciation and interest are prorated by months; expenses are recorded when incurred even if they are paid later [Slide 8].</p>\n<p><strong>Hypotheses:</strong></p>\n<ol><li>The airline's discount applies to the invoices of April, May and June only.</li><li>\"Payable to 90 days\" from 1 February means payment on 1 May; \"payable to 120 days\" from 1 May means payment at the end of August, outside the semester.</li><li>The losses from previous years are offset for tax purposes, but they stay in the balance sheet until the annual meeting distributes the profit of the year; the result of the semester is shown separately in equity.</li><li>Land is not depreciated.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Extension (not defined in the course decks).</strong> $\\text{ROA} = \\text{Income from operations}/\\text{Total assets}$ and $\\text{ROE} = \\text{Net profit}/\\text{Owners equity}$, both for the semester and on the balance at 30/06/2020.</p>", "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Deduce the purchase date of the patent and prepare the Income Statement of the semester</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Compute the yearly amortization of the patent and compare it with the 300 accumulated. For the parts, compute the consumption from the stocks; for the airline, the discount is 5% of 40% of 1.600 in three months.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Patent bought on 1 October 2018 (01/10/2018).</li><li>Revenue 9.504 (discount 96); parts consumed 1.500; depreciation 280 m.u.</li><li>Income from operations 1.964; interest 49; profit before taxes 1.915 m.u.</li><li>Taxable base 1.415; tax 353,75; net profit 1.561,25 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Purchase date of the patent</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$D_{\\text{patent}} = \\frac{1.200}{5} = 240 \\text{ m.u./year}, \\qquad \\frac{300}{240} = 1{,}25 \\text{ years} = 15 \\text{ months before } 31/12/2019 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\text{1 October 2018 (01/10/2018)}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Revenue</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Discount per month} = 0{,}05 \\times 0{,}40 \\times 1.600 = 32 \\text{ m.u.}, \\qquad \\text{Revenue} = 6 \\times 1.600 - 3 \\times 32 = 9.600 - 96 = 9.504 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 3. Parts consumed</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Consumption} = 600 + 400 \\times 2 + 300 \\times 2 - 250 \\times 2 = 600 + 800 + 600 - 500 = 1.500 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 4. Personnel, overheads and renting</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Personnel} = 6 \\times (600 + 180) = 4.680, \\qquad \\text{Overheads} = 6 \\times 100 = 600, \\qquad \\text{Renting} = 4 \\times 120 = 480 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 5. Depreciation of the semester</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$D_{\\text{patent}} = 240 \\times \\frac{6}{12} = 120, \\qquad D_{\\text{computer}} = \\frac{900}{3} \\times \\frac{6}{12} = 150, \\qquad D_{\\text{building}} = \\frac{2.000}{50} \\times \\frac{3}{12} = 10$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$D = 120 + 150 + 10 = 280 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<p>Check of the computer equipment: one year at 300 m.u. gives the 300 of the 2019 balance ✓.</p>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 6. Interest of the semester (accrued, nothing paid)</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$I_{\\text{mortgage}} = 2.400 \\times 0{,}04 \\times \\frac{3}{12} = 24, \\qquad I_{\\text{loan}} = 1.000 \\times 0{,}05 \\times \\frac{6}{12} = 25 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<p>The mortgage is $3.000 - 0{,}20 \\times 3.000 = 2.400$ m.u.</p>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 7. Tax</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Taxable base} = 1.915 - 500 = 1.415, \\qquad \\text{Tax} = 0{,}25 \\times 1.415 = 353{,}75 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 8. Income Statement 01/01/2020–30/06/2020</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Computation</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Repair revenue</td><td>Step 2</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">9.504</td></tr><tr><td>− Spare parts consumed</td><td>Step 3</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.500</td></tr><tr><td>− Personnel (wages and Social Security)</td><td>Step 4</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">4.680</td></tr><tr><td>− Overheads</td><td>Step 4</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">600</td></tr><tr><td>− Renting of the test bench</td><td>Step 4</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">480</td></tr><tr><td>− Depreciation and amortization</td><td>Step 5</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">280</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income from operations</strong></td><td>$9.504 - 7.540$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>1.964</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Financial expenses</td><td>$24 + 25$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">49</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Profit before taxes</strong></td><td>$1.964 - 49$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>1.915</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Corporation tax</td><td>Step 7</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">353,75</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Net profit of the semester</strong></td><td>$1.915 - 353{,}75$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>1.561,25</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the cash and prepare the balance sheet at 30 June 2020</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Collected at 60 days: January to April invoices. Of the overheads, January to April are paid. The hangar is paid in full to the seller: 600 of own cash and 2.400 from the mortgage.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Cash at 30/06/2020: 538 m.u.</li><li>Receivables 3.386; non-current assets 4.620; total 9.044 m.u.</li><li>Owners equity 4.261,25; non-current liabilities 3.160; current liabilities 1.622,75 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Cash account of the semester</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Inflows</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th><th>Outflows</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2019 receivables ($1.000 - 250$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">750</td><td>Suppliers 2019</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">500</td></tr><tr><td>Invoices January–March ($3 \\times 1.600$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">4.800</td><td>Parts of 01/02 ($400 + 400$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">800</td></tr><tr><td>Invoice of April ($1.600 - 32$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.568</td><td>Technicians' wages</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3.600</td></tr><tr><td>Mortgage loan</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2.400</td><td>Social Security, January–May</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">900</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Overheads, January–April</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">400</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Renting, March–June</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">480</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Hangar (building and land)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3.000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>9.518</strong></td><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>9.680</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Cash}_{30/06/2020} = 700 + 9.518 - 9.680 = 538 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Items still open at 30/06/2020</h4>\n<ul><li>Receivables: May and June invoices, $2 \\times (1.600 - 32) = 3.136$, plus the 250 deferred to 30/09/2020: 3.386 m.u.</li><li>Suppliers: the parts of 01/05 ($300 \\times 2 = 600$), due at the end of August.</li><li>Mortgage: installments of 01/10/2020 and 01/04/2021 ($2 \\times 120 = 240$) are current; $2.400 - 240 = 2.160$ non-current.</li></ul>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 3. Balance sheet at 30/06/2020</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Assets</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th><th>Liabilities and owners equity</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Patent ($1.200 - 420$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">780</td><td>Paid-in capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3.200</td></tr><tr><td>Computer equipment ($900 - 450$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">450</td><td>Losses from previous years</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">(500)</td></tr><tr><td>Land</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.000</td><td>Profit of the semester</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.561,25</td></tr><tr><td>Building ($2.000 - 10$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.990</td><td><strong>Owners equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>4.261,25</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Financial shares</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">400</td><td>Long-term loan (31/12/2022)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Non-current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>4.620</strong></td><td>Mortgage loan, non-current part</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2.160</td></tr><tr><td>Stock of spare parts (250 × 2)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">500</td><td><strong>Non-current liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>3.160</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Accounts receivable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3.386</td><td>Mortgage loan, installments within one year</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">240</td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">538</td><td>Interest payable ($24 + 25$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">49</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Suppliers</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">600</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Social Security payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">180</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Overheads payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">200</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Taxes to pay</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">353,75</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>4.424</strong></td><td><strong>Current liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>1.622,75</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>9.044</strong></td><td><strong>Total liabilities and equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>9.044</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "c", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute ROA and ROE of the semester, and the effect of not offsetting the prior losses</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Without the offset, the whole 1.915 m.u. is taxed. Which items of the balance change, and by how much?</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>ROA = 21,72%; ROE = 36,64% (semester, extension).</li><li>Without the offset: tax 478,75 (+125); net profit 1.436,25 (−125); taxes to pay +125; equity 4.136,25; totals unchanged; ROE 34,72%.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Ratios of the semester (extension)</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{ROA} = \\frac{1.964}{9.044} = 21{,}72\\%, \\qquad \\text{ROE} = \\frac{1.561{,}25}{4.261{,}25} = 36{,}64\\%$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Without the offset of the losses</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Tax} = 0{,}25 \\times 1.915 = 478{,}75 \\;(+125), \\qquad \\text{Net profit} = 1.915 - 478{,}75 = 1.436{,}25 \\;(-125)$$</div>\n<p>In the balance, \"taxes to pay\" rises by 125 m.u. and the profit of the semester (equity) falls by 125 m.u.; total assets and cash do not change, because the tax is paid only in July. The ROE falls to $1.436{,}25 / 4.136{,}25 = 34{,}72\\%$. The offset is worth exactly $0{,}25 \\times 500 = 125$ m.u. of tax saved.</p>"}, {"label": "d", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Renting the hangar instead of buying it; which case gives more profit and which more cash?</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Remove everything the purchase brought (land, building, its depreciation, the mortgage, its interest, the 600 paid) and add three months of rent.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Income from operations 1.824 (−140); interest 25; tax 324,75; net profit 1.474,25 (−87).</li><li>Cash 988 (+450); total assets 6.504; no mortgage.</li><li>Profit higher buying; cash higher renting.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. New results</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Income from operations} = 1.964 + 10 - 3 \\times 50 = 1.824, \\qquad \\text{Profit before taxes} = 1.824 - 25 = 1.799$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Tax} = 0{,}25 \\times (1.799 - 500) = 324{,}75, \\qquad \\text{Net profit} = 1.799 - 324{,}75 = 1.474{,}25$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. New cash</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Cash} = 538 + 600 - 3 \\times 50 = 988 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 3. Changes in the statements</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Buying</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Renting</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Change</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Depreciation</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">280</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">270</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−10</td></tr><tr><td>Rent of the hangar</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">150</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+150</td></tr><tr><td>Financial expenses</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">49</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">25</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−24</td></tr><tr><td>Net profit</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.561,25</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.474,25</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−87</td></tr><tr><td>Land and building (net)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2.990</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−2.990</td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">538</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">988</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+450</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>9.044</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>6.504</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>−2.540</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Owners equity</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">4.261,25</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">4.174,25</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−87</td></tr><tr><td>Mortgage loan (current and non-current)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2.400</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−2.400</td></tr><tr><td>Interest payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">49</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">25</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−24</td></tr><tr><td>Taxes to pay</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">353,75</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">324,75</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−29</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total liabilities and equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>9.044</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>6.504</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>−2.540</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Discussion.</strong> The <strong>profit is higher buying the hangar</strong>: three months of rent (150) cost more than the depreciation of the building (10) and the mortgage interest (24) together, because the land is not depreciated and the mortgage is cheap. The <strong>cash is higher renting</strong>: the company keeps the 600 m.u. of down payment and pays only 150 of rent. Buying gives more profit but ties up cash and adds 2.400 m.u. of debt; renting preserves liquidity, which matters for a workshop that ends the semester with only 538 m.u. in the bank.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": "<ul><li><strong>Dimensional check:</strong> m.u./month × months gives m.u.; rates × m.u. × months/12 give m.u.; ratios dimensionless.</li><li><strong>Half-year ratios:</strong> ROA and ROE of a semester are not annual returns; annualising them would roughly double them.</li><li><strong>Profit is not cash:</strong> the company earned 1.561,25 m.u. but its cash fell from 700 to 538 m.u.: the hangar, the uncollected May–June invoices and the growth of receivables absorbed it.</li><li><strong>Typical mistakes:</strong> depreciating the land, applying the airline discount to the whole semester, paying the mortgage interest in the semester, and taxing the profit without offsetting the losses as the statement allows.</li></ul>"}, {"id": "P15", "category": "extra_practice_problem", "archetype": "financing-leverage", "theme": "financing-leverage", "unit": 3, "title": "CAESA: financing a second autoclave with a semiannual loan or with new shares", "difficulty": 3, "points": 4.0, "time_minutes": 60, "tags": ["extra-practice", "exam-style", "financing-decisions", "capital-increase", "financial-leverage", "roa", "roe"], "official": false, "source": "Exam-style practice exercise written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), in the style of T3_Additional_EXERCISES.pdf; uses Topic 3_Financial Management (I).pdf, Slides 23-26 and 36", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank P15 - CAESA Financing an Expansion with a Loan or New Shares.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>The chief financial officer (CFO) of <strong>COMPOSITES AEROESPACIALES DE ÁLAVA S.A. (CAESA)</strong>, a company of Vitoria dedicated to the manufacturing of carbon-fibre fairings for helicopters, is closing the books of the year 2021. The balance of the company at 31 December 2020 (31/12/2020) is included below; the operations of the period from 1 January 2021 (01/01/2021) to 31 December 2021 (31/12/2021) have been the following:</p>\n<p><strong>I.</strong> The company has sold 1.200 fairings at a price of 30 m.u. / unit (every month the same amount, 100 u.). Of the sales of every month, 20% is charged in cash and the remaining 80% is payable to 60 days. The customer debt of the previous year was charged in January, and the suppliers and the unpaid taxes of the previous year were paid in January and in April respectively.</p>\n<p><strong>II.</strong> At 31 December 2020 (31/12/2020) there is a stock (stock in stock) of 500 kg of epoxy resin valued at 3 m.u. / kg. On 1 March 2021 (01/03/2021) the company purchased 4.000 kg at 3 m.u. / kg, paying 30% in cash and the remaining 70% in two equal parts, on 1 September 2021 (01/09/2021) and on 1 March 2022 (01/03/2022). At 31 December 2021 (31/12/2021) there remain 700 kg in the warehouse.</p>\n<p><strong>III.</strong> The fixed salaries are 1.000 m.u. / month, paid in the same month, to which 300 m.u. / month of Social Security must be added, paid the month after. Variable wages of the lamination staff amounted in 2021 to 1.200 m.u. (paid in cash). The external supplies (energy for the autoclaves) are 250 m.u. / month and are paid two months after being made.</p>\n<p><strong>IV.</strong> The autoclave of the balance was bought on 1 January 2018 (01/01/2018) and is amortized linearly in 10 years with a residual value of 1.000 m.u. On 1 July 2021 (01/07/2021) CAESA put into service a second autoclave of 6.000 m.u. (amortized linearly in 10 years with a residual value of 600 m.u.) together with a software license for the control of the curing cycles of 1.200 m.u. (amortized linearly in 3 years with residual value zero), both paid in full in cash that day.</p>\n<p><strong>V.</strong> To pay the 7.200 m.u. of the expansion, the board accepted on 1 July 2021 (01/07/2021) a bank loan of 7.200 m.u. at 5% annual interest, to be returned in 8 semiannual installments of 900 m.u. every 1 January and every 1 July, the first on 1 January 2022 (01/01/2022); the same days the company pays the interest of the semester calculated on the debt at the beginning of the semester. The long-term loan of the balance bears 6% annual interest, paid every 31 December, and is returned in one single payment on 31 December 2023 (31/12/2023).</p>\n<p><strong>VI.</strong> The financial results are declared at 23:59 hrs on 31 December 2021 (31/12/2021). The corporation tax is 20% on the Profits and is paid immediately. Of the profit after taxes, 10% goes to the legal reserve, 30% is distributed as dividends paid in cash at 24:00 on 31 December 2021 (31/12/2021), and the rest goes to voluntary reserves.</p>\n<p><strong>VII.</strong> The alternative rejected by the board was a capital increase on 1 July 2021 (01/07/2021) of 4.800 new shares of 1 m.u. nominal value with an issue premium of 50% over the nominal value, fully disbursed in cash, instead of the bank loan.</p>\n<p><strong>BALANCE 31 December 2020 (31/12/2020)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>ASSETS</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th><th>LIABILITIES</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>NON-CURRENT ASSETS</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>9.600</strong></td><td><strong>OWNERS EQUITY</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>12.000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Land</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3.000</td><td>Paid-in capital (10.000 shares × 1 m.u.)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">10.000</td></tr><tr><td>Autoclave</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">9.000</td><td>Reserves</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2.000</td></tr><tr><td>Accum. depreciation</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">(2.400)</td><td><strong>NON-CURRENT LIABILITIES</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>1.000</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>CURRENT ASSETS</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>5.000</strong></td><td>Long-term loan (31/12/2023)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.000</td></tr><tr><td>Stock of resin</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.500</td><td><strong>CURRENT LIABILITIES</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>1.600</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Accounts receivable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2.000</td><td>Suppliers</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.200</td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.500</td><td>Unpaid taxes</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">400</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total Assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>14.600</strong></td><td><strong>Total Liabilities + OE</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>14.600</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Required:</strong></p>\n<ol><li>Construct the Income Statement (Profit and Loss Account) of CAESA for the year 2021, justifying the depreciation of the old autoclave from the data of the balance.</li><li>Construct and justify the balance sheet at 24:00 on 31 December 2021 (31/12/2021), detailing the cash calculations and the classification of the new loan between current and non-current liabilities.</li><li>Determine the ROA, the ROE and the debt-to-equity ratio of 2021.</li><li>Indicate what changes would have occurred in the Income Statement, the cash and the balance sheet if the board had chosen the capital increase described in VII. Compare the ROE and the debt-to-equity ratio of both alternatives and explain, reasoned, the effect of the financial leverage.</li></ol>", "setup_html": "<p><strong>Hypotheses:</strong></p>\n<ol><li>\"Payable to 60 days\" leaves the November and December sales uncollected at 31/12/2021.</li><li>The interest of the new loan for July–December 2021 is accrued in 2021 and paid on 1 January 2022, the day after the closing; the installment of that day and the one of 1 July 2022 are due within one year.</li><li>Land is not depreciated.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Financing sources involved:</strong> a long-term bank loan (external liability, [Slide 31]) against a rights issue with premium (external equity, [Slides 25-26]); the reserves are self-financing [Slide 23]. The debt-to-equity ratio is course material [Slide 36].</p>\n<p><strong>Extension (not defined in the course decks).</strong> $\\text{ROA} = \\text{Income from operations}/\\text{TA}$; $\\text{ROE} = \\text{Net profit}/E$, on the balance at 24:00.</p>", "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Prepare the 2021 Income Statement</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Check the old autoclave: how much did it depreciate per year if 2.400 accumulated in three years? The new assets work six months.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Old autoclave 800 m.u./year ($3 \\times 800 = 2.400$ ✓); new autoclave 270; software 200; total 1.270 m.u.</li><li>Resin consumed 11.400; income from operations 3.530; interest 240 m.u.</li><li>Profit before taxes 3.290; tax 658; net profit 2.632 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Depreciation</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$D_{\\text{old}} = \\frac{9.000 - 1.000}{10} = 800 \\quad \\left(\\text{2018–2020: } 3 \\times 800 = 2.400 \\;\\checkmark\\right)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$D_{\\text{new}} = \\frac{6.000 - 600}{10} \\times \\frac{6}{12} = 540 \\times \\frac{6}{12} = 270, \\qquad D_{\\text{software}} = \\frac{1.200}{3} \\times \\frac{6}{12} = 200$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$D_{2021} = 800 + 270 + 200 = 1.270 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Resin consumed</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Consumption} = 1.500 + 4.000 \\times 3 - 700 \\times 3 = 1.500 + 12.000 - 2.100 = 11.400 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 3. Interest</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$I_{\\text{old loan}} = 0{,}06 \\times 1.000 = 60, \\qquad I_{\\text{new loan}} = 7.200 \\times 0{,}05 \\times \\frac{6}{12} = 180, \\qquad I = 240 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 4. Income Statement 2021</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Computation</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sales revenue</td><td>$1.200 \\times 30$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">36.000</td></tr><tr><td>− Resin consumed</td><td>Step 2</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">11.400</td></tr><tr><td>− Fixed salaries and Social Security</td><td>$12 \\times (1.000 + 300)$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">15.600</td></tr><tr><td>− Variable wages</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.200</td></tr><tr><td>− External supplies (energy)</td><td>$12 \\times 250$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3.000</td></tr><tr><td>− Depreciation and amortization</td><td>Step 1</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.270</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income from operations</strong></td><td>$36.000 - 32.470$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>3.530</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Financial expenses</td><td>Step 3</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">240</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Profit before taxes</strong></td><td>$3.530 - 240$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>3.290</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Corporation tax (20%)</td><td>$0{,}20 \\times 3.290$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">658</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Net profit</strong></td><td>$3.290 - 658$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>2.632</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.5, "prompt_html": "<p>Prepare the balance sheet at 24:00 on 31 December 2021</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Distribute the 2.632 m.u. (10% / 30% / 60%) and pay the tax and the dividend before 24:00. Of the 7.200 m.u. of the new loan, two installments fall within 2022.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Legal reserve 263,20; dividends 789,60; voluntary reserves 1.579,20 m.u.</li><li>Cash 4.792,40 m.u.</li><li>New loan: 1.800 current and 5.400 non-current; interest payable 180.</li><li>Owners equity 13.842,40; total 27.222,40 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Distribution</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Legal reserve} = 0{,}10 \\times 2.632 = 263{,}20, \\qquad \\text{Dividends} = 0{,}30 \\times 2.632 = 789{,}60, \\qquad \\text{Voluntary reserves} = 0{,}60 \\times 2.632 = 1.579{,}20$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Cash account 2021</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Inflows</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th><th>Outflows</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cash sales ($0{,}20 \\times 36.000$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">7.200</td><td>Suppliers 2020</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.200</td></tr><tr><td>Credit sales January–October ($10 \\times 2.400$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">24.000</td><td>Unpaid taxes 2020</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">400</td></tr><tr><td>Receivables 2020</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2.000</td><td>Resin ($0{,}30 \\times 12.000 + 0{,}35 \\times 12.000$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">7.800</td></tr><tr><td>New bank loan</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">7.200</td><td>Fixed salaries</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">12.000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Social Security, January–November</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3.300</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Variable wages</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.200</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>External supplies, January–October</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2.500</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Second autoclave and software</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">7.200</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Interest of the old loan</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">60</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Corporation tax 2021</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">658</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Dividends</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">789,60</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>40.400</strong></td><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>37.107,60</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Cash}_{24:00,\\,31/12/2021} = 1.500 + 40.400 - 37.107{,}60 = 4.792{,}40 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 3. Classification of the new loan</h4>\n<p>Installments on 01/01/2022 and 01/07/2022: $2 \\times 900 = 1.800$ m.u. current; the other six, $6 \\times 900 = 5.400$ m.u., non-current.</p>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 4. Balance sheet at 24:00 on 31/12/2021</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Assets</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th><th>Liabilities and owners equity</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Land</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3.000</td><td>Paid-in capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">10.000</td></tr><tr><td>Old autoclave ($9.000 - 3.200$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">5.800</td><td>Legal reserve</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">263,20</td></tr><tr><td>New autoclave ($6.000 - 270$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">5.730</td><td>Voluntary reserves (the 2.000 of 2020 + 1.579,20)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3.579,20</td></tr><tr><td>Software license ($1.200 - 200$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.000</td><td><strong>Owners equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>13.842,40</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Non-current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>15.530</strong></td><td>Long-term loan (31/12/2023)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.000</td></tr><tr><td>Stock of resin (700 kg × 3)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2.100</td><td>New bank loan, non-current part</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">5.400</td></tr><tr><td>Accounts receivable ($2 \\times 2.400$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">4.800</td><td><strong>Non-current liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>6.400</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">4.792,40</td><td>New bank loan, installments in 2022</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.800</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Interest payable (01/01/2022)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">180</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Suppliers of resin (01/03/2022)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">4.200</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Social Security payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">300</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>External supplies payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">500</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>11.692,40</strong></td><td><strong>Current liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>6.980</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>27.222,40</strong></td><td><strong>Total liabilities and equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>27.222,40</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "c", "points": 0.5, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute ROA, ROE and debt-to-equity of 2021</p>", "hint_html": "<p>All liabilities count, non-current and current.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>ROA = 12,97%; ROE = 19,01% (extension); $L/E = 0{,}97$.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{ROA} = \\frac{3.530}{27.222{,}40} = 12{,}97\\%, \\qquad \\text{ROE} = \\frac{2.632}{13.842{,}40} = 19{,}01\\%, \\qquad \\frac{L}{E} = \\frac{13.380}{13.842{,}40} = 0{,}97$$</div>"}, {"label": "d", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>The capital increase instead of the loan: changes, ROE, leverage and the leverage effect</p>", "hint_html": "<p>The new shares bring the same 7.200 m.u. of cash (4.800 capital + 2.400 premium) but no interest and no debt. Follow the higher profit through the tax and the distribution.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Interest 60; profit before taxes 3.470; tax 694; net profit 2.776 m.u. (+144).</li><li>Legal reserve 277,60; dividends 832,80; voluntary 1.665,60; cash 4.713,20 (−79,20).</li><li>Owners equity 21.143,20; liabilities 6.000; total 27.143,20 m.u.</li><li>ROE 13,13% vs 19,01%; $L/E$ 0,28 vs 0,97. The loan raises ROE because ROA ≈ 13% &gt; cost of debt 5%.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. New results</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Profit before taxes} = 3.530 - 60 = 3.470, \\qquad \\text{Tax} = 0{,}20 \\times 3.470 = 694, \\qquad \\text{Net profit} = 3.470 - 694 = 2.776$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Legal reserve} = 277{,}60, \\qquad \\text{Dividends} = 832{,}80, \\qquad \\text{Voluntary reserves} = 1.665{,}60$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. New cash</h4>\n<p>The 7.200 m.u. from the shares replace the 7.200 m.u. of the loan; only the tax (+36) and the dividend (+43,20) change:</p>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Cash} = 4.792{,}40 - 36 - 43{,}20 = 4.713{,}20 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 3. Changes in the balance sheet</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Loan</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Capital increase</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Change</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">4.792,40</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">4.713,20</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−79,20</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>27.222,40</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>27.143,20</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>−79,20</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Paid-in capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">10.000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">14.800</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+4.800</td></tr><tr><td>Issue premium</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2.400</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+2.400</td></tr><tr><td>Legal and voluntary reserves</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3.842,40</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3.943,20</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+100,80</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Owners equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>13.842,40</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>21.143,20</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>+7.300,80</strong></td></tr><tr><td>New bank loan (current and non-current)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">7.200</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−7.200</td></tr><tr><td>Interest payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">180</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−180</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total liabilities and equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>27.222,40</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>27.143,20</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>−79,20</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 4. Comparison and leverage effect</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Ratio</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Loan</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Capital increase</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ROA</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">12,97%</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$3.530 / 27.143{,}20 = 13{,}01\\%$</td></tr><tr><td>ROE</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">19,01%</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$2.776 / 21.143{,}20 = 13{,}13\\%$</td></tr><tr><td>$L/E$ [Slide 36]</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0,97</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$6.000 / 21.143{,}20 = 0{,}28$</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The assets earn the same income from operations in both cases (about 13% of the assets), while the new loan costs only 5%. Each m.u. borrowed earns more than it costs, and the difference goes to the shareholders: with the loan, equity is 7.300,80 m.u. smaller while the net profit is only 144 m.u. lower, so the ROE rises from 13,13% to 19,01%. (Separately, the profit is shared among 10.000 shares instead of 14.800, which raises the earnings per share, an extension.) This is the positive <strong>financial leverage effect</strong> (the relation ROE vs ROA − cost of debt is an extension). Its price is risk: the loan raises the debt-to-equity ratio from 0,28 to 0,97 and commits the company to 1.800 m.u. of installments plus interest every year, while the new shares never have to be repaid [Slides 25, 36].</p>"}], "interpretation_html": "<ul><li><strong>Dimensional check:</strong> units × m.u./unit and kg × m.u./kg give m.u.; ratios dimensionless.</li><li><strong>Profit vs cash:</strong> the net profit of 2.632 m.u. does not explain the cash: 7.200 m.u. of new debt came in and 7.200 m.u. went into fixed assets, and 4.800 m.u. of sales are still uncollected.</li><li><strong>The day after the closing:</strong> the interest of the new loan is an expense of 2021 although it is paid on 01/01/2022; forgetting it overstates the profit by 180 m.u.</li><li><strong>Typical mistakes:</strong> putting the whole new loan in non-current liabilities, depreciating the new assets twelve months, ignoring the residual values and treating the issue premium as capital.</li></ul>"}, {"id": "P16", "category": "extra_practice_problem", "archetype": "unordered-items", "theme": "unordered-items", "unit": 3, "title": "HESECA: the statements from a summary of ledger accounts, with prepaid insurance and a credit line", "difficulty": 3, "points": 4.0, "time_minutes": 55, "tags": ["extra-practice", "exam-style", "ledger-accounts", "prepaid-expenses", "credit-line", "bad-debts"], "official": false, "source": "Exam-style practice exercise written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), in the style of T3_Additional_EXERCISES.pdf; prepaids and current assets from Topic 3_Financial Management (I).pdf, Slide 14", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank P16 - HESECA Statements from a Summary of Ledger Accounts.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p><strong>HELIPUERTOS Y SERVICIOS DEL CANTÁBRICO S.L. (HESECA)</strong> operates one helicopter from Santander for charter flights, offshore inspections and maintenance of other operators' aircraft. A trainee of the accounting firm that keeps its books has prepared, instead of the financial statements, the following summary of the ledger accounts of 2022, from 1 January 2022 (01/01/2022) to 31 December 2022 (31/12/2022). The partner of the firm asks you to prepare the balance sheet and the Income Statement as of 31 December 2022 (31/12/2022).</p>\n<p><strong>Opening balances at 1 January 2022 (01/01/2022):</strong> Customers 3.000; Spare parts 1.000; Bank 2.000; Helicopter (cost) 24.000; Accumulated depreciation of the helicopter 4.500; Van (cost) 1.800; Accumulated depreciation of the van 300; Paid-in capital 18.000; Reserves 1.500; Suppliers 1.200; Unpaid taxes 800; Bank loan 5.500.</p>\n<p><strong>Customers account:</strong> opening balance 3.000; invoiced in 2022 for flights and maintenance 34.000; collected in 2022 31.000; on 1 September 2022 (01/09/2022) a balance of 400 m.u. from an operator declared in bankruptcy was written off as definitively unrecoverable.</p>\n<p><strong>Spare parts and suppliers accounts:</strong> on 1 March 2022 (01/03/2022) parts were purchased for 3.000 m.u., 50% paid in cash and the rest payable to 60 days; on 1 September 2022 (01/09/2022) parts were purchased for 3.000 m.u. more, 50% payable to 30 days and the other 50% deferred until 1 March 2023 (01/03/2023). The suppliers' balance of the previous year was paid in January. The physical count of 31 December 2022 (31/12/2022) values the stock (stock in stock) of parts at 1.400 m.u.</p>\n<p><strong>Expense accounts (all paid by bank unless indicated):</strong> fuel 4.800; salaries of pilots and mechanics 9.600; Social Security 3.000, of which the contribution of December (250) is paid in January 2023; hangar rent 200 m.u. / month; on 1 October 2022 (01/10/2022) the annual insurance premium of the helicopter, 1.200 m.u., covering from 1 October 2022 (01/10/2022) to 30 September 2023 (30/09/2023), was paid in full. The unpaid taxes of 2021 were paid in January.</p>\n<p><strong>Fixed assets:</strong> the helicopter is amortized linearly in 12 years with a residual value of 10% of its cost, and was bought on a date that the trainee could not find; the van was bought on 1 January 2021 (01/01/2021) and is amortized linearly in 6 years with residual value zero.</p>\n<p><strong>Financing:</strong> the bank loan was granted on 1 January 2020 (01/01/2020) for 7.500 m.u. at 6% annual interest; every 31 December the company returns 1.000 m.u. and pays the interest calculated on the debt at the beginning of the year. On 1 May 2022 (01/05/2022) a bank opened a credit line with a limit of 4.000 m.u.: HESECA drew 2.000 m.u. that day and 1.000 m.u. more on 1 September 2022 (01/09/2022); it pays 9% annual interest only on the amount drawn, every 31 July, 31 October and 31 January, for the three months then ended.</p>\n<p><strong>Closing:</strong> the results are declared at 23:59 hrs on 31 December 2022 (31/12/2022). The corporation tax is 30% and will be paid in July 2023 (\"taxes to pay\"). The partners decided on 31 December 2022 (31/12/2022) a dividend of 30% of the profit after taxes, to be paid on 15 March 2023 (15/03/2023); the rest goes to reserves.</p>\n<p><strong>Required:</strong></p>\n<ol><li>Deduce the exact date on which the helicopter was purchased, and construct the Income Statement (Profit and Loss Account) of HESECA for the year 2022.</li><li>Calculate the bank balance at 31 December 2022 (31/12/2022) and construct the balance sheet at that date, justifying the classification of every item.</li><li>Determine the values of the ROA and ROE ratios in 2022, and indicate the effect on the balance sheet if the dividend had been paid at 24:00 on 31 December 2022 (31/12/2022).</li><li>The trainee had recorded the whole insurance premium of 1.200 m.u. as an expense of 2022. Indicate what changes this error would produce in the Income Statement, the distribution of the profit and the balance sheet, and explain which statement it distorts and why.</li></ol>", "setup_html": "<p><strong>Reading the ledger:</strong> a ledger account shows the opening balance, the increases and the decreases of the year; its closing balance is opening + increases − decreases. The balance sheet takes the closing balances of the asset, liability and equity accounts; the Income Statement takes the movements of the revenue and expense accounts.</p>\n<p><strong>Hypotheses:</strong></p>\n<ol><li>The rent is paid every month by bank (12 × 200).</li><li>The insurance premium covers twelve months; only October–December 2022 are an expense of 2022, the rest is a <strong>prepaid expense</strong>, a current asset [Slide 14].</li><li>Credit-line interest: May–July is paid on 31/07, August–October on 31/10, November–December accrue and are paid on 31/01/2023.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Extension (not defined in the course decks).</strong> $\\text{ROA} = \\text{Income from operations}/\\text{TA}$; $\\text{ROE} = \\text{Net profit}/E$, on the balance at 31/12/2022.</p>", "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Deduce the purchase date of the helicopter and prepare the 2022 Income Statement</p>", "hint_html": "<p>The helicopter's depreciable base is 90% of 24.000. For the year: which part of the insurance premium belongs to 2022? On the credit line, interest runs only on the amount drawn and only since each drawing.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Helicopter: 1.800 m.u./year; bought on 1 July 2019 (01/07/2019).</li><li>Parts consumed 5.600; insurance 300; depreciation 2.100 m.u.</li><li>Income from operations 5.800; interest 330 + 150 = 480 m.u.</li><li>Profit before taxes 5.320; tax 1.596; net profit 3.724 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Purchase date of the helicopter</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$D_{\\text{helicopter}} = \\frac{24.000 - 0{,}10 \\times 24.000}{12} = \\frac{21.600}{12} = 1.800 \\text{ m.u./year}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\frac{4.500}{1.800} = 2{,}5 \\text{ years before } 01/01/2022 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\text{bought on 1 July 2019 (01/07/2019)}$$</div>\n<p>Check of the van: one year (2021) at $1.800 / 6 = 300$ gives the 300 of the opening balance ✓.</p>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Parts consumed</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Consumption} = 1.000 + 3.000 + 3.000 - 1.400 = 5.600 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 3. Insurance (accrual)</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Expense 2022} = 1.200 \\times \\frac{3}{12} = 300, \\qquad \\text{Prepaid at } 31/12/2022 = 1.200 - 300 = 900 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 4. Interest</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$I_{\\text{loan}} = 0{,}06 \\times 5.500 = 330 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$I_{\\text{credit}} = 2.000 \\times 0{,}09 \\times \\frac{8}{12} + 1.000 \\times 0{,}09 \\times \\frac{4}{12} = 120 + 30 = 150 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<p>Check of the loan: $7.500 - 2 \\times 1.000 = 5.500$ at 01/01/2022 ✓.</p>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 5. Income Statement 2022</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Computation</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Revenue from flights and maintenance</td><td>Customers account</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">34.000</td></tr><tr><td>− Spare parts consumed</td><td>Step 2</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">5.600</td></tr><tr><td>− Fuel</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">4.800</td></tr><tr><td>− Salaries</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">9.600</td></tr><tr><td>− Social Security</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3.000</td></tr><tr><td>− Hangar rent</td><td>$12 \\times 200$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2.400</td></tr><tr><td>− Insurance (October–December)</td><td>Step 3</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">300</td></tr><tr><td>− Bad debt loss</td><td>Customers account</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">400</td></tr><tr><td>− Depreciation</td><td>$1.800 + 300$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2.100</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income from operations</strong></td><td>$34.000 - 28.200$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>5.800</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Financial expenses</td><td>$330 + 150$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">480</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Profit before taxes</strong></td><td>$5.800 - 480$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>5.320</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Corporation tax (30%)</td><td>$0{,}30 \\times 5.320$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.596</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Net profit</strong></td><td>$5.320 - 1.596$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>3.724</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the bank balance and prepare the balance sheet at 31 December 2022</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Close each account: customers = 3.000 + 34.000 − 31.000 − 400. For the suppliers, follow each purchase to its due date. The decided dividend is a debt to the partners.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Customers 5.600; suppliers 1.500; payments to suppliers 5.700 m.u.</li><li>Credit-line interest paid 105, payable 45 m.u.</li><li>Bank balance 7.315 m.u.</li><li>Owners equity 22.106,80; current liabilities 8.508,20; total 34.115 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Closing the customers and suppliers accounts</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Customers}_{31/12} = 3.000 + 34.000 - 31.000 - 400 = 5.600 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Supplier payment</th><th>Date</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Balance of 2021</td><td>January</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.200</td></tr><tr><td>50% of the March purchase</td><td>01/03/2022</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.500</td></tr><tr><td>50% of the March purchase, at 60 days</td><td>30/04/2022</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.500</td></tr><tr><td>50% of the September purchase, at 30 days</td><td>01/10/2022</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.500</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total paid</strong></td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>5.700</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Suppliers}_{31/12} = 1.200 + 6.000 - 5.700 = 1.500 \\text{ m.u. (due 01/03/2023)}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Credit-line interest paid</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$31/07: \\; 2.000 \\times 0{,}09 \\times \\frac{3}{12} = 45; \\qquad 31/10: \\; 2.000 \\times 0{,}09 \\times \\frac{1}{12} + 3.000 \\times 0{,}09 \\times \\frac{2}{12} = 15 + 45 = 60$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Paid} = 45 + 60 = 105, \\qquad \\text{Payable (November–December)} = 3.000 \\times 0{,}09 \\times \\frac{2}{12} = 45 \\text{ m.u.} \\quad (105 + 45 = 150 \\;\\checkmark)$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 3. Bank account 2022</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Inflows</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th><th>Outflows</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Collections from customers</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">31.000</td><td>Suppliers</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">5.700</td></tr><tr><td>Credit line drawn ($2.000 + 1.000$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3.000</td><td>Fuel</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">4.800</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Salaries</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">9.600</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Social Security ($3.000 - 250$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2.750</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Hangar rent</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2.400</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Insurance premium</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.200</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Credit-line interest</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">105</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Loan interest</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">330</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Loan principal</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Unpaid taxes 2021</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">800</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>34.000</strong></td><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>28.685</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Bank}_{31/12/2022} = 2.000 + 34.000 - 28.685 = 7.315 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 4. Distribution</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Dividend} = 0{,}30 \\times 3.724 = 1.117{,}20 \\text{ (payable 15/03/2023)}, \\qquad \\text{Reserves} = 1.500 + 3.724 - 1.117{,}20 = 4.106{,}80$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 5. Balance sheet at 31/12/2022</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Assets</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th><th>Liabilities and owners equity</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Helicopter ($24.000 - 6.300$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">17.700</td><td>Paid-in capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">18.000</td></tr><tr><td>Van ($1.800 - 600$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.200</td><td>Reserves</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">4.106,80</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Non-current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>18.900</strong></td><td><strong>Owners equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>22.106,80</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Spare parts</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.400</td><td>Bank loan due 2024–2027</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3.500</td></tr><tr><td>Customers</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">5.600</td><td><strong>Non-current liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>3.500</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Prepaid insurance</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">900</td><td>Bank loan, installment 31/12/2023</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.000</td></tr><tr><td>Bank</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">7.315</td><td>Credit line drawn</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3.000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Interest payable (credit line)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">45</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Suppliers</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.500</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Social Security payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">250</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Taxes to pay</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.596</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Dividends payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.117,20</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>15.215</strong></td><td><strong>Current liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>8.508,20</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>34.115</strong></td><td><strong>Total liabilities and equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>34.115</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The loan: $5.500 - 1.000 = 4.500$ m.u., of which 1.000 is due on 31/12/2023. The credit line is a short-term bank credit [Slide 31]; the prepaid insurance is a current asset because it will be consumed within the year [Slide 14].</p>"}, {"label": "c", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute ROA and ROE, and the effect of paying the dividend at 24:00 on 31 December 2022</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Paying at 24:00 removes the debt to the partners and the same amount of cash; equity does not change.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>ROA = 17,00%; ROE = 16,85% (extension).</li><li>Paid at 24:00: bank 6.197,80; dividends payable 0; totals 32.997,80 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{ROA} = \\frac{5.800}{34.115} = 17{,}00\\%, \\qquad \\text{ROE} = \\frac{3.724}{22.106{,}80} = 16{,}85\\%$$</div>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Paid 15/03/2023</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Paid at 24:00</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Change</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Bank</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">7.315</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">6.197,80</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−1.117,20</td></tr><tr><td>Dividends payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.117,20</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−1.117,20</td></tr><tr><td>Owners equity</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">22.106,80</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">22.106,80</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0</td></tr><tr><td>Total assets = total liabilities and equity</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">34.115</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">32.997,80</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−1.117,20</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "d", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>The trainee's error: the whole insurance premium as an expense of 2022</p>", "hint_html": "<p>The error moves 900 m.u. of an asset into the expenses of 2022. Follow it through the tax, the dividend and the reserves. Does the bank balance change?</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Insurance expense 1.200 (+900); profit before taxes 4.420; tax 1.326 (−270); net profit 3.094 (−630).</li><li>Dividend 928,20 (−189); reserves 3.665,80 (−441).</li><li>Prepaid insurance disappears (−900); bank unchanged.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Effect on the results</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Profit before taxes} = 5.320 - 900 = 4.420, \\qquad \\text{Tax} = 0{,}30 \\times 4.420 = 1.326, \\qquad \\text{Net profit} = 4.420 - 1.326 = 3.094$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Dividend} = 0{,}30 \\times 3.094 = 928{,}20, \\qquad \\text{Reserves} = 1.500 + 3.094 - 928{,}20 = 3.665{,}80$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Effect on the balance sheet</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Correct</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">With the error</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Change</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Prepaid insurance</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">900</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−900</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>34.115</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>33.215</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>−900</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Reserves</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">4.106,80</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3.665,80</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−441</td></tr><tr><td>Taxes to pay</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.596</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.326</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−270</td></tr><tr><td>Dividends payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.117,20</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">928,20</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−189</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total liabilities and equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>34.115</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>33.215</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>−900</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Check: $441 + 270 + 189 = 900$ ✓.</p>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 3. Which statement the error distorts</h4>\n<p>The bank balance is the same: the 1.200 m.u. were paid either way. The error is an <strong>accrual</strong> error: it charges to 2022 nine months of insurance that belong to 2023 [Slide 8]. The <strong>Income Statement</strong> of 2022 understates the profit by 630 m.u. (and that of 2023 will overstate it), and the <strong>balance sheet</strong> omits a resource the company still owns, the cover of January–September 2023. It also understates the tax and the dividend, so the partners receive 189 m.u. less than they should.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": "<ul><li><strong>Dimensional check:</strong> m.u. × rate × months/12 gives m.u.; ratios dimensionless.</li><li><strong>Ledger logic:</strong> every closing balance follows from opening + increases − decreases; the customers account alone gives revenue (increase), collections and the bad debt (decreases) and the receivable (closing balance).</li><li><strong>Profit vs cash:</strong> net profit 3.724 m.u., while the bank rose by 5.315 m.u.: $3.724 + 2.100$ (depreciation) $+ 3.000$ (credit line) $- 1.000$ (principal) $- 900$ (prepaid insurance) $- 2.600$ (rise in customers) $- 400$ (rise in parts) $+ 300 + 250 + 796 + 45$ (more owed to suppliers, Social Security, tax and interest) $= 5.315$.</li><li><strong>Typical mistakes:</strong> expensing the whole insurance, charging the credit-line interest on the 4.000 m.u. limit instead of the amount drawn, treating the dividend decided as already paid, and forgetting the bad debt in the customers account.</li></ul>"}]};
